        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>KY DEAR Nicriaz,</p>
        <p>A flat hat, yet not the pancake hat, wrapped in fur, calls for admiration—the hat with a large flat. topped crown and a brim that turns up ClOBB at either side on the top level, and comes out to a point over the face. At the back trimming fills the space for a large bow, or knot, and ends that just drop on to the hair dispense with the need of brim. Chinchilla or sable covering the whole of the brim keeps most of the crown, often quilted silk or lace, from view, and in the case of the sable two tiny heads meeting in the centre of the front look rather interesting. Of course the sable -tails mingle with the large millinery bow that is of • iik or velvet, unless it be that a lace scarf, carried ...xund the crown to just peep over the edge of the *,, ties with a bunchy bow at the back, The</p>
        <p>or, rather, similar, shaped toques are built :Araly of the fur, but then these carry a flower, ...wise chrysanthemum, pressed on to the left side, , the front.</p>
        <p>'The dressmakers say that the mourning period 'ever, there will be a distinct liking for rich scutumn tints, such as the red of the rowan berry, the purple of heather, the gold of gorse. Ent *mong Society people, of course, there will be no such crude change; half-mourning for our late Queen begins again after the six weeks' Court mourning tor the Empress Frederick expires, and it includes nothing mo:e vivid than grey; and, in. (leed, as a matter of fact, there is more black and black and white expected than anything else. General mourning gives a different complexion to dress, plunging straight from the respectfnl livery of black to the hues of ordinary existence, and it is to the multitude, therefore, that the subdued yet ,glowing •yes of September will appeal.</p>
        <p>Beaver hats are to be worn this autumn, ani are already to be seen in black, grey and the -natural beaver colour. It is very glossy and silky, and looks very quaint and pretty with an autumn costume. White felt has not worn out its wel- come, and is certain to reappear when the summer holidays are over. The brims of the newest hats are turned up from the face in the fashion of the cloth toque. This nits the majority of English faces. Tucked crinoline is a favourite material for hats. In black it will be worn on into late autumn with suitable trimmings of velvet or taffetas and feathers. Ostrich feathers are more worn than ever, if such a thing is possible. Some- times as many as 15 appear upon one hat, three or four of them being full length, and the others rather shorter. On very tall girls this panoply of plumes does not look too much, but on short ones it looks very wrong indeed.</p>
        <p>I recently saw an alpaca gown in a soft tone a/ ivory white. The triple skirt has eauh section (or flounce) bordered with a line of black velvet, and trimmed with incrustations of lace. The skirt opens up the front, the fastenings being hidden under the lace, which is so arranged as te fill up the corners with very handsome effect. The collar is white silk bordei ed with lace, similar to that on the skirt,. The vest and undersleeves are in silk muslin and lace. The bodice and sleeves are very finely tucked. Alpaca is now accepted as being among the materials suitable for smart afternoon wear. As it is an English material, this is good news for all. In choosing alpaca, the very fine and silky sort, or the other extreme, with its large mottling, should be chosen. The intermediate varieties no not wear so well, and soon get limp and shabby-looking. It pays well in the end to give a good price .or alpaca, but this is equally true of every material. Belts of silk, wide at the back and coming as a mere line at the front, belong to all types of even- ing frocks, the effect being especially where the belt is pointed and the decolletage is V shaped, to diminish the covering of the wearer's back. Little need is there for this particular effect, as in all conscience the corsage is cut low enough. The beauty of much of the applique trimming reserved largely for the foot of evening skirt lies in the composition of its flowers. Silk braid gathered cockleshell fashion forms the soft petals, and tiny ribbons their dainty centres. Lace itself over chine silk partakes of a curious mystic gleam of gcolour that corresponds with that of the gold-braid lace in which van-coloured threads are allowed to atingle, both serving as the finish at the lower edge of a lace dress.</p>
        <p>Your loving JaNNIal.</p>
