      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>A pathetic incident in the murderous attack upon the President of the United States, was his first thought and I solicitude that his wife should not be told he was shot. ,1 Mrs McKinley was rscently so dangerously ill, that she I Was not expected to recover, an for years she has been in delicate health. She is the daughter of Mr James A Saxton, a banker at Canton, Ohio, and after leaving school Miss Sexton travelled a good deal, visiting this, and other countries of Europe. Upon her return she , went in for a business training, becoming a cashier in her father's bank. Just about that time, Major McKinley, who had been servine with distinction in the Civil War, came to Canton to study law. He belonged to a protcundly religious family, and was ap- pointed superintendent of the Methodist Sunday schcol, in which Miss Saxton had a class, and later she became his wife. The Bible to he presented by the Archbishop of Can- terbury to the King at the Coronation, will be supplied by the British and Foreign Bible Society. From the ' time when George 111 said be hoped there would not be a cottage without a Bible, or a child that could not read it, the Royal Family have always taken the greatest interest in the woik of the Institutmn, and the King has consented to accept the Book as the gift of the Society. It will therefore, never be likely to meet with the fate of the Bible that was used at the marriage seivice of Queen Victoria and Prince Albert, which was recently I offered by auction, and sold to a gentleman at North- I ampton for forty guineas. The Bible was 'presented by her Majesty to the Rev John Sleath, D.D., of the Chapel Royal, through whom it passed into possession of the Rev John Couchman, the late Rector of Thornby,. Northamptonshire, and whose effects were recently sold. , Since her return from the Continent, Princess Henry ,of Battenbeig has been staying s t Osborne Cottage, which Her Royal Highness will make her headquarters until after Cbristmas. The house is more commodious than its name would suggest, and it has been the scene of many juvenile picnics, as the children of the Duke and Duchess of Cornwall and York have been staying a good deal at Osborne during the summer. The Princess takes much the same interest in the Army as our late Queen did, and the other day distributed the South African war medals to Isle of Wight troopers of the Im- perial Yeomanry, who have been at the front for fifteen months; and to the Active Service Section of Princess Beatrice's Own Isle of Wight Rifle Volunteers. Her Royal Highness has also been showing her sympathy for the sick and wounded in a very practical way, by taking parties of convalescents for a short sea trip to the Needles and back, on her yacht. The shortening days and the changing tints of the autumn foliage, remind us that summer is rapidly on the wane. In the great towns and cities the coming and goings of the seasons are not so noticeable as in the country, but when the town-bred children go a-black- berrying else knows that autumn is tapping at the door, and that we may seen expect the fall of the leaf.—At many churches the harvest festival has already been held. Slime people think that we ought to have a National Harvest Festival on some given date, instead of on different Sundays, as may be fixed locally. It is, however, morr apprrpriate to confine the festival to a local celebration, as the date of the barveet not only varies a good deal in different parts of the United Kingdom, but we are really more dependent upon the , harvests abroad, in Argentina, Russia, or North Americs, which may be at quite different times of the year and of scarce or bountiful character. The blackberryls one of the mcst popular of the wild fruits, and is still free to all who care to seek it in the woods or along the hedgerows. Its only protection are the prickly branches which do so much damage to fin- gers and clothing. Blackberry brandy, which is made by steeping the fruit,with the addition of sugar and honey, is not so extensively made as it used to be, but it may still be met with, as one of the home made niceties in country cottages. Mushrooms, which used to be con- sidered as much a wild product as the blackberry, have now been declared in many parts to be private property and it is not always safe to take them, when one has the opportunity. It is believed that the higher Courts would not uphold this view, but landowners, and far- mers can always protect themselves by scattering a little mushronn spawn, and some of course make a small sum by belling their field mushrooms, which are of better flavour than the cietivated variety. Autumn hats are varied, no one particular shape dominating. In mcme the crowns are high enough to admit of a wide scarf being twisted round them, gener- ally of speckled silk or ribbon,and there usually appears a hackle at the side, or back. These ornaments are now relegated to positions where, without a hand-glass, the wearer cannot gee them. Possibly we are wearying at last of the prominence long accorded them. But to return to the hats themselves. Where the crowns are not some inches raised, they are round and flat on the head, like mats ; the wide and usually puckered brim escaping from beneath all round. Artificial roses are faied and gene, but for the season we have fruit, large and small, ripe and unripe. There are nodding grapes and cherries, apricots and peaches, which, happily, are not so weighty as they appear to be. Some of the smartest t ques are showing brims of tucked white chiffon, with sprigs of black lace orawn over it. This looks very delicate and pretty, and the chiffon lasts fie sh longer than when wholly uncovered For caps and:light millinery, generally, some DEW arti- ficial leaves are available. These are of cream lace, but unlike the usual fanciful fillings show veins and net- wosk, sufficiently like those in Nature, to suggest "skeleton .' leaves from which the idea of constructing them was probably taken. They look very smart on a white chiffon grownd, but more chic still over black. Scarves and fichus for evening wear continue in favpur, and increase in attractiveness. Of gauze or gossamer they are usually built, and painted with the faintest tenderest colours, in floral designs. They do not afford very much warmth of course, yet they are some slight protection and there is an old tinte look about them that pleases. We think of the days before evening bodices were cut an low as to make us hail these scarves, not so much as additional grazes, but as desirable screens. For while they follow the outline of the cut-down bodice at the back, their path must be a modified semi-circle, not a sharp V runniug indiscreetly tar down the back-bone. Mrs Fenwick Miller thirks that women are entitled to more credit than they usually receive for their work in the home. In an article in one of the Magazines,she grows somewhat indignanr over the universal way in which the " breadwinner " monopolises the credit white little or none is bestewed upon the wife, who looks after the business of the hoisseheld. The fact is, as Mrs Miller says, the few earns her own living, even though the world chooses to think that the husband " keeps " her. Some do not, no doubt, hut the great ' majority of women overwork themselves in restless</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>-- OUR FIRST AUTUMN SZILIE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>JONES &amp; THOMSON,</head>
        <p>JONES &amp; THOMSON,</p>
