        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"HAROLD," said the dear girl, "am I the only girl"— Harold groaned involuntarily. "Am I the only girl you ever told she was the only girl you ever told she was the only girl you loved?' CROSSED. —The woman wrang her hands hysteri- cally. "I have crossed the rubicund !" she cried. The man, her husband, stood aghast and trembled. "The red-faced cook, you know !" faltered th) -woman, in a faint voice. BUT ADDED FUEL.—" Was that your dog that was howling all night?" "I think it was.' "Why in thunder don't you .feed him ? " "" Heavens, man, it's indigestion that makes him ! "</p>
        <p>w Ornxism.—Dr. Gayfee : "Well, how are you to-day ? " Byler : "M? rheumatism has gone to the other arm, doctor." " Splendid ! I told you I'd soon 'rout it out." _</p>
        <p>- MRB. TRAcx : "Do you realise, my dear, that you have never done anything to save your fellow men any suffering?" Mr. Tracey: "Didn't I marry you?" How Saz MARRIED.—Bess: "So Jeannette married a farmer. I thought she said she would marry only a man Of culture?" Nell: "And so ahe did—a man of agriculture." HEWETT,: "I sat at the table next to yours at the restaurant yesterday, and I don't see how you could laugh at the stable stories that Grewitt was telling." Jewitt : "He was paying for the dinner." _ _</p>
        <p>• CALLER: "Why do you play the piano con- ;stantly when your husband is busy' at his literary work?, Doesn't it annoy him?" . Hostess: "On -the contrary, he insists upon my doing it. You %see, he is engaged in writing a tragedy, and he wants something to make him savage.! "I SAY, pa,. ',did you ever, have another wife besides ma?" "No, Willie. But why do you .ask ?" "The family record in the Bile says you married Anon Domini 1871." 171 B: "I believe that a man should let his acts .peak for themselves:" She: "Am I to under- stand, then, that when you took my hand in _yours last night you intended it as a proposal f -marriage ?" MRS. MATCHMAKER : Wise, I thAte from :your interest in my daughter Pearl that you're a gem connoisseur." Mr. Wise : ‘,` It's due, madam, :to my great admiration for mother of Pearl." HER POINT or VIEW.—" Darling," exclaimed ',the happy husband, after the -minister ).D.a pro- nounced them one, lam not worthy of your .love !" "Of course you're not," she replied; " but at my age a girl can't afford to let even an 'opportunity like his go by." DEC7DI'firTIL MAN. Miss Wunder: "What makes Mr. Smooth so happy and jolly looking -these days ?" Miss Gabby: Ch, he doesn't have •to buy any presents for ins fiancée—tells her he is isaving up to start housekeeping." A LADY who advertised for a girl "to do light housework." received a letter from an applicant, who said her health needed sea air, and asked if the lady would kindly inform her where the light- house was situated. A DRAMATIST, when rehearsing recently, thus aCiiressed his leading comedian: "My dear boy, be good enough not to gag, please. Speak my lines and wait! for the laugh." The actor an- swered: "Yes; but my last 'train goes at twelve !" _ _</p>
        <p>- . NELL 4: "Why, he wrote a lovely poem to her." 'Belle : "Yes, bvt she never reaa it. When she saw the title of it she tore the whole thing up in a fit of anger. You See, he called it 'Lines on .Mabel's " • \ _ _</p>
        <p>- MAGISTRATE : "What's, the charge, officer? Drunk?" Constable: "No, sor ; mad." M.,: "How do you know?" "C. : "Well, sir, he is the proprietor of a daily paper, and I heard him ,tell a man that the paper probably had the smallest circulation, and—" M. "'the padded -cell. Quick ! RURAL AUNTY : "What in the world is that -thing ?" City Niece "That's my upright piano:" "Piano?" "Yes; it's draped in the new fashion --completely hidden, you knots'."' "Oh ! Well, that's sensible. Can't be seen or heard, can it ?" "I SHOULD think you would be ashamed to live on' charity. Why don't you do something? Haven't you ever learnt that a man is known by his works ?.." "Well, so's a watch. I ain't no "TELL me honestly," said the novel reader to -the novel writer, "did you ever see a woman who -stood and tapped the floor impatiently with her too for several moments, as you describe?" "Yes," 'was the thoughtful reply, "I did once." "Who .was she?" "She was a clog-dancer." WicKwIRE: "Look here! This is the fourth time this morning you have been in here asking for the price Of a meal." Dismal Dawson: "Yes I am the absent-minded beggar, don't ye know." RETURNED TOURIST : "is Mr. Goodheart still Paying attentions to your daughter ? " "He isn't paying her any attention at all." " Indeed ? Did he jilt her ? " "No, heinarried her." SIMPSON "I'm wonderfully pleased with our new dog, Maria. Did you hear him barking in the night? Burglars must have been sneaking about." . Mrs. S. : "No, it wa,snn't burglars at all. That boy of mine put a clothes-pin on his tail." TrAcitErt': "Miss Sinnico, please parse the sen- - tence, 'Adolphus married Caroline.'" Miss S. : "-"" Well, 'Adolphus' is a noun, 'because it is the name of a thing; 'married' is a conjunction, because it joins Adolphus and Caroline; and Caroline' is a verb, for it governs the noun." "WHAT is this?" exclaimed a compositor who -was expecting to be promoted to a proof-reader- -Ship shortly. " ` Sermons in stones, books in tho running brooks.' Impossible ! He means, of -course, 'Sermons in books, and, stones in the running brooks.'" And the new reading of Shakespeare appeared next morning. A MELBOURNE candidate, 'after laying down a 'somewhat advanced programine, exclaimed: "Yes, gentlemen, and I will go one step further," saying which he stepped over the edge of the , rostrum in short-sighted enthusiasm, and fell among the re- porters beneath. MAMMA: "Oh, Bertie, here you are again all covered with mud from head to foot." Dttrtram (in tears) : "It isn't my fault. I was sailing on a plank, when Willie Meggs gave me a push and over I went." 'Mamma " Well, what are you crying about'? Is it _painful ,to be covered With mud?" Bertram : " No, it ain't; but I thought 'that if I came in laughing you would whip me." Music AT HOME.—The unmusical member of musical family Will appreciate a conversation reported below: "Do you play any instrument, Mr. imp,?"J " Yes ; I'm a cornetist." "And , your sister ? " :` She's a pianist." "Does your mother play?" "She's a zitherist." "And your father?" He's a pessimist." " WEL= !"" she cried from the window, "you two 'boys get out of that hammock. You know ylm will break it down ! " "Two little fellows like me and Tommy break it down?" inquired the boy. " Yes, you will." "An' last night, when that Mr. Smithkins called on you, an' you got tired sitting on the couch, and----" "Never mind, Willie," she interrupted hastily. " l'erhaps its all right, -.after all." "MAY I wake the baby, mamma?" asked five- year-old Johnny, "Why, what „do you want to wake her, for ? " asked the mother. I want to see if she can cry loud enough to drown the noise of my new drum," replied the small investigator. "Do you make special terms to bridal parties?" asked the innocent-looking bridegroom. "Yes!' replied the hotel clerk ; "we always charge 'em ,double rates." . • SHE: "When are you going to give me the 'money to buy that new dress ? " lie : "Next week.', That's what you said last week." "Yes, and that's whit I say now, and am going to ,say next week. I'm not the kind of man who says T one thing one week and another thing next week." A NARROW EscApE2—The grey-haired stranger ' bent over the desk. "Arc you the person who wrote the account of the Munn reception?" "I . am," said the flippant young man. "Well, in speaking of my daughter, you use this paragraph, She swept about the room with an inherited grace -that was fascinating.' Now, what did you mean by that?" "Why, it struck me as a good chance for a, neat compliment to her esteenied parents." "Sure you didn't mean to insinuate that her lather laid the foundation of his fortune by selling "brooms ?" "Certainly not." "Because I did, :you know." "I didn't know it." "Then that's fall right. Good-day." '</p>
