      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WELCOMING THE NEW</head>
        <p>WELCOMING THE NEW</p>
        <p>WARRINGION WESLEYAN MINISTER.</p>
        <p>Meeting in Bold-Street Schoolroom.</p>
        <p>The Rev William C J Neville who succeeds the Rev H G Roberts, M.A., in the Warrington Wesleyan circuit received a cordial welceme to his new sphere of work at a meeting of officers and friencs held in Bold-street schoolroom on Monday evening. The proceedings took the form of a social evening. The room had been tastefully decorated for the occasion and presented the appearance of a drawing room, the introduction of a number of lighted fairy lamps having a pleasing effect. The first half-hour was spent in introducing the new minister to the many friends who had assembled. including a large number from the outlying districts which are included in the circuit. The introductions over, a meeting was held, presided over by Mr John Taylor. There were also present t—Reve Theodore Bishop, C tl Hocken and J Gunnell, Mr Alderman Shaw Green, J.P., Mr J W Havward,J.P., Mr William Silcock, Mr Meldrtsin (Lvmm), Mr Smith (Penketh), Mr Richard- son (Padgatt), Mr Atherton (Penketh), Mr Gregson (Mesmer-strew), Mr Replaner: (Meere). The CIAIRMAN who met with a cordial reception, said be was very pleased to be presant as that was the first public meeting he had attended for 18 months, owing to ill-health. He had tbe honour some two years ago of presiding at a meeting of a similar character,when they rave a welcome to the Revs T Bishop and C H Hocken. He regretted that after looking over the reports given from time to time, with regard to the numbers, that they had decreased about 18 during the past three years. He hoped that Mr Neville would increase the numbers. (Aryl/Luse.) He had no doubt Mr Neville's three years ministry in Warrington would be a blessing to the chinch and a great comfort to himself. They were very pleased he had brought a wife and family with him, and hoped they would have a happy time amongst the Wesleyens in Warrington. (Applause.) • After a hyden had been sung, Mr J W HAYWARD said they not only welcomed Mr Neville but they sym- pathized with him for leaving a people with whom be bad ministered for the past three years. They wel- comed kim very heartily to Warrington, and hoped that he would have a successful ministry. • . Mr WILLIAM Siwocx hoped Mr. DI, yllle would have three years' pleasant ministry amongst them. There was a good deal of work to,be done in the sown, especi- ally amongst the yeung. Addresses of welcome were also delivered by Alder- man Shaw Green (representing Bewsey-roail), and Messrs Meldrum (Lymen), Smith (Penketh), J E Richardson (Padgete), Atherton (Penketb), Oregson (Ellesmere-street), and Revlance (Moore). The Rev Wiserax C JD/Einem who on rising to reply was heartily applauded, said he had been accorded a hearty Lancashire reception to use the words of the Chairman. Be tianked them from his heart for their kindness. He had listened with great interest and pleasure to the various speech s that had been made, and he felt sure that the kindly words that had been spoken had been the true expressions of kindly, warm, Methodist hearts'. Mr Hayward, in his address. sympa- ' thized with him (the speaker), and he needed sympathy just then. It was all very strange to him coming armrest them. Be had left a circuit where he had spent three very happy, and he trusted useful years. Ha had come down from the West of England to sea Linea- shire Mahodism, and to lend a hand in the work that was being done in this part of the country. He had left behind him many kind friends whom be could not forget. He had eeceived a letter from his society steward —the steward of the church over which he had had charge— and in it be spoke of the reception which wan to be given to him (Mr Neville) that night, and added that they would no thinking of him and praying that he might have a favourable entrance amongst the people of Warrington. He felt; although strange. that be bad come amongst friends. (Hear, bear.) He had had one or two strange surprises already. Be preached at Pad- gate 9, week the previous day,and as be walked towarde the church he met a young man he had known from his youth, who happened to come from the same part of the same island that.gave him (the speaker) birth. He was surprised and pleased to meet such an old friend. He went to Penketh yesterday, and met there some who knew friends of his in the Whitebaven circuit in which he travelled some years ago. He tound that these friends were relatives of Mr William Wallver,arad he had no kinder friend in the Whitehaven circuit than Mr William Walker. And that night unexpectedly he had seen a gentleman he once met in the South ot England In the adjoining circuit to Portsmouth circuiese that he had seen old friends and was in the midst of new ones. Having thanked the Chairman and his wife for their kindness to him on his entrance into Warrington. Mr Neville remarked that it gave him very great pleasure to be associated in the work of the circuit with Mr Bishop. Be had beard of him before he came to War- rington, and was anxious to make his acquaintance. He was also pleased to meet his colleagues Mr Hocken and Mr Gunnell. and he hoped they would strive together for the wolk which was dear to them all and to the audience, namely, the work of the Lord Jesus Christ. (Applause.) He bad come to Warriegton with only one end in view, namele, to please Him who had called him to bri'a soldier. He ahontri not seek the favour of anyone else. He feared to offend against Him, and in all his comines in and goings out amongst them he should try to carry out that resolution. His work would be necessarily imperfect, but it would be done with that end in view—to please the Lord from whom he had received his commission. He knew very little about the people of the circuit yet, and the condition of the work, hut he could see that his time would be fully occupiel. Be could not hope to do all that the friends would like him to do—in order to do that he would have to be divided into several sections—but he would work to the best of his ability and give the full measure of time and energy which the circuit had a right to expect. (Applause) He had been given to understand that whilst one met with grumblers in every circuit there were none to be found here—(laughter—and he had no doubt they would get on without such persons. (Laughter.) He would like to say a word about Mr William Silcock, and to acknowledge his kindness in the conduct of the correspondence on behalf of the circuit. Mr Silcock bad spoken cf his (Mr Neville's) interest- in young people. ,He was young himself, and he was very much intkessted in the work of' the Wesley Guild. Be should be hippy to be emaciated with it and with all that concerned young people. He was willing to be guided, and all the young people were by the ripe and rich experience ef those who had lived longer in the world, but at the same time youth Meant energy, and be had no doubt that if they combined the two qualities they would be able to do good work in the circuit. In conclusion, the speaker said be was much ieterested in the 20th Century Fund, and at the commencrment the Superintendent of his circuit promised 3CO guineas, hut they had paid over 1,000 guineas. (Applause.) That had been done by petient and careful work. He believed the opening of the fund bad done good. Proceeding to refer to his duties amongst them he said they were laid down for him In the usual Westeyan formula "to preach, to per- form all acts of religious worship, and to administer Methodist discipline." He leaped they would all work together and Alive together for the church and the gottpsl. Mr. Neville then said that while in his Ports- mouth circuit be visited the ship Victory, on which Nelson flew his famous signal" England expects every man this day to do his duty." Be would alter that and made it read " Christ expects eve's, man and woman this day to do his or her duty." (Hear, hear) The Rev Tinsonoan Brdnor in alluding to the recent Methodist Conference said he had succeeded in getting Dr Allen, the ex-president, to come to Warrington at an early date, and 'Dr Davidson bad promised to come next year-Be concluded by proposing a hearty vote of thanks to the circuit stewards. This Was carried with accla- mation, _ _</p>
        <p>Light refreshments were then baneed round, and the ladies responsible for the excellent arrangements made for providing the refreshments were Mrs Bishop, Mrs Hocken. Mrs R Silcox*, and Mns Parkinson, and the Mimes Weaver, Collin, and Barlow. A pleasant meeting shortly afterwards concluded. A short musical pro- gramme way arranged by Mr P Hickman and proved very eojoyable.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr Osmond Tearle.</p>
        <p>The news of the death of this talented actor re- calls the fact that Mr Tearle received his early tuition in the histrionic art at the Theatre Roy alo Scotland-road, Warrington, under the supervision of the late Mr Brinsley Sheridan,for whom be acted as stage manager for a considerable number of veers It was during his connection with Mr Sheridan that his talents developed, and it was owing to the kindly advice of Major Pickmere, who was recog- nized as one of our most talented amateur actors that Mr Tearle made a study of Shakeeperian playa. His first fascination for the foot lights arose from his taking the part of Trebonius in "Julius Ctesar," when the students of St Francis Xavier's College, TAiverpobl produced that play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IHE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 13 1901</head>
        <p>IHE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 13 1901</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Oval ist."</p>
        <p>I wonder if there is anything whatever that gives sportsmen generally more pleasure than the avenging of a deteat. Personally, I don't think there is. There is the satisfaction of winning and the savage joy of having paid off old scores all rolled Into one. Warring- ton players and spectators experienced all this on Satur- day, when the team which deprived Warrington of the Northern Union Cup last season—Batley to wit—were soundly thrashed at Wilderspool. Ever since the cup- final fitsco at Leeds the Warringtonians have been inwardly praying for another chance of proving that they vitre not the invertebrate incompetent lot they appeared. The chance came on Saturday, and as it was the opening match of the season it was accepted with added relish,</p>
        <p>Battey have been a famous team for a long time, many of their performances being quite historical. To win the Northern Union Cup three times in rive years is an achievement of no mean order, while the "gallant youths" have always been well up in the League tour- nament. Still there was just as much confidence, if not more, that Warrington were a better team as there was before Batley beat the wire-pullers at Leeds. That con- fidence was not misplaced this time, for the Warrington lads played at the top of their form and completely vanquished the cup-holders by 15 points to 3 points.</p>
        <p>The warmth of the weather may possibly have had some effect on the temper of the players, for the match was not entirely devoid of unpleasant incidents. Batley did not in the least like the way Warrington bustled them about, and several instances of irritation were noticeable. One of them ended seriously. Burgess and Davies (the crack tbree-quarter) had a small set-to with fists and were promptly ordered off the field. Burgess retired as meekly as a lamb. Davies was more dke a tiger, for he jumped the rails and "went for "a specta- tor who had aroused hisfire. The spectator was seriously assaultesl and then the police had the task of rescuing the foolish footballer from the bands of the infuriated crowd. I was all over in a minute or two, but it was a:disgraceful scene while it lasted, and particularly damaging to the reputition of a class player like Watty Dawes. He will probably come in for some wholesome suspension at the hands of the League Committee, and he will deserve it.</p>
        <p>In other respects the game was a fine. one. Batley held the tapper hand at the commencement, but they were noticeably slow behind—so much that the War- rington backs bad no difficulty in checking any aggres- sive movement. By and by combination on the home side improved and Dicksason massed the Batley line with a fine effort. Harris followed suit very soon after, and at the interval Warrington had just reversed the score in the cup-tinal—to tries to nil—the nil this time being Batley's bitter portiou.</p>
        <p>Warrington piled it on rapidly in the second half. Fish had gone half back in place of Burgess. and he very soon got the home backs going in fine style. A beautiful bit of play between himself and Isheewood resulted in his scoring after a ran half the length of the field, while later on Dickenson and Harris also scored dashing tries. I fancy Burgess's absence was rather a good thing for Warrington, as he was not playing • smart game at all. There is no doubt, however, that Davies was a big loss to Batley, and they very soon admitted that they were a beaten side, when he bad gone. Just before the close leozzird took advantage of Warrington's laxity to score a solitary try and save his side the additional indignity of being nilled.</p>
        <p>Warrington were a long way the better side on Satur- day and their play was in direct contrast to the display they gave in the previous encounter with Batley. The backs played with the utmost confidence, resource and dash and literally made epos round the visitors, who suffered much in this department from the absence of Fitzgerald and Oakland. Forward Batley were the stronger, but Warrington had a corresponding advantage In speed and made more progress when they got the ball. The honours all go to the threegotarter line, Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson and Harris playing a truly re- markable game. There was not a pin to choose between them individually, all four playing superbly. Hallam was also excellent at back, the forwards and halves being only moderate. It is in there two places where the team's princir al weakness lies. A half back of the class of Fish and a ccuole more forwards of the Boardman stamp would make the team the equal of any in the North of Eogland. Can these men not be found and secured for either love or money ? There should not be an overwhelming difficulty in the way of getting for- wards, at any rate.</p>
        <p>Un to the time of his suspension Davies played well for Batley, but none of the other players caught the eye much. Goodall and Garner were safe, but the team as a whole seemed slow and short of dash. I fancy they must have played just as much above their form in the final as Warrington played below theirs.</p>
        <p>It was very satisfactory too to note the siza of the crowdiTis true the select nioep-nny side was sparsely populated, hut the crowd on the "popular"—very pno- ular now—side was dense. Receipts totaled up to £122 in gate money, a start which augurs well for the finan- cial success of the season so far as Northern League matches are concerned.</p>
        <p>By the way, I see the Pcoring board has been moved somewhat, with the result that no ore can see it who is not within actual reazh of it. The thing merely wants raisicg six feet or so and it will answer its very usetul purpose as satisfactorily as ever it did.</p>
        <p>Another little matter ! The home players made a striking debut in primrose and blue striped stockings with the bars running crossways. It struck a good many people that the jerseys would look very much better if they were made on the same plan. It has been one of my pet notions for some time to see the Warring' ton team in primrose and blue with the stripes broadened and running across inrtead of down. Such an innovation would smarten them up I feel sure, and they ai e far from lookina as smart as they are yet. Let us look well, even if we do not do well, when we visit Yorkshire. There is no reason why we should not do both.</p>
        <p>The season just started aught to Ile a remlrkable one, I? only to justify the. almost sensational legislative pro— ceedings which just preceded its inauguration In no other terms can one fittingly describe the fact of no fewer than four clubs in the Northern League having each a couple of points deducted from their score before a single match had been played, and a single point earned. It is true that in the instance of Salford only has the formal resolution deducting the points been passed, but that is a mere detail IncidPntal upon the priority of meting, for load the League Committee met last week after instead of before the Union Executive had concluded its prvceedings, the other three would doubtless have shared the same fate as Salford.</p>
        <p>That Hunslet, Warrington, and Swinton will even- tually do so cannot well be doubted, as the League By- law 28 is clear in making such deduction imperative in the case of a club committing a breach of the Profes- sional laws: Now, last ;week 'Hunslet were fined by the Executive £l.O for a breach of section (b) of Law 1. and also of Laws 2 and 5, Warrington convicted of a breach of Law P. and Swint on found guilty of infring- ing the same law. "While there's life there's hope," but I fear the two points so gallantly won on Saturday will have to be forfeits&amp;</p>
        <p>',The opening games of the Northern League have most unpleasant significance for Yorkshiremen," says a Yorkshire paper. "so hopelessly do they appear to have been outclassed by their rivals across the border that divides the camps of the followers of the two Roses. Where the shoe pinches is in the decisive reverses in- flicted upon Batley, Halifax, and Brighouse Rangers by Warriegten, Broughton Rangers, and Salford respec- tively. It may be that the early period of the season finds the players in the Yorkshire clubs scarcely in I condition to do justice to their reputation, though why this should be more true of them than of their Lanca- shire rivois one cannot pretend to say, and it is at best but a shadowy sort of consolation. It is to be feared, however, that even were there a modicum of truth in it the disparsity between the scores would still remain. unaccounted for. Altogether the three Red Rose teams aggregated 53, and the solitary contribution from the; Yorkshiremen was a try. Bearing in mind how easily the White Rose was vanquished last season, it bas to be confessed that the Lancashires Lre too 'clever for us,and all the arts of both attack and defence will have to be I cultivated with greater assiduity if Yorkshire's prestige. even in club games, is to be restored."</p>
        <p>Runcorn the only local Northern League Club besides Warrington,made a bard fight at Hull, but were eventu- ally beaten by goal and two tries to three goals. The disparity in the scores is not great but it indicates that the attack of the Cheshire lads was net as good as that of their foes. [full have a strong team this season and should do as welt as any of the Yorkshire Clubs. Ham- brecht of Eraiallcy is a valuable recruit of theirs. On the Runcorn side Jolley and Butterworth were the best men.</p>
        <p>Worringtan have a very stiff task on band again to- day, and it can hardly be expected that they will emerge successfully from the fray. Last week it was a c.ise of the home side anxie 113 for revenge and this week it is the same except that the home side is Bradford and Warrington the visitors. The Bradford men will nct have forgotten the knockout blow administered to them in the cup-tie at Wilderspool last season, and their defeat last Saturday by Hunslet will not lessen their anxiety to annex two points at Warrington's expense. Warrington are sure to be over weighted forward,. but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>if the backs can get many chances they should be able to bold their own.</head>
        <p>if the backs can get many chances they should be able to bold their own.</p>
        <p>The same team almost, has been chosen to do duty. Boardman is a notable absentee owing to injuries, while Edmondson and Fell dad Eden make a welcome re-appearance. The selected fifteen is as lollows Hallam, full back ; Fish, Lherwood, Dickenson and Harris, three quaners ; Burgess acid Allen, halves ; Monism, Fell, Edmondson, Scboltze, Eden, J Swift, Jolley and Cunningbam, forwards.</p>
        <p>For those who cannot make the long journey to Bradford there will be the attraction of a Lancashire combination fixture at home, when the " A team opeu their reason with R.7chdale Hornets "A " team. The "A " team matches this season are all league games and some better football should be shown than heretofore. Warrington will be represented by the following selection :—r Korgot, full back ' • Ball, Rat- cliffe, Lawless and Taylor, threeq oarters ; Duckworth and Hockenhall, half-backs ; Swift, Pernberton,Higham, Ashton, Antrobus, Purslow, Trees and Edwards, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Fletcher Russell's who got the worst of their encounter witu Runcorn " A " last Saturday, open their season at Lovely-lane tc-day with Salford " A" team. The Reds are bringing a powerful comhinatioa and a well-contested game should result. The chosen teams are as follows :—Fletcher Russell's : Full back, Rowlinson ; threequarters, W Clare, C Wosd, J Nevins, J Oakes ; ualf-backs, J Ashurst (captain), J Foden ; forwards, G Alien, W Barraec. P Hill, Cir Joiley,T Potter, R Thomas, J Lythgoe, J Wnittaker. Salford : Full back, A Norris; threequarters, A Dti/leS, J Fletcher, H Had wen, E A Bone ; ual.-back!, E Harter, E S.nith ; forwards, G Heath, V Jones, R ttainsuurv, M Gledhill, B O'Hara, G Fisher, W Howarth, and 11 Earnshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington nnade a poor show last Saturday when they visited Belton Park. 'the Liverpudlians batted first and put together 128, of whicti 65 were contribu- ted by Findley. On going to the wicket the Warrington batsmen fared badly, and in a little over an hour were dismiE.sed tor 55. Three member of the team—J Gib- bon (13), Tnewlis (12) and J W Bibby (10)—compikd double figures, whilst cne last four batsmen were all di4.-nissed at one total. J W Bibby batted steadily for his not out innings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>AGONISING TORTURE.</head>
        <p>AGONISING TORTURE.</p>
        <p>Mr James Jone', 49, Chester-road, Tuebrook, Liver- pool, gives a useful experience, which should benefit those who suffer trorn gravel and bladder trouble. He suffered from • complication of kidney disease, bladder and urinary troubles, and gravel. His was bad case neglected, and, to use his own words, " he suffered agonising torture." He became so weak that he could scarcely stand, and at night he had to get up many times to ease the terrible pressure or distention of the bladder.</p>
        <p>Be began to take Dodd's Kidney Piny, and after four locxes found that vie y were acting on the material that hau accumulated in the bladder, which commenced to come away in small pieces. Now he is satisfied that his kidneys are completely cured, and that there is no evidence of stone or gravel about him. Gravel and bladde: troubles are visible effects of kidney disease. By taking Dodd's Kidney Pills, the safe and sure remedy, the kidneys will do their duty, and make atone and gravel impossible. Dodd's Kidney Piils are 2i Si per box, of all Chemists, or post free on receipt. of price from The Dodds Medicine Co., 23, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. Remember the name, D-o-d-d-'s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Late Mrs. F. Slater.</p>
        <p>On ,Sunday evening the 13..? v A Macdonald, fot merly curate of St James', Latchford, and at present in tem- porary charge of the parish of St Chad's Over, preach- ing to a crowded congregation made touching reference to the loss sustained by that congregation and parish, by tbe lamentable deakt of Mrs Slater, the beloved wife of the esteemed and respected vicar of the parish. Mr Macdonald, who spoke with much feeling,tauched upon several traits of the beautiful character of the deceased lady, specially emphasising the wonderful power of sympathy which she ex'ibited at all times for the sick and the suffering, the sorrowful and the distressed. For this and many other rare and excellent qualities, con— tinued the rev gentleman, Mrs Slater's memory would long be cherished and revered, not only in the parish of • St Chad's, Over, but also in that of St James'. Latch- ford, where the deceased lady was so well known and greatly beloved. Mr Macdonald concluded by asking for the earnest praye:s of toe congregation on behalf of the grief-stricken fomily, for the children so early de- prived of the care and guidance of a loving mother.and tor the husband so untimely bereft of the help and mnfor: of a raps:, faithful and dovoted wife.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>The chapel anniversary services in connection with the eVorkrogrnen's Mission, Bank Quay, were held on Sunday, and were attended with success. The morning service was held in the chapel, when a sermon was preached by Mr A Coroey. The singing was of a hearty character. In the afternoon a mirdcal service was held in the new Scheolroorn, Thewlis-street, presided over by Councilor William Roberts. The Male Voice Choir. which was conducted by Mr S Hassan, rendered the following items: "Lead, kindly light," " The Mighty Lord,' "Perfect in Christ," and the"Elallelujah Chorus.'' Messrs G Geuteh and S Hassell contributed the duets, "Now we are Ambassadors," and "For so loath the Lord," and Messrs A Griffiths and S Hassell, "Love Divine." The followice iactividual solos were given: "The good Shepherd," Mr A Griffiths; and "The Sanct- uary," by Mr S Millingtcn. A collection was taken on , behalf of the chapel funds. In the eveoing the service • was conducted by Mr Corney. The special Contribu- tions were solo "Just for to-day," Mr S Gough; anthem, "The Lord is my Shepherd," the choet ; and solo "The King ol Love my Shepherd is," Mta3 L Devies,ot Holija fare. The collection for the day amounted to £7 5, 51- • Church Parade of Railway Servants. Mr Thomas Edwards, a valued member of the choir and bible class at St. Paul's Church, died a short time I ago, leaving a widow and several childrtn. A collec- tion was made in the chiatch for these by the bible class and choir, and realised a very acceptable sum. Mr Ei wards had long been a member of the Amaleamaeed Society of Railway Servants, a organization with a great record for kindly deeds of charity, especially to those connected with the society. By an arrangement between the Vicar, the conductor of the biede class, and a the committee of the A.S.R.S., processioa from Bank Quay Station, by a circuitous route to the church,for a funeral sermon, was arranged for last Sunday! St Paul's Band kindly led the procesiioa,at the head of which was the new railwaymen's banner. The sum of 7 15i was collected en route to the cnurch. At St PlUr3 the ser- vice was conducted aad a sermon preached by the Vicar. Commencing with a processional hymn, "Sons of labour," the Service was a beautiful and impressive I ceremony, the chief item b2ini; Gounod's anthem " By Babylon's wave," sung to the accompaniment of the organ and piano by the large choir, who bad attended to show their sympathy with the family of their deceased colleague. The hymn commencing " Abiee with me" was sung to the accompaniment of tne band. The collection realized £2 15s ioi, and thus the handsome sum of ten guineas was subscribed (Wring the after- noon. The organizers expressed their gratitude to the choir and band of St Paul's Church for their hearty co-operation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ANGLI NG.</head>
        <p>ANGLI NG.</p>
        <p>The Bridgewat—er—Challenge Shield.</p>
        <p>The ninth annual competition for this handsome trerhy was hold under very pleasant conditions in the Worsley Canal Fishery on Sacurdsy. It witt be interest- ing to notify the various positions of the five Warring- ton teams. The highest was the Mersey A.S , who occupied the fifth position. ibey were the first of the five Warrington teams with an aggregate weight of 11th 11r.z 6drs, and the club is to be commiserated with on its hard luck, one of the team having the misfortune ' to lose a large fish and another had his line broken by a large fish or they would probably have won the shield. The next position (15) was tne Howley AS., followed by (17) the Workiig Men's A.S the Victoria AS (25),and the Warrington Club (26) Thirty-two teams competed, the previous highest being twenty-nine. Toe entry was corn- posed of six teams from Farnworth, six from Leigh, five from Warringtoo,tbree each from Salford and Tyldesley two each from Pendleton, Wigan, and Clifton, and one each from Northwich, Swinton, and Manchester. The arrangements made by the referee, Mr Barlow, Wigan, and the clerk of scales, Mr Al 4 Haraness, were spoken of in the highest terms. The following is the official declaration, subject to protest, if any, and the usual scrutiny. Winners of shield : Duchy Angling Society, Pendleton, who also secure the gold medal, with a catch of two pounds. The next six in order of merit, and who take the remaining prizes,are the Amateur Angling Society, Wigan, the present holders, who out of four entries secured the first place twice and now the second, and in the other event the twentieth, llb 14 z Mrs ; Tyldesley Angling Society, lib 12oz 14drs ; Plank Lane Angling Society, lib 12oz 1142-irs ; Standard-Mower Angling Society, Leigh, lib 9a5 Sirs ; and Tyldesley Angling Society (2nd team), jlb 8-z 12drs. The treasurer for the Duchy Angli.g Society, who have meritoriously sscured the honour of holding the shield, is Mr J Valentine, the well-known football player, who will have the custody of the shield after presentation.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is highly nourishing and easily digested, repairing waste and preserving health, It is absolutely pure and untampereci with, being entirely free from drugs, alkalies, Or any admixture. CADBURY'S is a perfect food, suitable for all %gee and all seasons of the year. (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>THE POOR RATE.</p>
        <p>A mee lug of this Board was held ia the Board Roam at the Workhouse on Wednesday moruirig. Mr A J Jolley presided and there were also preheat :—Mrs Clay. Rev Father Wilson, Rev J H Riadette, Rev T It Matthews, Dr Murray, Mesfrs William Hickman, J S Davies, T a Ashcroft, A Jolley, G Pilling, James Payton, A Brookf , G Mather, James Robinson. William Houghton, J Clarke,T Halton,R Farshaw, with J C Sutton (clerk), S Sutton assistant clerk), and A Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HOUSE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HOUSE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of t-he "H—cf u-sk-5 Cnmitceestated that the resignatido of Nurse Evans had tren received. The matron was instructed to obtain a successor. An appli- cation having been made by tbe Master for leave of absence on account of ill-health it was resolved that the same be granted and that Mrs Ward be allowed to accompany him. Miss Ward was instructed to perform the duties in their absence.</p>
        <p>Toe minutes were opp-r-ovved. A NEW DIEIARY.</p>
        <p>The Dietary sub-committee recommended that for children in class 5 the breakfast on Sunday be as on other days ; also, that for supper on Mondays, Wednes- days and Fridays the rations be scz of bread, lcz of treacle, and half a pat of tea; o,i Toes lays,Tnursdays, and Saturdays, soz of bread, loz of jam,a.nd half a pint of tea. That for Ciasses 1, la, 2. 2a, 2b, 3, 34, 4 4a, 4, the supper rations for week- lays be aitere..l,and a breaa and skim milk ration be sub%tituted for the existing ration of porridge; the above modification to take effect as from the end of the current quarter. It was also recommended that the Clerknatity I heLocal Government BJara in due cJurse, and taat the necessary aterationA in the dietary tables be made.</p>
        <p>The above recommendatioi had been accep'el by the House Cooothitte., and was approved by thu Workhouse Medtcal Officer.</p>
        <p>In answer to a question by the Rev J H HIDDETTE, The CHAIRMAN said the new dietaries had only to do with the classification for dietary purposes, which was quite a distinct thing from the classification scheme. Mr HICKMAN explaint d hit the children had beea in the habit of having porridge twice a day but it was now proposed to substitute bread and treacle or bread and jam for one meat. "Toe CHAIRMAN said the Board's attention was drawn to the matter by the Inspector anis recent visit. It was only fair to the Board to state that the previous dietary table was submitted to the Local Government Board and met with thPir approval. The new dietary would not mean any additional cost. Since March last the inmates had had porridge both morning and evening and that had caused some dissatisfac tinn. The House Committee was unanimously of the opiaisn that it was desirable to make some change and had approved of the sub-committee's recommendations. Members of the Ladies Committee as %ell as inmates bad also c-irnmented upon the dietary of the hospital suffering rather from sameness as the house dietary had done in this respect. But the hospital dietary was en- tirely under the control of the doctor who could alter it when he thought tit. It rested with the doctor to bring the hospital dietary into line with the improved house aletary. So far as his (the Chairman's) inquiries had extended their dietary at Warrington was a very good sample indeed. I: was cheap and pleasing with the ex- ception of the porridge cf which there had been k superfluity ; in fact it had b3en a little stodgy but they had got over that difficulty now. The recommendations were approved. RE-APPOINTMENT OF DR. SEPRTON.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr WILLIAM HOUGHTON seconded by Mr -- m ATHER, the following resolution was Passed : "That inasmuch as Dr Richard Sephton has for some years past satisfactorily filled the office of Medical Officer for the NJ. 3 District of the Union, and his present residence atd surgery,although situated out of the diFti,ct, is accessible and convenient to the Union patients, and there being no duly gialified man resident within the district, he be re-appointed Medical Officer of that district for a further period of 3 vears,terminat- ing on the 29:b day of September, 1904."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NORTH WESTERN POOR LW CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>NORTH WESTERN POOR LW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the chairman, vice-chairman,and clerk, or assistant-clerk, represent the Board at the North-Wasterti Poor Liw C,iferenc, to he beld in the Town Hall, Chester, on the 20th and 21st September.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>CLERK'S STATEMENT UPON ESTIMATES.</head>
        <p>CLERK'S STATEMENT UPON ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>' The CLERK submitted the following statement upon estimatcs tor the half year ending Illy Day, 1902 : The first of the following figures shows the estimated expenditure for the ncxt half-year ending at March, 1902. The second figures state the actual out/av in the corresponding half-year ending at marc,i, 1901. For inmaintenance, workhouse £2,500—£2252 93. Schools £875—E838 3 ; niut relief £2 200 —£2,184 17 11; lunatics in asylums £l,BOO-1,7265s 91; principal and interest on losns £1 600—£1,554 18s 91; salaries of officers £1.900—£1,823 103 ; officers' t.rione, workhouse, £3OO —k2Bs 4 5; schools £160—£152 193 81 ; extra medical fee £2o—£ll;superannuatiod allowances £B5 —£B2 149; buildings, repairs, architect's commission &amp;3, £l.ooo— 163 41 ; furniture and property £200—£324 Is ; rentri,rate.taxos,insurance,office gas,water,and coal,&amp;c £250—£240 4s 11; stat'ooery, printing and advertise— ments, £2oo—£lB6 135 31 ; paupers in hospitals and schools £7O —£62 1s 61: registration, £160—£154 us ; vaccination £4OO —£34B 115 81; legal proceedings £3O —£27 65 61; union assessment expenses £l5O-135 ; conveyance to hospitals £l5 —£l4 9s 81 ; subscription to hospitals, &amp; £25 —£24 143; audit stamp £l5 —£2o ; medical fees, visiting pauper lunatics £2—£l 7461; school fen for non-pauper children £l5-29 9s 21; removal expenses £2o—£ls 83 51 ; conference and deputation expenses £l5-7 ; convoyance of hitirVicS £65-62 163 81 ; r sit of pay rations £2o—£l7 55 sundry acmunts £2s—£l6 93 21. In the corresnooding half-year ending March, 1901, an outlay of £1.823 in respect of new infirmary ac3orints, and an outlay of £207 03 41 in respect of the Newton land purchase arose, rnalsing a total expenditure in the half-year of £14,983 174 31 The exc,ptional expenditure in the next half-year will require £1,940 in respect of the Newton new buildings, and of £BOO in respect of the Workhouse improvements. the total of estimated outlay being £15,057. less £3,097, requiring a nett total revenue of £12,960.</p>
        <p>Mr SUTTON aiSO WM, a short, statemPnt upon past events is conmection with Union administration and expenditure during the period of his personal con- nection with the board.</p>
        <p>It was correct and important to say that it was due to the past policy of successive Boards, that the average half yearly call had fallen to sixpence—.nd that the present Union ratepayer derived benefit out of its obser vance. It would not be poseible to suggest that under no conditions would increase of the sixpenny rate be foand necessary. There wit no ground, however, for chinking that any such increase will he other than tem porary—it might ypt prove that a sixpenny rate would meet all requirements in the ;next half year, if not, an additional p‘inny would probably suffice. Tee loan account had fallen to £36,297 6; 91, The Union assess able valuo bad risen to £405,000, The CLERK, in answer to the CIikIRMAN, said he thought it would come to this, tOat tor one half year they would want an extra penny rate. making the poor rate seve.npence instead of sixpence fmi one half year. It would be more convenient if he could put that penny upon the following half year rather than the impending half year. Be would be in a better position to advise them at the Octobar meeting whets the next call would be fixed. Be was glad to say that the large works in town were fully employed and if they had a light winter they might be able to meet all their liabilities without the necessity of an additional penny rate. timtisox asked what was the increase in the County rate. .</p>
        <p>The CLERK said it was a slight increase upon the previous call. It was a cpristantly growing rate. The CLERK stated that it would be necessary for the Beard to pass the following resolution :—.l That the estimates as prepared by the Clerk 133 approved and tipit at the special board meeting to he held en Wednes- day, October 9:h, the Clerk submit to the Board orders on the overseers of parishes for the payment of such sums as may be required as the contribution of the Parish to the common fund of the Union, and [Cr other expenses separately chargeable on the Parish." The Cuazaatate said he felt very strongly that the Board ought not to pass the resolution quite in the! form proposed. They ought to say definitely that the call shall not exceed sixpence during the coming half/ year. The Clerk's statement contained a good deal o matter which would be of use,especially to the new mem- tors of the Board if they would digest it. It was well '! known to the old members of the Board that an one particular point be and Mr Sutton had never agreed and he solonosed never would agree. That was in respect of the pay ment of a good deal of capital charges out of current revenue. In his last half yearly state- ment, the clerk emphasised the fact that the Board had spent £5,000 out of revenue on bnildinge, fittings, kz., IT) connection with the DPW buildings • The Board was now spending another £2,000 on new buildings out of revenue. It was of course satisfactory as far as it went that they bad beerVable to do this without making nails above sixpence in the £ on the rates. But as be skid six months ago, he for one would not consent to a call for mare than sixpence for the purpose of perpetu- ating a policy which he held to be a mistake. Be thought they bad been bearing more than their Share of Union expenses for it was clear that if the Board had not paid these large Sums out of revenue during the last four years the poor rate need not have been sixpence in the L. To go and charge soother penny in the LI for the sake of paying the £2,000 required for the new relief offices at Newton, seemed to be wrong in principle and was putting an unnecessary burden on the shoulders of the present generation of ratepayers. Mr FORSRAw Where is the money to come from ? The CHAIRMAN We can borrow it. We are doing something for the benefit of the community for many years to Come and we ought to spread the charge over a term of years.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : The interest and repayment of the loan would be an annual charge en the common fund charges. The Castro/AN My own feeling is, with all respect to the Clerk, that a boAer, more enlightened, and more satifactory policy might have given greater advantages to the ratepayers ad the poor without increasing the burden of the rates. Proceeding, the Chairman said he would read a paragraph from the Clerk's financial state- ment of six months ago which was allowed to pass without comment, but which be considered was a grave reflection on the Board. It read as follower—" Papers explanatory el union estimates were first prepared in my first year of office is Chairman of the Board, and have been continued during the period of my long service as Clerk. Examination of growth of local indebtedness, not of a locality, tut of all the king- dom, has been a frequent theme, treated in a mild spirit of criticism. In this form has avoidance of avoidsb'e loan debts been persistently advocated, and a few lines now upon the result may be usefully introdu- ced in the present paper. Purchase of the Padgate estate and erection thereon of the noble institution forming homes and schools for poor children bad been impracti- cable without the aid of borrowing powers. Yet the procedure Guardians had been long bent on was reluc- tantly delayed for rateable values to assume higher proportions, and for ease being given to loan burdens. Ia like manner, year by year, for many a year ,in succession, was the more and more pressing 'need for improved and extended accommodation for sick and helpless poor abided with, that assessable values might strengthen and union loan debts weaken. The purpose of possess- ing an infirmary in no respect second to the best of institutions of the ki::d to be found elsewhere, without additioa to local rates, has been achieved. Whereas the additional land, with buielings, fittings and works have required an outlay of £40,000 the present total out- standing loan liabilities of the Guardians norm all union lands, buildings and property. total £37,273,a debt that would be fully covered by a single rate or is 10d in the £on the present union rateable value." It was clear from that statement continued the Chairman, that reforms had been delayed in order that the assessable values might increase. He should strongly protest against an increased rate for the purpose of paying for tie New:on buildings which were properly a capital charge and ought not to be paid for out of current revenue.</p>
        <p>Mr FORSHAW :I cannot see anything wrong so long as it is paid out of a sovenpenny rate. The CHAIRMAW : The Clerk tells you it may be necessary to have a sixpenny rate. The CLERIC said his view was that they could not hive a sixpenny rate as a permanent rate if the Board did not take the coure he nad advocated all the way through. Be eontended thas if the Board, instead of paying off some of the capital expenditure out of revenue, had borrowed the whole of the amount, the repayment of principal and interest would have increased the rate to sevenpence in the £. By paying the cost of the Newton relief offices out of revenue it would only mean an increased rate of a penny in the £ for one half-year whereas the repayment of principal and interest would probably mean a permanent sevenpenny rate. Toe amount paid in interetit on account of loans was now over £l,lOO a year which in itself was eval to a penny in the £on the rate!. With regard to the statement that the present generation was paying more than their fair , share, he thought they ought to remember that the present geueiration had received from their predecessors a groat amount of property for which there was not the least charge. The present workhouse property was subject to no capital charge because it had been paid for ny generations who had preceded. This had been , accomplished by spendinr; thousands of pounds ) tof revenue without pressing hard on the ratepayers By that means they had kept the rate down to sixpence in the £. Be believed that the piling on of lea 4 debts would I bring about a day of reckoning. These debts were increasing throughout the country by thousands of pounds every year and now amounted to millions and millions of pounds on the supposition that those who were to follow us would find use for the properties and would be in a position to meet all the payments that were put upon them. Tne course the Board had I followed was the course pursued when he was Chairman. Tney might that day start a turning point, ibut what its effect would be was a matter tor the I future. _ . . _</p>
        <p>Mr FM/SHAW said one of the most pleasing features of the Clerk's report was the statement that the Board only owed £36 000 on an assessable value of £405,000. He had always approved of the policy of Mr Sattun tor obviating the rat,ing of loans. He preferred that the Board should pay their debts and not leave Them for succeeding generations. The Rev J H BIDDETTE considered it was an inoppor- tune discussion on an academic qaestion. . .</p>
        <p>The Caexamax : It is a very practical question when it means a penny in the ppunci on the rates.</p>
        <p>The R,v J El RIDDETrE s4id he took it that tha Chair- man'4 indictoo-nt ot toe Board was that the Guardians in the past had sacrifionl the iatere9ts ot the poor in order to keep the rate at sixpence. The CEIAIRIUN : That is my point. Tne Rev J H RIDDRTTE said they all admired the chairman's masterly grip of the finances whether they agreed with him or not. Be supposed they had reached the ead of their programsse of reform.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN . We haven't. The Rev J U RIDDETTE : I hope we have in regard to bricks and mortar.</p>
        <p>Mr FlicamaN : Oh no ! We are just beginning. Toe Rav 11 RIDDETTE : When you got your new offices I hope you will have got all you. require.</p>
        <p>Mr HICKNE' AN' Wnat abna"t a new. Wolknouge ?</p>
        <p>TN3 R.v J a RIDBETTE : 9(9/114 to me that the time to discuss the ques.cion of loans would be when you intend to build a ri.v7 INarYlcute. A 9 regards the paltry expense of £2,033 I certainly agree with Mr Sutton. _ . _</p>
        <p>The CRAIRM&amp;N 8/I.id he would move that an adden- dum be affixed to the resolution "That the charge 6131 11 be on the basis of a sixpenny call." The CLERK said his impression was that it would be more convenient to the officers not to put any addi- tional rate on the coming half year but to defer it to see if the money were required for the following half I year. Mr HICKIFIX!,I said the Cork had informed them that he might be able to carry the matter through without an increased rate. Tne CH &amp; taideisi : Suppose we have a bad spell of trade ,or a severe winter, the calls for out-reliet will disor- ganise the Clerk's calculations. The CLERK ! lam inclined to think they will be all right. ' Mr HICKMAN did not think the general public would grumble to an increased rate if they knew the poor received it but they would grumble if it were spent in bricks and mortar.</p>
        <p>Mt PILLING sec:laded the Chtirtna-i's proposition. Mr BILLINGE said IC W4B unr•erstood that the expense of the Newton relief offices would be paid out of revenue.</p>
        <p>The CSLIRMAN said no 81.1e1 uaders'anding existed in his mind. The moaey was to be paid out of rcvenue if a siKpcnny rate would suffice but not otherwise. •</p>
        <p>- - Or, vote being taken the Chairman's reaolutioa was carried by a small majority. The CLERK thoaght the Heard ought to pass a definite instruction authorising him to raise a loan for the new relief offices at Earle3town. My HICKMAN : The Beard can look into the matter and if it is band that th,! meoey cannot be paid out of I a sixpenny rate, a ap3cial meeting can be called to authorise the raising of a loan. The Ewan:ores said that could to done at the next 3oard meeting. The subject then dropped. THE NEW UNION OFFICES.</p>
        <p>Mr. JAMES SICELLAND, Of 65, Bacvey street, wrote EIS follows :—" flaying s in a local papr that yoa are requiring more Mil :e accommodation, I beg leave to offer four freehold shops and the assembly room which is now occupied by the Spiritualists society in Bewsey street for your consideration: If you think the sit e is suitable I shall be able to give you conditions and particulars of the same." CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>• It was deeded that the grocery, drapery, brush es and ironmongery tenders be referred to the House Com- mittee. The following tenders were accepted :—Coal. Richard Evans and Company Limited ; flour and oat- meal, Carter and hon. Bridge-street ; bread, James Hallwood ; beef, H Singleton ; milk, Farmers Stores dairy (for workhouse) and J W Houghton (for schools) ; clogs, William Jones ; potatoes, James Cliffe ; straw, B Grimshaw ; leather, E Pickavance aud Son. Bridge street, coffins, Lowden Welt; shoes,R Roberts and Son</p>
        <p>ing, Hav;ldine. \srlA‘-•4&gt;. • ••• .c.f .." • la • BREW MINI 111 E. - .•</p>
