      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 13ra 1901.</p>
        <p>WITH the assurance that The Wild Beast of President MCKINLEY has Anarchism.</p>
        <p>been fortunate enough to escape the fatal effect's intented by the assassin's bullet, comes the revulsion of feeling against the criminal, and all who may be fitly branded with him as murderers, in intent if not act, of all ruling personages. The question naturally arises, what is to be done with the species anarchist, which has sworn vengeance and death against all forms of government and those who represent them? The purpose of the Anarchist is ostensible, and is a subject of boast. It is planned and carried out with a sort of mock heroism that is catching. It has frequently, also, an element of meanness about it, as in the present case, when an alien uses the hospitality and right of asylum afforded to him or his family to plot the overthrow of the very liberty which has ensured his resid- ence and freedom of action. It is an unreasonable abuse of liberty in the name of liberty ; it is the wild beast of humanithand the first impulse born of resentment is to shoot it down. This method, however, is too crude to be effectual either as punishment or prevention; and though there are not wanting advocates of the summary form of vengeance on both sides of the Atlantic, the com- mon sense of humanity, not to say its finer feel- ings, rebels against it. Lynch law is neither just nor wise, and if civilised forms of government are to have any claim to superiority over the savage and the Anarchist, it must be in their essential wisdom and justness that this superiority must be shown. Anarchism is only a refined form of savagery, to which it is essentially a throw-back, and it must be stamped out: ligut there must be no question of sacrificing the possibly innocent with the guilty, nor of making written or published attacks on the wrong- doing of Governments in their impersonal capacity a pretence for restricting liberties that are dear. to all true men. Given these two provisos, it seems as if no effort could be too strenuous to hunt out those who preach and practice murder as a fine art, and an act of high politics. Neither is there any punishment too severe that is known in civilised countries. Human life is more sacred even than liberty, and perhaps if Governments themselves were more careful on this point of ethics, they might persuade those who are in error on the point to become wiser. To kill or execute an Anarchist is a good thing ; but to cure him is better. 1311 the wild beast, and you have only his skin remalning. Cage him, and you may turn him to many useful purposes.</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR RUCKER, in The Hypothetical in Science, his presidential address at the opening of the British Association at Glasgow, rendered a signal public service when he reminded the savants who had gathered round him of the hypothetical basis upon which most of their work rests. He approached the task in a duly humble spirit. The brilliant records of the past had already been fully dealt with by his two predecessors in the chair, and it only remained for him to fulfil the humble task of fixing attention on some of the hypotheses</p>
        <p>and assumptions on which the fabric of modem theoretical science has been built. In doing so it may yet be proved that the learned doctor hag done the greatest work of the three, and that which is fraught with most practical utility. has become so customary on the part of the scien- tific expert to be first axiomatic, then dogmatic, and finally to interpose barriers against further progress which are neither scientific nor reason- able, that it is well, as Dr RUCKER has done, to look around at the beginning of the new era and inquire how far the foundations are soundgupeo which so brilliant a fabric has been raised. The scientist, like the cleric, is greatly exposed to the temptation of becoming a doctrinaire. Us has his theory, and houses it as a touchstofl by which he tests every new idea claiming 01 position in the category of truth. By his par- ticular saw he adjudges every new proposition, and by the judgment it stands or falls. This is all very well for limited purposes, but it is apt to lead to a narrowness of outlook, and the certaie rejection of much valuable wheat amid the inevit- able quantity of chaff. It need not be supposed that assumption is Unnecessary or valueless in the pursuit of truth. Substantial progress in ail science is impossible with the hypothetical basis • There must be a working hypothesis, which, if not universally, is at :east generally applicable to the phenomena pertaining to the subject matter 113 hand. In other words, in order to classify your facts and render some sort of intelligible system out of the chaos which nature at first sight pre- sents to the student, you must construct a theorf which will fit in as near as possible with the facts and phenomena with which you wish to deal. This was what was done for astronomy by the promulgation of KErmen's laws and the for- mulation of the Newtonian hypothesis of gravita- tion. It tis what has been done for chemistry by the atomic theory. It has brought the investigation of these subjects within the domains of what is termed an exact science, But people are apt to forget that, generallY speaking, a hypothesis remains a hypothesis, and is liable to be upset at any time by some newer revelation of the truth of things, or some fitter adjustment of the facts that are known-- and of others not previously known—but whith have subsequently come to light. This is what the doctrinaire does not like. He would rather have the old, because he understands it, and is attached to its formulas. They have done him good ser- vice, and he cannot face the task [of going over all the ground again. At the same time, there as° others who will seek fresher fields for investigation. Their cry is: "Prove all things." So the battle goes constantly on, and it is an appropriate spirl in which to enter upon:a new century, if scientific investigators are invited to search into the founds' tions of their faith, to see if they be sound. Net. that Professor RUCKER thinks them otherwise- While in a most able address he has shown the difficulties and limitations of the germ theory, which he appropriately selects as a suitable illus- tration of his theme, he does much to establish it as the most probable basis at present known for an intelligent appreciation of the nature of matter solar at least as certain well-known properties are concerned. At the same time he admits that much is left unaccounted for, and he seems Pre- pared for other hypotheses which have been put forward, or may be yet put forward, claiming ee equal, or, it may be, a superior place in the world' of investigation. That is the most vales" feature of the presidential address. It implies , a well-founded reverence for the old, but gives [us° a welcome to the newer aspects of thought, ana the newer forms of experiment. That is the srarl of true progress; in it will be found at once the hope, the life, and the salvation of humanity.</p>
        <p>An indiscreet effort has been made in Wales to narrow down the liberties conferred on the traveller to obtain a drink after walking three miles. Two men were said to have acknowledged that their pedestrian exercise was undertaken solely that they might obtaiu refreshment, other" wise prohibited, and hence the proceedings. After a patient hearing both cases were dismissed. would be rather a pity if thirsty men, who go oeD for a day's enjoyment in the country, should hey° it placed on their consciences to say which was the dominant thought in their minds—the pleasure of pacing the country roads, or the res and the quaff of ale afterwards. It is an unnecee; sary test of a man's honesty and shoal" be avoided. If, on the other hand, the three- mile limit is abused,and it is thought that a longe distance would meet the needs of the bond fide traveller, Parliament should pass legislation .0e the subject. The licensing law should be definite and clear, and no attempts either to evade it or to over-apply it should be tolerated. As a matter .of fact, very little harm is done by the three-mile excursions, except that in the evening they some; times get noisy, and disturb the peacefulness o,i hamlets on the Sunday evening, when their strident shouts jar on the ear. But a little more vigilance on the part of the police should put ao end to disturbances of that kind.</p>
        <p>While preachers in less militant portions of the United Kingdom are content to deplore the dese- oration of the Sabbath and urge their hearers t,(l discountenance it, the "releegious pal rsons" of Andrew's seem to have another method of dealing with the matter. A certain party of visitors hal" ing a villa with a lawn attached, ventured to dulge in a game of lawn tennis one Sunday after noon lately. This was too much for their neigh' bours, and the matter was broached at the Town Council meeting. The provost, in dealing vgi°l the question whether such practices could he stopped, said a law was passed in 1579 against ti-la playing of games on the Sabbath, and it had never been repealed. As, however, the penalty under the Act seems only to be is 8d the matter does not look very serious for the visitors. The S! Andrew's incident is amusing in its way, I'° it touches what threatens to be a very seriow question in our national life. The spirit of difference to the observance of Sunday is a grow,: ing spirit. The signs of it are to be seen ever? day, and nowhere more than in the Metropol,..l9 and its suburbs, where pleasure taking and rulim-; ing is the chief occupation of a large proportion the population on the Day of Rest. Not only Is idleness and the neglect to attend a place of W 0 ship seen on every hand but the practice is becen.l.: ing fashionable—not only in ,4 society " but uf lower grades—of arranging games,cricket mateh° and the like for that day. The bicycle, too, ia great seculariser, and the "spin into the countr!id claims many devotees who half a decade ago igew„t not by any chance have neglected attendance "ev church. The question is perhaps one of the °0 serious with which the preachers and the teac",,,M, of the present day are faced, but the St. Andre i&amp; method of dealing with it does not commend itse Another step has been taken with regard to tallt: future of Warrington Bridge. It is only a nwyjnas or two since a scheme for building a new oiled 00 n brought before the Council and finally rejecte.s _ve the ground of expense. Probably it would the been adopted but for the fact that town is at present very heavily ri:and and has a number of schemes ea tion which are calculated to prevent any reducivee_ in the rates for some time to come. Last e the Council at its monthly meeting had, °Pore, two alternative schemes before ic ana „</p>
