      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>4, The attempt upon the life of Presileht for several clays past dwarfed in importance ad interest every other topic, home or foreign, of tihlic thought and discussion. At the time of ~.„titing the arrest of Miss Goldman of Chicago, and 4,;t1 continues. and even rapid progress of Mr 4(3l'llioley are the leading items of intelligence eug taiked about. Czolgosz, the would-be assassin ce he made confession of the crime, has per- Vsted in saying that it was his own act, conceived ul7 himself, inspired by soother, and that other a ' Woman—the wretched fanatic known as Emma i 'ioldraan, whose theories have long been notorious. Goldman became'an anarchibt of the danger- ;" type when the Chicago executions look place :11 1887. and Czolgo.z traces back his own craze to • sooae prominent personage to one of her ora- t°,ns. Perhaps the worst and basest feature of it is the fact that most of these anarchists are °reigners who have left, or been driven from their 1711 countries, and been offered refuge in "the land ~', liberty"; men travelling about and finding an I..BYIUm in America, even as very many of them do our own country. It is the very nature of such miscreants to turn their weapons against the land s ; • harbours and the hand that feeds them, and it `8 high time international action was taken to 4Weep them from the face of the earth.</p>
        <p>'or the second time within six months the 'llerphilly district of the South Wales coalfield has 4en the scene of an underground disaster. The 4)le before this was accounted for by the inflam- -I,ksbility of coaldust,and the probability is' that the litteSt is due to the same cause. The scene of the r'tectirrencet is about eight miles from Cardiff. On th;s industrious valley the hand of death has again len,though the death*roll may not be a particu- tajlv heavy one as disasters of this kind go. With )"at splendid alacrity and courage that are stimu- ja,• t,ed by danger and the presence of death, the re- gang went to work late on Tuesday night, but e earliest news indicated such embarrassments a! to make it probable that for some time the 'Saisition of anything like complete information Iv°llld be prevented. All that could be ascertained 410aP to midnight V 789 that there were about twenty ven in the pit at the time an explosion occurred, ))4., about 6.30 in the evening. and that these must I:ve been exposed to the deadly influence of the tirae-blast. But more will be known by the time ,e, lines appear. The district generally has an Iheasant reputation in regard to these disasters.</p>
        <p>In connection with the present outbreak of I ;441411-pox in London the interesting and gratifying noticed tbat some doctors are so unfamiliar the once exceedingly prevalent malady that ilner ailments have in several instances been Mk- t 11 for it. If anyone had told our great grand- \s'ouners that the scourge of their day would become tb.rate at the beginning of the twentieth century oft,:t many skilled doctors would never meet with a opo.e, in their ordinary practice. the prediction (1,01,1 d have obtained but very little credence. Yet ttie:' is the fact. The 3ledical Officer of Health in Dor of our largest cities has just stated in his re- it bt that the last case recorded there was two and ali Years ago. It ie pleasing te hear that the t„ineeent outbreak in London is kept well under, tre,eB,,t;he close every-day association with the met- from all parts of the provinces could cieezbily fail to have serious consequences if the vhitC6e became uncontrollable. Great care has en° been taken to isolate all suspicious cases.</p>
        <p>Questions relating to rural teaching and the exo- dus of the labouring population to the towns are re- ceiving special attention just now in some of our country districts,and as one step towards the solu- tion of the drifting to the towns problem. it has been suggested that less time should be devoted in rural schools to what is popularly known as "book lea ming,"and more to familiarising the young with all the woaders of country lifehand the possibilities of turning the knowledge so gained to the best ad- vantage. There is no doubt that much in this di- rection can be achieved by wisely organised local effort, and it is quite reasonable to suppose that if such effort proved productive of really valuable re- sults the Government could without difficulty be persuaded to extend a helping hand to those la- bouring in a comparatively neglected field. The provision of experimental gardens and promotion of technical classes for the teaching of some of the minor cultures,as well as:some of the domestic crafts and accomplishments, would be a sine quei non for the diversion of the youthful rural mina from tee more or less unsatisfactory attractions of city or town life. What seems to be needed in country schools for girls is practical instruction in cooking, nursing, the laws of health, and elementary sanita- tion. For boys the curriculum would be more ex- tensive and even more interesting, since it would include object lessons on all that the meadows, the hedgerows, the orchards, and the vsgetable and flower gardens can produce. The :change from the modern rural day school to the evening continua- tion school should be made automatic fond alluring To this end it is very desirable that local conferen- ces should be held of persons interested in educa- tion, and particularly that which has a bearing up- on the well-being of the young in agricultural districts.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt that the Commission appointed by the Government to inquire into the communica- bility of bovine tuberculosis to man has an impor- tant, and at the same time an interestins work en- trusted to it. Bat, whatever the scientific verdict may be on the subject, there will remain the pre- sent necessity for boiling or pasteurising the family milk before using it. This advice has been given again and again, but it is ignored with fatal fre- quency, and much trouble and public risk have been the penalty for the carelessness. Reports presented to health committees of large and important muni- cipalltuthorities in the provinces from time to time mention many cases of scarlet lever occurring in houses supplied from some particular source, and boiling the milk is shown to have been systematic- ally neglected or only partially enforced in the homes where the disease has thus made its appear- ance. The experience, of course, is only a repetition of what has occurred many times before. But it is still necessary to urge, for instance, that it is not safe to give instructions to servants to see that the milk,which may be the vehicle for several diseases, is boiled. Neglect of sufficient precaution to see that the milk is properly boiled, and that each supply,morning and afternoon,is so treated, has led to a good deal of carelessness on the part of servants, and to consequent mischief even where heads of families had plumed themselves upon their excep- tional care in this matter; while absolute indiffer- ence doss much to negative the efforts made by health departments to combat certain well-known foes to humanity.</p>
        <p>Much has been written and said during the past few weeks of the lack of proper consideration on the part of the "tripper." Not only does he con- trive to make himself disliked by many of the people who cater for him at seaside and other places, but as railway employ:3 too well know he has often a stupid way on the return journey of throwing empty bottles and other hard su.bstancas out of the carriage window of the fast moving train. He may not be the only offender in this respect, but for some weeks past he can be said to have been the chief. It is unfortunately the case that the practice both in summer and winter, obtains to a much greater extent than is generally sup- posed. One railway company during last month had two of their permanent way staff seriously , injured in this way. Train travellers might make it a matter of conscience, not only themselves to refrain from doing that which is so fraught with danger to others, but to place a corresponding res- traint upon anyone in the same carriage who appears tempted to commit the offence. Some of , those persons burdened with a consciousness that they were 'horn to put the world right must occa- sionally travel by train, and might be expected to carry the matter a little further even, but everyone has neither time nor the inclination to prosecute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>j</p>
        <p>- !lady discussion this scheme was adopted, on a te being taken, by the narrow margin ens vote. The proposal which has now "een officially sanctioned is the one cora- l:110111y known as the cantilever scheme 4ed it has the great merit of being economical, the. estimated cost amounting to only £3,125 4gninst a sum of £20,000 which would have been ,required for the construction of a new bridge. ;there was an amendment that the matter be de- erred for another month,apparently because some members were not present when the resolution ,adepting the cantilever scheme was passed by the `zeneral Purposes Committee and because others Were unmistakably anxious to gain time in order In push forward the more ambitious proposal.</p>
        <p>It is a matter of some surprise to us that so I2s,.,any members should be desirous of spending .f.,)(0,000 on a new bridge. Obviously the wiser course seems to be to devise some expedient by vhich the difficulty of crossing the river may be 'essened with a corresponding reduction of ex- Dense. The idea of placing cantilevers on the outside of the present structure to carry two ten- foot footpaths and include the existing footpaths I.ti the roadway may not appeal to the aesthetic Individuals who look upon Warrinton bridge as one of the few works of archi- tectural art the town possesses, but it is Practical, economical and will relieve the congestion of traffic at this point to such an ex- tent that no further provision will be needed for Some years. We do not go so far as to say that it Will suffice for twenty years, but even if the -ezpedient does for half that time it will, in our °Pinion, be justified, even if a new bridge is then a, n absolute necessity. Drawings of the bridge as n will appear when altered were submitted,and to ;hose who do not profess to have such a highly- 'oeveloped artistic perception the bridge looked none the worse and much more commodious.</p>
        <p>The question of the Mayoralty has suddenly become the one of paramount importance 111 Warrington. As a rule it does not Op up until the end of September, but ris year it has sprung into prominence oefore the accustomed time. Why? The cir- !:lFastances seem to suggest that the reason for unusual haste is to be found in the absence of the Mayor on holidays. At any rate the ucus meeting of the Council was held last hursday evening last instead of three weeks oence, five Aldermen were re-appointed, and t. he Mayoralty was discussed and voted 9150 n. We regret to state that the feel- on the matter was not unanimous. ;11 either direction unanimity would have been 11 preferable to dissension, and we should have "Ised to see that unanimity based on the grounds of equity and precedent. Instead, twelve members voted for the proposition that Mr J Charlton Parr be asked to accept the office, and nine for the touater proposal in favour of Alderman Henry 'Roberts the present Mayor, while eight did not vote at all. This leaves matters in a very awk— ward, if interesting, state and the outcome of it 411, we would not venture to predict.</p>
        <p>Naturally, the Conservative members of the '-'ouncil were the main supporters of Mr Parr, !hough one or two Liberals did not remain loyal to the political beliefs they are supposed to hold thd the same may be said of one or two Tories I:.)iere was a little cross-voting and, on the whole, this is not surprising to those who have Vatched the trend of recent events. One 'and all spoke highly of the services of Alderman Roberts, but the pievailing opinion atnong the Conservative members was that they should have the norninatiOn of Mayor for the Qoronation year, seeing that they had agreed to the Liberals having the preference in the two past sears. This would be saGisfactory enough if it had not been the rule rather than the exception `.4l.lring the past ten years at least to ask the retir- Ing Mayor to accept office for a third year. More- °ver it is manifestly unfair,after the arduous;work the past two years—work which the Mayor has 4ttended to with exceptional ability and industry ----to cast him on one side when the third year comes simply because it happens to be coronation year,— the special significance of Which for Warrington is highly problematical. It atilt more unfair to go outside the Council for a uccessor,as well as a grave reflection on the capac- ty of the members of the Countil. And finally, ho .whole business savours very much of i base ngratitude "to Alderman Roberts for the splendid work he has done. In all prob- ability Mr Parr will hate nothing to do with the •Alayoralty when the request of this small majority Is placed before him, so that the advocates of a change look like finding themselves in a serious eronia very shortly. That is the status quo, 43 " sequel to which must soon disclose itself. We -4wait it with interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
