      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>POOR RATE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE POOR RATE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday about forty more persons were summoned for neglecr.ing to pay their poor rate.llalf of the cases were settled and in the rest the usual orders were made.</p>
        <p>T. PAIIL'SIWESLEYAN CHURCH RUNCORN. On Monday the meetings for women, conducted by Mrs Wells, in connection with tbis place of worship were resumed for the season after the summer break. There was a good attendance of members and workers. The meetings have been a great benefit to a number of homes. HOPE HALL MILE CLASS, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Bible Class in cinnection with the above Mission, whica is conducted by Mr A Jones, went for a most enjoyable trip to Southport on Wednes- day. Fortunately the weather was all that could be desired, and the fifty persons who went had a splendid time of it. After regaling themselves with the fine view to be obtained from the pier head and promenade, the parks and gardens had a large share of the attention of the trippers. A drive through the suburbs completed a capital day's entertainment, and the return journey was made in good time. The catering was done bv one of the finest reitaurants in Southport, and after tea Mr Jones was suitably thanked for the trouble which he had taken on behalf of the class.</p>
        <p>- INTERESTING WEDDING AT RUNCORN. At Runcorn Parish Church on Monday the marriage took place of Miss Jessie McClean, second daughter of the late Mr David McClean, of Stone- leigh Cottage, Higher Runcorn, and Mr Samuel Fred Pearson, of Parkside, Oreenway-road Runcorn The event excited considerable interest, and there was a goodly number ot friends of both parties pre- sent. The ceremony was performed by rhe Rev E S Oliver. The bride was given away by her uncle. 39r Arthur Riley, of Bridge-stre.A,and was attended by Edith McClean as bridesmaid. The bridegroom's best man was Mr Thomas Bowyer, his brother-in- law. After the ceremony the wedding party repaired to Stoneleigh Cottage, where a number of friends were entertained. Later in the day the happy pair left for Llandudno to spend the honey- moon. They were the recipients of a large number of presents. knell MASONRY AT RUNCORN. The annual installation ceremony ot the Bridge- water chapter of the Royal Arch (758) was held on Wednesday. at the Masonic Hall, Runcorn. The following officers were invested :—Comp's, J J W Potter, Z; H Branch, H; H U Linaker, J; Alfred Dodd, SE; AE Jervis, S ; J Raschen, ; Pll Atinett, treas ; J White, PG T, DU ; J Hill, Jan. The installation ceremony was conducted by Comps J White ; F 11 Smith, IPZ;J Armstrong, PP U H; T Goff, P P, Et St Br ; S Davies, PZ;PP C, Std. B ; Wllankinson, P Z, P P, SUJ; JW White suer 11 Annett. The usual banquet was afterwards held and the loyal toasts were honoured in the custom- ary manner. KITCHEN RANGES.; Tile Grates, Marble and Wood Chimney pieces. 4.5. GAnwn,sl tess. Bridge-et—Adv.</p>
        <p>A VAGRINATION CASE AT RUNCORN, At tbe Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Messrs F J Norman in the chair and R H Posnett, Thomas Bold, Lightburn-street, applied for a certi- ficate to exempt his child, Frank Bold, born on the sth June. from being vaccinated. Applicant said he had a conscientious objection and believed it would be prejudicial to the health of his child. No further questions were asked and the application was granted. RUNCORN JURYMEN AND THE PARK. At the conclusion of an inquest at the Runccrn Police Court on Monday evening, a juryman proposed that the shillings the jury had received should go to the park.—There was no response from the jury, and the juryman said, "There's my shilling," suiting the action to the word by planking the money on the desk.—The Coroner (Mr R Dobson): Each gentleman can please him self.---fhe Juryman: Beg pardon for proposing it—The Coroner: It has Nothing to do with me, you know. You must please yourself.—The Juryman : came to my mind.—Another Juryman : There is no fund open for the park, it is being formed out of the ratee.—A third Juryman: Far better give it to the Cottage Hospital Fund.—Even this suggestion met with no response, be- yond the Coroner reiterating that the jurymen could please themselves. LOCAL WEDDING. RILLCROSS—LEATRWOOD, , At SG Paul;s Wesleyan Church on Thursday after- noon a very interesting weding took place be- tween Mr J Killcross to Miss M E Leathwood. Both parties are exceedingly well known in Runcorn, Mr Killcross being a prominent church official at Trin- ity Church, Runcorn and Miss Leathwood being closely connected with the St Paul's Wesle3 an Church. Toere was a full choral service. The Rev J B Foster officiated and there was a large and fashionable congregation. Mr W Cooke, A.R.C.0., presided at the organ and previous to the ceremony played several wedding vol- untaries. The bride Was attired in a most becoming manner in a white silk grenadine gown, with orange blos,om and looked exceedingly pretty. The bride was given away by her father Mr Peter Leathwood, and the whole ceremony was conducted in a most impressive manner. While the newly-wedded pair were signing the register the organist played Wagner's " Wedding March." The groomsmen were Messrs Caleb Killcross and J W Leathwood, and the bridesmaids were the Misses M Leathwood and Withington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>rHE EXAMINER FRIJ)AY SEi3TEMBER</head>
        <p>rHE EXAMINER FRIJ)AY SEi3TEMBER</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF A RUNCORN FOOTBALLEA.</p>
        <p>The mortal remains of the late Mr Joseph Nickson, one of the most popular footballers the town has ever produced, were conveyed to their last resting plaee, the Runcorn Cemetery, on Sunday afternoon. amid general expressions of sympathy and regret. Flags floated at half-mast at many of the buildings, and the drawn blinda and closed shattors along the route of the proces- sion bore eloquent testimony to the popularity of the deceased among his townsfolk. The eortege lined up in High-street, where the deceased lived, early in the fore- noon, and as the funeral was a semi-public one there would be at least two hundred persons present, intent to pay their last tribute to one who was so highly respected. Heading the procession was the Runcorn Football Club who were represented by Messrs Walter Jones, J Edison, J Mouton, Grounds, C Pownel, A Ellison, A Abel, A Grounds, W Faulkner, H Cawley, El Wright, Robinson, Gayter, Farmer, Rea :e, Langley, Warder, Stubba, ringbolt, Richardson, J Butterworth, Faulkner, Jones and Jolley. Toe Lily of the Valiey, Grand United Order of Oidtellows :—Bros J Holt, S Littler, R Evans,W Millington,W Worthington, W Weave, W Turtle, Coamberlain, A Derbyshire, R 0' Nield,J Griffitha,R Garvie,C Basnett,F Bolt,? Deacon, W Crabbe, C Ball, F Jackson, E Jones, J Lightfoot, T Caruthers, J Caruthers, T Ward, P Whitlow, S Hughes, J Hughes, J Bowyer, S Haddock, G Hignett, G Lee, J Lightfoot. W Thompson, A Hasoell, J Wainwright, G Jones, R Smithin,T Harding, S Forster, T Williams, G Woods, T Crawford. The Liberal Club; Messrs Brimelew, P Jones, W Rushton, J Rawlinsoa, W Riley. J Strange, J Parker, T Illidge, R Hughes, A Worrall, S Bailey, P Cornea, T Shallcross. and J Smith. The Bentley Football and Cricket Club ; D Joseph, J Joseph, D Raffell, 3 Jones, D Rigby, H Ballanger, F Brown, H Citeave, H Platt, W Bowser, W Wakefield, II Raffell, S Rhodes, A Harvey, G Parry, W Simons. Among others present were Messrs A Clarke, R Wilsosu, H Nickaon, E Robinson, B Jolly, 1' Brown, 9 Houghton, W Beckett, Betty, Wilson, Tatlock, W Houghton, J Hignett, Vveston, Lightfoot, Urmson, F Wilkinson. The carriers were Messrs Woodwood, J Ford, Woods and Robinson. The chief mourners were Mrs Nickson, wife; Mr and Mrs J Nickson, parents ; Mrs Bay, sister ; J Nickson, brother ; Miss G Fearnett and Miss A Clarke, neices ; T Fearnett, father-in law ; 3 Fearnett and W Fearnett, brotbers-in-law ; James Fearnett, brothet-indaw ; Mrs %V Fearnett, J Cooker and Mr Fearnett. The funeral service was conducted impressively by the Rev W Scboles M.A., and the service of the Oadfellows was read by Mr S Littler. Wreathes were sent by " Father and Mother," "John Riley," " Jack and Arthur," Mr and Mrs S Houghton, G R Billington (Preston). "The Liberal Club," "George and Gerty," " Dear Wife:* Mr and Mrs Clarke.Mr and Mrs Wakefield,Mre C Houghton. Mr and Mrs W Fearnett, "The Employees of Moore and C 0.," Mr and Mrs C Ball,Mr and Mrs 0 ckaworth, " Jim and Beseie," Mrs T Fearnett, Mr and Mrs Parry, "Annie and Jessie "and " Runcorn Friends."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of Vais Board was held on Tuesday afternoon at Donor% Work'pouse. Prevnt Messrs Joseph Stokes (in the chair),A. R Norman,J.P.C.O. (vice-chairman), T L-e,T Woods,'T Reynold 3, J Douglas the members of the Rural D•strict Coyne.' and Messrs. F Aghton (clerk), W E Rough (deputy clerk), and J Cunnalo (master).</p>
