      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BOARDS OF GUARDIAN AND TRRIR OF FICIERS.</head>
        <p>BOARDS OF GUARDIAN AND TRRIR OF FICIERS.</p>
        <p>Mr TATCI7;t-t; Walker clerk to the Uayfiel4 Union for- warded the following resoluqon and asked for the support of the Runcorn Board :—" That this Board request the Right Hon. Walter Long, M.P., president of the Local Government Board to bring in a bill in the next session of Parliament to give Boards of Guardians in E oeland and Wales full power to discharge any of their officers for misconduct without having first to appeal to the Local Goverment Board for their sanction ; and that nrinted circulars bs sent to each Board If Guardians in Eagland and Wales, asking the members to rises a resolution supporting the request for such a Bill to be introduced, believing that if Boards of Guardians (the representatives of the ratepayers) are vested with such powers the many scandals that ocour in the Poor Law Unions in England and Wales will diminish " It was decided to support the action of the Hayfield, Union on the motion of Mr Talbot seconded by Mr Antwies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>Mr Wright submitteci.--;-;;;rt as to the probable balance in favour of or against each parish at the end of the current half year ending March. 1901. the esti. mate (calculated on the present assesiable value) of the amount of coutrib rtions required from etch parish dur- ing the half-year ending lady Day 1902. The total capital for the current half year was £8,568 and the probable expenditure £7,235, having a balance at the end of the current half year of £1,333. The probable expenditure during the half year ending Lady Day 1902 was as follows :—Common charges, £2,585 ; county rate, £2,890 ; balance and contingency £2,544, total £8,019. Deducting from this a probable balance at Mionaelrnas 1901 there remained the sum of £6,665 required from the over- seers. The asaessab:e value of the Uoi In was £210,619 A rate of 7id in the on the assessable value would produce £6,577 and it was recmnmended that a call for £6,888 (being f glint to the call for the corresponding period of last year) be made upon the overseers. Mr Wright remarked that it was the same as last year and the report was adopted.</p>
        <p>THE POll3 LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>On tbe prop-o;i:ion- of Sr Wright it wan req.3l7el that Messrs Spruce. Lewis, Woodward, and the Clerk be deputed to attend the paor law conference at Chester on the 22ad inst. THE PANTASAPH ORPHANAGE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that some time ago a committee was appointed to visit the Pantass ph Orphanage. He was sorry the Chairman of the Committee, Mr Unaker. was not present but a letter embodying his views and that at the Committee had been sent to him. In it he stated that the care bestowed on the girls was such that nothing indicated the class of society from which they were drawn. .The education was an ideal one, all domestic work being thoroughly taught from washing to fancy needlework. They also had to pass the s:h standard. There would be less squalor if the system were adopted more extensively. The Chairman endorsed the opinion expressed and said that a notable feature was that any child haying any special gift had it care- fully developed. Mr Lee supported the communication as a member of the committee.</p>
        <p>THE FIRE CIMMITTEE. Mr Moseley Williams reported that the Committee had met the representatives of M(ssrs Merry weather, and they had approved the suggestlona made by them against fire. He (the speaker) understood it would not cost at the outside above 43150. Mr Reynolds seconded, aud the recommendation was approved.</p>
        <p>OUT-RELIEF. Mr T Wrisht reportrd that the CommittAo harl made payments for out-relief as follows :—Rancarn. £B5 ; Frodsbam, £2O; Budworth. £26.</p>
