      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The Thelwall nuisance again troublel the spirits of the worthy councillors at Dutton on Tuesday. Thanks; however, to the most energetic committee appointed to examine first hand the source or sources of the effluvium this truly übiquitous odour has at length been, to a certain extent, located. One thing that the committee are quite sure about is that most of them have entirely altered their opinions on the subject. And another thing is that the cammittee are now posi- tively convinced that hie smell does not arise from Messrs Long's tanyard. To an outsider it seems mast peculiar that the Council should employ experts; incur expense; go to no end of trouble in listening to the epistolary com- munications from inhabitants re the nuisance squabble, almost like Kilkenny cats in considerina wnether Messrs Long's were the cause of the original sin ;and then,alter months of worry,annoyance and expense, appoint a committee who calmly visit the scene and cake a most business-like view or the situation by adopting measures which are bound to ensure a proper result. Had this committee been appoiNted when the question was first mooted how different all things would have been And yet there are persons who say tat red tape is entirely confined to the Gtt 0. But then these people do not know the inner working of a Dis- trict Council !</p>
        <p>Runcormans will be interested to learn that com- mittees in the County Council and the Rural Dis- trict Council are going to meet at an early date to consider the making of a new road from Runcorn railway station to Weston Point. If such a road becomes an accomplished fact it will truly be .e. boon and a blessing to men. Not only should it have the financial effect of increasing the value of property along the line of route, but it would also brisg the outlying townships of Weston and Weston Point in closer communica- tion with their centre. This will presumably have a tendency to make trade brisker between Weston and Runcorn and further strengthen social inter- course between the inhabitants. We merely want a few electric cars to run round the district so that Halton and the Point can be switche I together as it were; the electric light at a few street corners and laces to make the gloom of winter night a little less intmse; some of the slum property, which is a blot on the fair fame of the town, knocking down; afe more of the streets rolled and paved to make the town a little more bearable in dirty weather; a range of baths in which the inhabitants can dis- port themselves; and then Runcorn will not be a bad place to live in after all.</p>
        <p>For the past few weeks Runcorn has been verit- ably passing through a period ot sadness and de- pression. Sickness stalks abroad in our midst, and typhoid and influenza are raging very severly in- deed. It has been Et itel that there were at least a dozen funerals on Sunday but this is entirely erroneous. Still there were sufficient to show that Death with his resping sickle is busily garnering his grim horsed. The fall of the year is always a trying time to young and old alike and to this fact may, psrhaps, he attributable much of the preva- lent disease. The town is much the poorer by the death of the sturdy Wesleyan veteran, Joseph RIX, at the ripe old age of eighty, and the lamented decea.e of Mr. Joseph Nickson, the foot- baller, at the early age of twenty-eight, affords another il'ustration of the uncertainty of life. It was a poet who had studied life very closely, and , who was doubtless familiar with the cynicism of Ecclesiastes who wrote : Lire is a lottery awl all things show it ; I thought so once but now I now It.</p>
        <p>once more the reign of the ball and the-willow is at an ena and the.. popular winter pastime has now commenzA. Cricket in Runcorn does not awaken muck enthusiasm among the townfolks and even among the juniors, the summer pastime is played but indifferently. This, is probably due to the fact that Runcorn is essentially a town in which the working element predominates and, as cricket is a recrevion more for the gentler folk rather than the workingman, it is correspondingly regarded with disfavour by the majority of the community. Be its ir may every one is glad when stumps are drawn for the last time and the leathern oval displaces the comp)sitim round for anothe r season.</p>
        <p>Perhaps the most widespread hobby in nuncpra during the summer has bean the almost inordinate love for the use of the camera which has sprung up in the breasts of toys, youths and men. Nearly every parson who can possibly beg borrow or steal a" Kodak "is the proud posQessor of one, and the sacred shrine of the dark room which was once the secret haunt of the professional, is now desecrated by a ruthless horde of amateurs. As a hobby it is unquestionably one of the finest as wall as one of the most expensive if properly managed. And though one is inylriably tempted to whistle "Up came Johnny with his camera" when some enthusiastic " snap-shottist " is seen looming in the distance there is no doubt but the camera sileatly does the community a good service if only by keeping a certain section out of mischief by employing them in worrying over the torments of toning, fixing chemicsl 53111:i00, P 0 P. and the other stages of photography. Everybody knows the distich vrhish tells that a certain gentleman, who shall be nameless, finds plenty of work for idle hands to do. Further comment is needless.</p>
        <p>Coroner Dobson is a lover of details and as ouch is a most punctilious observer of small matters which if absolutely ignored would have no harmful effect but would con.iidarablv facilitate the busi- ness of the Coroner's Court. It is questionable whether any other coroner r.quires the jury to be sworn first at the police station, afterwards walk them to view the body, even though it be a couple of miles away, and then bring them back to court to hear the evidence. Most coroners allow the jury to see the body first, meet at the station afterwards and then go on directly with the lowness in hand, or take the in- quest wherever the body may be .lying. The whole of the proceeding at Runcorn is absurdity itself and is a gross waste of time to busy buginess men. It is, however, but another instance of official red tape, and, though, perhaps enjoined by law, should be a custom more honoured in the breach, than the observance.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn police are to be congratulated. For the last fortnight there has practically been no crime in the to vn and the morals of the inhabitants must be undergoing a process of evolu.ion. In this happy state of things the gentlemen in blue play a part which mot people seriously uoder-rate. PciNV have any idea of the monotonous round of duties which encircle the policeman's lifs,and when their efforts to preserve order and keep the peace meet with success, it is the least we Can d 3 to thank them fur their hard work.</p>
        <p>POOTA LL. The wearers of the green opened their season somewhat disaurouslv on Saturday when they succumbed after a stiff fight to th-) onslaught of the burly Yorkthiremen of Ifni!. In mlre ways than one the defeat is to be regretted for it not only places the club two points to the hal od the Northern League table but it also causes a certain amount of unnecessary uneasiness among a certain class of supporters who are calculating the chances of Runcorn gaining the premier position i t the League.</p>
        <p>There is, however, littl—e—cause for alarm. Tue play of the Cheshire nisn has not degenerated in spite of what carping critics may say. And there is every reason to believe that the reverse will operate as a healthy stimulant on each individual player. Undoubtedly the clubs that Runcorn have to face are, on the whole, much superior to those encountered last senson,and the"myrtles" will have many a hard fight ere the leather be once more placed under lout and, key for its summer holidays. But the play' on Satur- day against one of the finest teams in the Union demonstrates that the staying power, the combina- tion, the defence and the attack are as fine as ever they were. It is easy to account for the defeat. Runcorn were decidedly unfortunate in journeying with a depleted team, Faulkner, the half-back tac- tician, remaining at borne. The long journey, too, told, necessarily injuriously, on the players, and this, combined with the fact that during the first half a strong wind and a glaring sun bad to be faced made matters look as though all things worked together to militate against the best inter- ests of the Cheshire Champions. Despite these odds, however, the team combined together splendidly.</p>
        <p>This week, doubtleee, Runcorn will score a triumph, the match being at home, and it is to be hoped that for the rest of the season the Linnets will go on from conquering to conquer.</p>
        <p>13 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this authority wits held at Dutton Workhouse when there Were presente•—Mr J Knowles, J.P., (chairman), S Woodward, (vice-chair- man), J B Chidwick, T Wright, J J St kes, .11* Mclntyre, J Spruce, TABairter, E Clarke, A Talbot, A G Guest, T Antwias, P Gleave, F Darlington, T Lewis, H Mawdsley, G Pilling. E Brethertou, It Bill- ington, G F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (i.esistant clerk), J Swinton (surveyor), and Dr Adams (Modica! Officer of Health).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY A UTHORITY._</head>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY A UTHORITY._</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr John Lithiby, assistant secretary to the Local Government Board, drawing the Council's attention to the Provisional Order constitut- ing a Part Sanitary Authority for the port of Manches— ter. Under Article IV. the Rural District Council of Runcorn will be represented by a member of the Joint Board to be elected by a Joint Committee to be appoin- ted. The selection of members of the Joint Committee to be appointed for the purpose must be made at a meeting of each Riparian Authority concerned. The first election of a member of the Joint Board Iby a Joint Committee should take place at a meeting of the Joint Committee to bo held at such place as the Clerk to the authurity may fix within six weeks from the commeneAmeut of the order. The Council must make the necessary arrangements to carry out the dates in connection with the matter which devolves upon them so that the Joint Committee on which the Couneil are represented may discharge its duty in the prescribed period. The Clerk explained that five persons bad to be nominated by the Council, four by the Runcorn Urban District Council, two by Lymm Urban Council and one by the Bocklow District Council. Toese 12 persons would then appoint one member to sit on the Port Sanitary Authority to represent the four districts. The matter was adjourned to a future date. THE NEW ROAD FR)3I RUNCORN STATION TO WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr R Potts,clerk to the County Council under date sth September :—To Mr Ashton.— Referring to my letter of the Bth November last the sub-committee will meet at Runcorn Railway Station on Wednesday the 18th instont in the afternoon for the purpose of inspecting the proposed new main road from the station to Weston Point. I furnish you with the information in case your Council should deem it expedi- ent to appoint representatives to meet them. Resolved on the proposition of Mr Moseley-Williams :molded by Mr Mclntyre that a deputation consisting of Messrs S;okes, Chadwick, Wright and Guest meet the County Council.</p>
        <p>THE TUBERCULOSIS 4IIESTION AGAIN.</p>
        <p>A letter was read ttom Mr 8 B Provis, secretary to the Local Government Board unier date the 61ti Septemb with regard to tuberculosis in cattle. The Board desired to impress upon local authorities that pending the in- vestigations and report of the Royal Commission there should be no relaxation on their part in taking proper precautions for dealing with milk from tuberculosis cows intended for the food of man. The matter was referred to the Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
