      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>THELWALL_NUIS &amp;NOE.</head>
        <p>THE THELWALL_NUIS &amp;NOE.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr J A Hutchin. under date 7th September, to the eff ?cc that when travelling along tbe main road from Warrington to Boutsford be had noticed a disagreeable odour when passing under the Canal Btilge at Taelwall• During the present week he 110:IC ,d the stench emanated from the smlll stream run- ning down the lane. . _</p>
        <p>_ Mr R N.+Ylor, Cusrden EN!, Thelwall, Warrington, wrote as follows :—" I beg to advise you that the vile smell from this tannery still continues. This evening I understand it has besn especially bad, and at 11 45 there was turned into the brook some sickly smelling matter which made several people ill and unable to eat. I have hem along the Tannery Brook this alternoon,and it certainly smells very offensive its entire length. We have constantly to leave home for a little fresh air, and when we returned at 12 40 to-day we found our servants full of complaints, and the house completely filled with a vaporous gas from the t inlery discOarge. Whetcer it is arsenious hydrogen or some other clangeroos element which polluted the atmosphere, the scientists most decide. Yoar expert, Dr Penny, in his report on page 4 states "this irritating vapour and smell are caused by a preparation in which brewers'batm or yeast is one of the ingredients '' The next par reads as follows: --"Yeast or brewery fieut i ono of the most objec- tionable known ; and this mixed with the waste liquors from ssaks. tanpits, &amp;I, in my Orricliol, mast produe. an effluvium nui-ance. having all the ells-awe:li of the smell complained of when let off into any tank or drain." This conclusion he seems to have arrived at after microscopical examination, and I cannot find that the reports of Dr Adam st and Mr Scud ler in any way disprove any of Dr Pentri's contentions, so we must conclude their accuracy cannot be disputed. Relying upon the sense and fairness that most actuate your Council in dealing with this long discirsed (pectin," etc.</p>
        <p>Mr John Lithiby, assistant secretary to the Local Government B.ard wrote as follows :—I am directed by the Local Government Board to advert to previous cor- respondence with reference to the nuisance arising from Messrs Long's tannery at Thelwall in the Runcorn Rural Dls:rict and I am requested that when the District , Council have considered the report of their committee the Board may be informed what action has been decided on in the matter. The Board would be glad to I have a map or plan of the locality showing the proposed arrangements. At a meeting of the Committee on the 29th August appointed to conai lee the q section of the sewerage of that part of Thelwall which has its ootfall in Wastc- lane, Mr A H Talbot in the chair the Committee Inspected the nul. all and found that the sewage from some of the houses draining into it was treated ths ough a , settling tank before it entered the sewer but the sewage from the other houses appeared to drain into the sewer without any treatment whatever. The Committee I therefore recommended to the Coancil that the Surveyor be instructed to report as to which houses are con- nected with the sewer, and as to all other houses which In any way have their °offall in the brook. That the Inspec•or of Nuisances be instructed to take a sample of the effluent flowing from the settling tank referred to and to take a sample of the EAI oent as it leaves the setting tank upon Mr Naylor' s property and submit them for analysis. All persons turning crude sewage into the stream at once to take it out or treat it So as to make the Effluent fit to turn into any stream. If upon receiving the analysis of the two f fta en t s referred to the Council should find it unfit,that steps be taken to compel' the parties to take it out or make it fit. That the owners of the pit adjoining the highway near " Oak House" be requested to fill up or keep thoroughly cleansed that portion adjoining the point where it flows into the highway drain. The Manchester Ship Canal Company be re- quested to clean out the culvert under the Bridgwater Canal. The committee inspected a wharf of the Bridge- water Canal near Messrs Long's tannery and are of opinion, it being for the unloading of manure. that it is a possible cause of much of the nuisance. Messrs Long's tannery was inspected and they were unable to detect any nuisance. _ . . . _</p>
        <p>Mr Talbot in moving the adoption of the report said that he went with a biased mind to Mr Long's tannery. After inspecting the place thoroughly he did no think that the nuisance arose from the tanyard at all. There was nothing there but the ordinary tanyard smell. Be could not,of,ccurse say that there was no process going on not injurious to health because they simply did not,know. Mr Long had given his word that the process, had been going on for 15 years, and it was not at all an uncommon one. He thought the Council had been misled by hear- ing so many letters. They had all gone off to the same poiet.—Mr Long's tanyard—and overlooked the fact that there might be other nuisances. They now pro- posed to purify the stream which would do away with anything injurious to health. Mr Williams said that he bad received a letter trom Mr Woodwooi to the effect that he had quite changed his mind and was convincd that the tannery was responsi- ble for none ot the trouble. The recommendations of the committee were approved.</p>
        <p>A RE-A PPOIN TMENT. On the proposition of Mr Williams, seconded by Mr Mclntyre, it vras resolved that Mr Farrington he re-apnointed sanitary inspector for the next twelve months at a saltry of £l9O per annum, and inspector of canal boats at £lO per annum.</p>
        <p>CRAW FORD'S CREA3I</p>
        <p>CRACKERS:</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
        <p>THE HALF-YEARLY BALANCE AND ESTIMATE.</p>
        <p>I The Clerk submitted a report as to toe probable balance in favour of or against each parish at the end of the current half year ending Miclafe'mas, 1901, and estimate (calculated on the present assessable value) of the amount of contributions rEquired for each parish during the half year ending Lady Day, 1902. The total capital for eurrent half year was £579 ; probable expen- diture. £4,149; balance at the end of the current half year, £1,641. The probable expenditu e during half year ending Lady Day, 1902. was as follows :—General expenses (public health) £640; highways expensea, £2,330 • balance and contingency. £1,612; total, £4,562, After deducting a probable balance at Michwmas, 1901, of £1,641 there remained a total amount rcquired from the overseers of £2,941. The assessable value of the diatrict was £154,626. 'The amount of contribution frouu the various rarishes(after addition or deduction of balance) for half-year ending Lady Day, 1902 was as follows :—Acton Grange,£73; Alvanley,£3o; Antrobus. £5O ; Appleton. £9B ; Aston, £9O ; Aston Grange. £9; Bartington, £lO ; Budworth, £39 ; Clifton, £7O ; Crowley, £25 ; Daresbury, £2l ; Dutton, £144 ; Frodsham. £219 ; Frodsham Lordship. £145 ; GraPPen- hall, £125 ; Halton, £92 ; Hatton. £27 ; Helsby, £162 ; Kechwick, £59 ; Kingsley, £96 ; Kingswood, £3O ; Latchford (Without), £44 ; Manley, £3O ; Moore, £33 ; Newton-by-Daresbury, £23 ; Newton-byFrodsham, £l5 ; Norley, £6O : Norton, £l2O ; Preston on the Hill, £11; Sevenoaks, £lB ; Stockham, £l3; Stockton Heath, £170; S;retton, £36 ; Sutton, £124 ; Thelwall, £9O ; Walton Inferior, £BB ; Walton Superior' £4O: Weston, £237; Whitley Interior, £3O; Whitley Superior, £35. A rate of 4:kl in the £on the assessable value would produce £2,898 and it was recommended that a call far £2,941 (osing equal to the call for the corresponding period of Last year) be made upon the overseers. The recommandation was confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS DEATH OF A WOMAN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS DEATH OF A WOMAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon an inquest was held before Mr It DeLeon, coroner,touching the death of Eliza Fearnett, a married woman, who was found in the Bridgewater Canal on Sunday morning.—Phe police were represented by Inspector Egerton, and Mr Dutton was the foreman of thr jury.—Matthew Woodwool, 52, High.street,fried 1 fish and chip potato dealer, deposed that the body was that of his sister, Elizi Fearnett. She kept house for ' him and was 58 !years of age. Witness list saw her alive) at three o'clock on Saturday afternoon, when she was going to buy envelopes and paper from the market. She was at that time sober, but she took "a little drop now and:then." A mark above the right eve was caused by her falling off the chair on to the floor when in a state of intoxication. Toere was no reason for her to take her life away and she had not committed suicide. He did not make any inquiries about her diaappearance and he stayed up until 12 30 on Stturday night expecting her to come in. She had never stayed out late before. Witness had inst got tic at 11 o'clock on Sunday when the police called. —Elizabeth Englesfleld, 5. fleath-road, a widow, stated that the deceased cams to her house OA Saturday at 430 and stayed about half an hour. She said she was going to look for a shop in Canal-ereet. She went down the bridge steps that led to the towing path, pre- sumably to get to Canal-greet: She was quite sober.— John Creswell, a boatman on the • • Kate and Henry " said that on Sunday morning he was at the Top Locks and saw the body in toe canal. He reported the matter to the police as he could not obtain assistance. The corpse was afterwards conveyed to the mortuary. —P.C. Wilson gave corroborative evidence.—A verdict of "Found drowned" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held on Thursday evening. Pre.ent E 'raison, J.P.. C.C., (in the chair), F J Norman, J.P.. J Littler, J Pritchard, J.P., W J Canning,ham, J W Lightburn (cerk), and A Lightburn (assistant clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>VICTORIA ROAD BOARD ECHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE VICTORIA ROAD BOARD ECHOOL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Scholl Management Committee accounts amounting to £6BB 45 id were paa.ed for pay- ment. Mr Moulton. headmaster of the Victoria-road Boys' School appeared before the committee and made an explanation re the admission of scholars at the schnoi.</p>
        <p>Passel on the proposition of the Chairman, seconded by Mr Littler.</p>
        <p>GRANTS EARNED BY THE SCHOOLS.. It was repprted that the following grants had been recekPd :—Annual grant': Victoria-road evening semi, £9O 16s 61 ; £9B 73: Victoria-road, £915 ; tiranville-stropt, £lO6 ; total, £1,210 3q61. Fee grant : Mill Brow. £l3 103 ; Vintoria-road, £lO3 153; Granville- street, £l7 2s 61; total, £134 7s 61.</p>
        <p>THE EVENING SCHOOLS PROBLEM AND THE COENTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the Cheshire County Council had resolved that as the Runcorn Sthool Board had during the twelve months immediately preceding the 31st July, 1901, maintained from the School Futld, classes for the maintenance of which the School Ruhd was not lawfully applicable, it was agreed that the Board carry on the classes for one year from the ditto mentioned, the cost to come out of the fund in order to I maintain the same scals of operations as in t.,,e previous year. Mr Norman understood that they would be allowed to work exactly as last year. The Clerk explained that the communication from the Council meant the indemnification of the itoard against Ithe surcharges in consequence of the Cockerton judge— ' merit.</p>
        <p>Tic Chairman said they really wanted to work under the directory instead of the E location Act. The Act subjected them to a 17s 6 limit but under the directory the Beard received all the grants the school earced. Mr Psi:chard :_The onlv advantage is a financial one.</p>
        <p>Mr Norman: The point to be made clear i 9 that we are going to be quite the same as last year because there are cases wuere this has not been so.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: We want the best bargain we can get on account of the rates.</p>
        <p>A sith-cornmittee consisting of Messrs Irnison, Pritchard and Cunoinebarn was appointed to make arrangements for the evening echools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BAZAAR AT THE MERSEY MISSION RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>BAZAAR AT THE MERSEY MISSION RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A very interesting sale of work and garden party was held at the S?ainen's Instirute and pleasure grounds on Wednesday afternoon. The stalls had been very prettily arranged and the lower school room looked at its best. The opening cereumay was performed by Mrs Crossland Taylor, hon. sec., for the lielsby and Dis- trictalission and she was introduced by Missioner Shaw. The following ladies officiated at the various stalls:— The toy stall, Misses Lily Shaw, Tanks, L Tonks, A Ward, Baines, Gibson. Mission s".all, Mesdames Shaw, sen, Tonk?, Cliffe, W Shaw. Sunday school stall, Misses Mack, Evan?, J :Vinson, Wardle and Norton; sweet stall, Misses E Jones, E loasar and A Shaw ; refreshment stall, Mesdames Atkin, Rose, Gibson, J Aitkin and Miss May Aitkin ; flower stall, Misses Cooper, F Shaw, Watson, and D Cooper ; tea room pre- sided over by Mesdames Shaw, jun., Oathwaite, Crabb, and the Misses Coward,Bowyer, Cliffe, and Mrs R Shaw. The cloak room was in charge of Mrs Lockett, Daring the afternoon and evening numerous games were en- gaged in incloding " Punch and Judy,"" Aunt Sally " " cocoanutshies " and a very interesting exhibition game of bowls was placed on the new green by Councillor Dsan and Levi Fowler (Champion of Weston Point). The 'Volunteer Band played at intervals which added considerably to the interest of the proceed- ngs. Needless to say,. Mr Shaw, missioner, ably assisted by Messrs Bylield and W Shaw, jun, put their usual ammint of energy into the affair and con- tributed to make the proceedings an entire success. A I good stle was done the proceeds of which will be de- voted to buying new hymn books for the MisFiOn.</p>
        <p>WANTED COOK. HOUSEMAIDS. WAITRESSES, for Manchester ; excellent wages; call or write to Miss I{.4.nTasow, 6. Oldham-street, Manchester. Retabliehed 1862.</p>
        <p>NvAN THD. 0.101811k1.3 aad GUMS, best places ; excel lent wages.—Call or vrrtre to Mi• s HAANISON, 6 011 ham-street, Manchester, Established 1662.</p>
        <p>AGOOD Way to get a nice,,Town or Country SERVANT, write or call to Miss HARRISON, 6, Oldham-street, Manchester. Established 1862.</p>
        <p>Electric Lighting Extensions. On Friday nexr,, Mr M K North, £3l 1.C.E., will hold an inquiry on behalf of the Local Government Board, at toe Town Hall, into the application of the Town Council to borrow £15,000 for electric light- ing purposes. The Inspector will be przTared to receive the evidence of any person interested in the matter. The Result cf a Fall.</p>
        <p>_ Edward Muchlor, 22 sears ot age, living at 27,5 t, Barnabas'-place,was admttted into the Infirmary on Sunday, suffering from a broken leg. The injury was sustained through a fall at his home. Hs was detained as an in-patient. Buckley-street P S A. The half-yearly distribution of prizes took place at Buckley-street Free Methodist Church on Sunday afternoon in connection with the P S A Society. There was a large attendance of members and friends at the open meeting. The chair was occu- pied by Mr Thomas Trow, and the prizes were dis- tributed by Mrs Clay. Solos were given by Miss Bridge and Mr A Catterill, the accompanist being Mr A Prow. There was a substantial increase in the membership, as evidenced by the number of prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON BURGLARIES. Prisoner Committed for Trial.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BURGLARIES. Prisoner Committed for Trial.</p>
        <p>Admission of the Crimes.</p>
        <p>The further magisterial proceedings in connec- tion with the Warrington burglary cases, took place on Saturday when Mosul or John Logan,labourer, 22, Leonard-street was brought before Mr Chandley and other magistrates, charged with breaking and entering the Imperial Hotel, Bewsey-road, on Mon- day night, and stealing 10s in cash, and a bottle of brandy, the property of Tnomas Partridge. He was further coarged with stealing from Mr Jack- son's pawnshop, in Orford-lane, a pair of trousers. A third charge against the prisoner was that of stealing on the 25th Marcia from the Longford Gas Works a man's overcoat a dog licence, and a re- port sheet, the property of William Henry Smith. The Chief Constable stated the facts of the case and said that since the prisoner was before the Court on Thursday, further iniuiries had been made. but the most thorough investigation satis- fied him (Mr Talbot) that the prisoner was unac- companied when he committed the offences, and in arresting him the police had for a time put a stop 'to those nefarious proceedings which had been terrorizing Warrington and district for some time past. He might have brought other charges against the prisoner, for he had strong evidence in support of nine cases of robbery, but three was the limit for committal purposes. Proceeding to state the facts, Mr Talbot stated that with reference to the Imperial Hotel charge, the prisoner obtained admission into the building during the night, and when the landlord came downstairs at five minutes to six on the Tuesday morning he found customers waiting to be served. He was astonished and asked how they had got in, and the reply was that they had found the door open- An examination of the premises was made,and it was found that the money and brandy had been stolen—altogether the lose was 15s. The depredator had evidently cut his hand while breaking into the till, as there were blood stains on it, and it was subsequently found that the .prisoner's hand bad been cut. A thick plateglass window had been broken with the auger produced, and the prisoner had thus obtained admission into the building. Information was given to the police, and the prisoner was arrested. and he afterwards admitted the offence.lt appeared that :a young man named James Herbert Hahne was returning from the Volunteer ball on the Tues- day morning in question, at about three o'clock, wiien he saw the prisoner loitering about in a sus- picious manner near the hotel. He subsequently saw him at the Police Station with other prisoners and at once identified him. On the morning of the robbery the prisoner was also seen at a public-house in Padgate. He had a bottle of brandy with him, some of which he poured into his beer—an extraordinary proceeding. With reference to the Orford-lane burglary Mr Burn, the manager, for Messrs Jackson, lived on the premises, which were secure when he retired to bed about half-past ten on Monday night. At half-past four the next morning he was aroused by a smash- ing of glass. On getting up and going down he could not see anyone about. He found, however, that the shop window in Forshaw-street had been. broken and that a pair of trousers had been abstrac- ted from the window Mr Burn afterwards identi- fied the trousers at the Police Station. Prisoner had admitted having stolen the trousers. Ile did not use the auger in the legitim- ate fashion, but used the other end. Refer- ring to the third charge the Chief Constable said that Mr William Smith,foreman in the employ of the Warrinotcn Corporation. who lived at 153, Winwick-road, was in the office at five minutes to twelve on Tuesday night, March 20th. He placed his coat in the meter house:at the works and sub- sequently missed it. In the pockets of the coat were a dog licence, and three keys and a report sheet. He gave information to the police, and when prisoner's house was searched after his arrest the missing articles were found. When charged he admitted the theft. _ _</p>
        <p>Evidence of apprehension was given by Detec- tives Barlow and Foster who stated that prisoner admitted all the offences. -</p>
        <p>• Prisoner who had nothing to say was committed for trial at the Liverpool Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>CROFI HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>CROFI HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Show and Sports.</p>
        <p>The second annual flower show and sports under the auspices of the Croft Horticultural Society on Saturday were a complete success. Favoured by fine weather, the village presented a festive appear- ance, till the sports drew large crowds from War- rington, Ashton, Newton and other neighbouring places. Turning to the show and flowers and garden produce, the exhibits in Con- nection with which were shown in the school- room it may be remaaked that the entries were more numerous than ever, and the standard of ex- cellence was fully maintained. A difficult task awaited the judges, who, however, performed their wcrk to the evident satisfaction of the exhibitors. The garden produce CiBES was extremely good, and the vegetable marrows, kidney beans, &amp;c, were a revelation. Some very fine tiger lilies were shown. and visitors to the show were well repaid for the trouble of the journey by their inspection. The sports, however, occupied the most attention. The results were as follows :</p>
        <p>100 Yards Flat Race (Boys).-1, P Marsh, War- rington ;2. A Wilcock, Warrington (scratch) ; 3, .1 Shaw, Croft 18. A goad race and very close finish. Half-mile Bicycle Race.-1, W Shaw. Ashton, 10; 2, D Sherratt, Bamfurlong, 22; 3, J Barr, &amp;ides- town 34. Won by yards, half wheel between second and third, 220 Yards Flat REC.) —I,J Wightman,Earlestown 15; 2, A Evans. Winwick, 16; 3, J J Matthews, Runcorn, G. Yards separated all three. Mile Bicycle Race.-1, W Shaw, Ashton, 20; 2,T Sberratt, Bamfurlong, 40; 3, I Garviss, Abram, .10. Some excellent riding was seen'in this rdce and a close finish was the result.</p>
        <p>440 Yards Flat flace.-1, W Hampsnn. Leigh, 15 2„) Wizhtmau, Earlestown, 25; 3, J Jump, Win- wick, 30.</p>
        <p>100 Flat race (men).—!ienai-final : Ist beat : 1. W Dean, Newton, 9; 2, W Birchall, Leigh, 10. Second heat. : 1, W Sutton, Ashtnn, 6; 2, J Taylo r Croft, 9. Final: 1, W Dean; 2, W Birchall ; 3, W Sutton.</p>
        <p>_ _ 440 Yards Obstacle Race.-1, J Boite, Tarporley 2, T Powney, Warrington; 3, JMATatc, Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>An Old Woman's Sad Death —Fall through a Bedroom Window.</p>
        <p>At the Sloop Hotel,Sankey Bridges, Mr Brig- house conducted an inquiry on Monday evening respecting the circumstances attending the death of Sarah Stretch.—The first witness was John Stretch, joiner, 29, Liverpool-road. who said the deceased was his mother, and as 75 years of age. She lived with him at the above address. She had been strange in her ways for a considerable time, and he had to put a catch on her bedroom door to keep her from falling downstairs as she was in the habit of getting out of bed during the night. On .Thursday, the 15th ult., at a quarter paitfive in the morning, he went into the deceased's room to see how. 800 was as she had coniplained of not feeling well the previous day. He found the window open and his mother was miss- ing. The washstand that bad stood near the window had been removed. He raised an alarm, and a neighbour named Ann Bate, found tbe deceased in the Sloop yard about 200 yards from her home. She was picked up and carried to bed, and Dr Murray, of Penketb, attended her up to the time of her death which took place at a quarter to twelve on the previous night. She had not been I able to explain bow or why she got through the window. She told Dr Murray that she bad injured herself by a fall in an entry.—Ann Bate, the neigh- bour referred to, said she went to look for the deceased at the son's request and found her in the Sloop yard. She was partly undressed and was bleeding from a wound on the side of the head and bruises on her arms and feet. She asked deceased what she was doing, and she replied that "she was going home." Witness believed the deceased was out of her mind, She had tried to get through the window several times since the accident:—The jury returned a verdict of death from severe injuries caused by a fall from a bedroom window, and that deceased committed suicide while of unsound mind.</p>
