      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>"THE DESPAIR OF THE BENCH "-JULIA ANN SHIELDS 53RD APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>A well-known character to the police, in the person of Julia Ann Shields, appeared at the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday, before Messrs Birchall (presiding) and Geddes, on the charge of sleeping in an outbuilding in Vernon's Yard. Dail-street. that morning, without visible means.—P.C. Monks proved the case-- The Chief Comtable said this was prisoneos 53rd appearance.—The Chairman (to prisoner) : Wnen did you come out of gaol last ? Julia replied that she came out last Thursday.—The Chairman : On Thursday ! It ia one incessant committal. You are no sooner out than you are in again.—The Chief Constable said that the Bench knew what efforts were made on behalf of prisoner, which bad unfortunately not succeeded. This was a young woman whom he was genuinely sorry for. When sober she was unnaturally (Fie, and whea she had drink she was mad. She had neen sent to homes, but refused to stay in thorn.—The Chairman said th,,re seemed to be no chance of doing good in this case. Prisoner was the despair of the Bench. They would have to send her back to prison for seven days. His heart ached to send her to gaol from time to time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—BefOre Mr. Btrehall (in the ehair). Conneillor Smethurs.. and Messrs J. W. Hayward, S Wright, and H. "</p>
        <p>Tialrnton. NITER THEFoOTSALL MATCH.—Michael Logan, descried as a labourer, was charged with being drunk and disorderly In Horsemarket-street oa 'Saturday aiterno3l.—P.o Corbett proved the came and stated hat the prisoner was not only drunk and disorderly but made use of defiant I inguage. He expressed his intention not to be locked up by 43 slops."— The Chief Constable It was after the f3otball match ? Corbett Yes sir -Pined 5s and costs. A. SUNDAY NIGHT DliTußßANcE.—William Campbell and John Wareurton. were charged with hAying been drunk and disorderly in Church-street on the previous nlght.—P.C. Clough proved the case, aad stated that the disturbance created by the prisoners was .• more like a riot" than any- thing else and a large crowd had assemlaerl.—dergeant Wiliiamson gave corroborative evidence.—The Chairman said the Bench was determined to put a stop to such conduct, and the prisoners would be fined 10i and costs each. "Gar HOLD or HIM "—WilliArn Darcy was charged with heing drunk in Naylor-street on the previous night. P.C. Clough proved the Case. The pi lamer said he was a native of Warringtotabdt he bat been out of town some time. Ile met some frien is and they treated him, bat what he hMld was •• too strong for him and got hold of him." The Chief Constable He means the policeman got hold of him. (Laughter.) Fined 5s and costs.—For being drunk and diserderly in Sankey-street. on Saturday, John PAU, a labourer, was fined 10s and elate. YOUTHFOL GAMBLERS FINED. —John Thompson (14), 66, Pierpoint-street ; Richard Corfleld (13), 88. Catberine-stre.t ; James Tarpey (14), 1:6, Pierpointestreet ; and George Earley (15). Pierpoint-street, Were charged with playing pitch and toss in Catherine-street on the Ist September.—P.C. Clough having given tvidence, the Chief Constable stated that notW tbstanding the fact that offenders were tin-d every weeps. and convictiens were duly recorded in the pre,s, there was no diminution of the nuis- ance. Otfand-rs were Ida le to three months' imprisonment, but when side by side with that they were only fined 53 it did not impress them.—The magistrates inflicted a tine of 103 and costs in each case.</p>
        <p>SLIGdTLZ INEBRILTED."—John Hughes, a joiner, living at 151, Knotsford-roil. was summoned for nsving been drunk and disorderly in Knntsford-road on the Znd September —P.C. Ssnkey proved the ease. Defendant, who sail be was " slightly inebriated." but not disorderly was fined 5s and costs.</p>
        <p>trUESDAY.—Befare Me3SrS T 8irc133.11 (In the chair), Hyriaer Young and Gedde3. "</p>
        <p>- - VILLAINOUS LANGUAGE."— A. SINISTER THREAT. —Jarllss Leigh. a forgeman residing at 16, Bowes-street, Was charged on a warren, with in eking i.e of threats towards George Wa!ton cn the 7th inst--Mr Steel appeared on behalf of the complainant and stated that his client was the proprietor of some swing buts and shooting galleries On the fair ground at the beck o: the Woe Coat School. lie had had cause to complain of forgemen coming early In the morning and interfering with the swinge, defendant befog one ot the men. He went to defend- aut's house and complained to his parents about the matter. On Saturday evening complainant was on the fairground h ,re the defendant came to him, and taking out a pocket knife and opening It, t Id complainant he was a " marked man." He threatened to " put his entrails under his feet." and hit complainant in the stomach with the unsharpened end of the knife. Defendant had since expressed his sorrow and was willing to he bound over to pay the costs.— The Chairman said the language used by the defendant was villeicous.—Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of £lO. and ordered to pay the costs —Au advocate's fee was also allowed. 37TIT APPEARANCE.—John Whittaker WAS charged with being drunk and disorderly In Eorsemerket-street at 7,4) on Monday evening—P.S. Evans proved the case—The Chief Cona able said the prisoner made his 37th apprarancedhe sth this year. Until recently he worked as an out-porter at the station, but lately he had been drinking cuntinually—lfine of 178 and costs with the alternative of 14 days' Imprison- ment was in2icted —Whittaker went to gaol.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY—Before Messrs Blreball (in the chair) arii J Geddes.</p>
        <p>IMPRISONMENT EMI A VIoLENT CHLRACTER.—TsIac Smith, Glasshouse-row, was charged with assaulting Thomas F.ar- ringtoa on Monday.—Cornplaißant, who appeared id Cann with two badly discoloured eyes, said that he was returning home at halt-past eleven on Monday evening, when prisoner met him and " started to butt him in the face." He after- wards Kck ed him all over the body. Prisoner's daughter hit Witness in the eye with her clog. For the defence Mrs Smith said that Farrington asked her husband to fight. Farrington was drunk, tier husband never kicked Farring- ton. About twelve men and women were hitting her husband. Smith said that Farrington asked him to fight, and he did not want " much persuasion."—The Chief Constable said that this was Smith's 20th aPP-,rewe. He had been repeatedly convicted of assault. The Bench committed the prisoner to goal for two months. " SPoSeiNG " ON THE MILITIA. --Thomas Brainey, 18, Percival's-fold, was charged with having been drunk and dirorderly in Horsernarket street, at half-dast eleven on Tuesday morning —P C. Cooper stated that he noticed prisoner for about an hour, and he was behaving in a very disorderly manner, pushing and j,)stling p,ople about , Witnebs look him into custody la-it hal to get the assistance of PC. Hewitt to take him .o the Police Stition.—P.C. Hewitt corrroborated. Questioned by the Chief Constable, the Constable said he had to leave his traffic point at Market Gate to assist Cover to get prisoner to he station. He would not leave it under ordinary conditions—The Chief Constable remarked that he controllet the traffic while the toot:astable was away. He also stated that a great many Ce- urr,ed Militiamen had received their deferred paylon Tu:sday and all the" comee tot's" and slinks of the town had been •• sponging" on them. Fined 58 and costs or 'eve? flays.</p>
        <p>treor7ANTs.—Joh-o.Tay-lO;,--ii-m-e. Grundy, and Andrew Earker, ot St Helens, were charged with sleeping on the premires of Messrs Monks, Had and Company, Liverpool- road, without any visible means of subsistence —Thomas Mold, nigh watchman, said he found the prisoners sleeping at the forge at eleven o'clock. They said they had walked from St Helens. The workmen hart complained of suppers and coats being stolen from the works.—The prisoners were unt to gaol for seven days.</p>
        <p>The best way to destroy wasps, says Mr lA° Gibson, of Hexhara, is to make a solution of cyanide of potas- si.um, say, to 1 tz, in pint of water, and to soak a niece of cotton wool or rag in this and insert it inside the hole of a wasp's nes: at night, when they are all in. It is most certain in its action.</p>
