      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PENRHYN CHOIR AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PENRHYN CHOIR AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Successful Concert in Parr Hall. What can aptly: be described as a rich musical treat was afforded those who attended the concert given in the Parr Hall, Warrington, on Monday evening by the renowned Penrnyn Choir. This Choir is acknowledged to be the best male Yore Choir in the world, having defeated the world's choirs at Chicago, and it was therefore not surprising to find that an undsually large audience assembled to hear it on Monday evening. The spacious ball was almost ! filled in every part, and all who attended were ! unanimously agreed chat the concert was one of the most successful, from a musical as well as a financial standpoint, which has been held in War- rington fur some time. The object for which the concert was promoted was one which forcibly ap- pealed to many. As is doubtless well-known, the choir is composed of workingmen, who were until the present lamentable strike commenced, em- ployed on Lord Penrhyn's quarries at Bethesda. They are now travelling the country, giving co.n- certa almost every evening, in order to raise funds for the relief of the aged men, and the women and children, who are suffering so severely at Bethesda. The financial result of Monday evening's concert was eminently satisfactory. In addition to the tickets which had ben sold, £l7 was taken in cash at the deers', and the collection which was made during the evening realized a further sum of .Cll 13s.This amount estab- lishes a record, it being the largest sum taken at any one collection, by the choir. Tree total proceeds are expected to amount up- wards of £4O. The chair was occupied by the Rev E R Taxbuck, who was supported on the platform by the Revs John Yonge and J F Matthews, Councillors Breugh and James Evans, Mr T Birchall, J.P., and Mr E W Lloyd (financial secretary of the choir). The programme opened with the chords "Roman Soldiers" in which the ability of the choir had ample scope, and was a foretaste of the evening's enjoyment which was to follow. The Cliamatieg then read a letter from the Rev F E Powell, vicar of Sc. James', Latchford, who wrote say- ing; that he regretted exceedingly that a pressing engagement debarred him from enjoying the musical treat which he had anticipated. He wished the concert all success. Coetinuine, Mr Meet:lce said that his posi- tion that night would be best explained by the fact that he was a clergyman, and consequently not liable to be biased by one question of employer or employee, and therefore ais sympathy—he hoped it was pretty well known in Warrington by this tint—was with any person or any body of persons in distress from whatever cause, and he yie7ded to none in that feeling. He must first of all congratu- late the ehoir most heartily on their efficiency in chorus work. Their ability in the musical direction, was not to his mind, BO important as the spirit which prompted them to go about the couatry,working as they had done for some time now for the unfortunate victims of the long Penrhyn quarries dispute. They were not paid workers in this great cause, but were all volunteers and received no wages. (Applause) They were all members of the Penrnyn Trades Union— (applause)— and were in receipt of strike pay,ancl they had thought well to ensp'oy the musical talent which they possessed in the raising of funds for tho le who were always the greatest sufferers as a consequence of a trade dispute—the aged men, the women, and children. It was to be distinctly understoo I that the profits would not go to any strike fund but would all be aevoted to the relief fund in support of the aged men, women and children. He should like to say a word as to the position of the Warring- ton Trades Conical, who had conic forwar 1 and seconded this band of workingmen in their noble aim. They had done everything possible to make the concert a success, and he congratulated them upon the results of their efforts. It was well-known that they clergy and ministers did not desire in any way to take sides in trade disputes, but they could net keep their minds from considering whatever questions of the day were avitating the public mind and so they had all of them hi supposed foe many years, been intently interested in the question of trades disputes and had been wondering whether they could possibly see any way of preventing disputes in the future. He had welcomed with all his heart the formatien in Warrington of a Conci'iation Board in connection with the local Trades' Council. They might nave an interest on one side or the other or on neither, but at the same time he thought they were all agreed in unanimously hoping for some means of lessening the number of strikes. He had thought for many years that there ought to be some scheme of compulsory arbitration. He noticed that during the past week the quostion had been brought forward at the meeting of the Trades' Congress. Let him sketch what he thouyht might possibly ba a way of introducing compulsory arbitra- tion. He would suggest that a body of judges should be appointed as arbitrators in all 'eeades' divutes. Without excepion be thought all Englishmen wou:d agree that our judges were the least , open to corruption of all judges ,n to,, world, th at they ! commanded the confidence of the whole country, and were believed in by all who were interested in the an- tins brought °dote them or not. Sapposiagit body of four or six judges were appointed to the post of arbitra- tion j Ages in all trade disputes, the parties would be compelled before proceeding to the extreme measure of , a strike to go before one or two of the judges, ! and have the matter stated pro and yen by their representatives. He did not propose for a moment that the decision of the judge should be binding on either masters or men, but it was welt known' that no strike could be successful that went on contrary to public opinion, and it, after a full and fair trial the jiideee made their decisioa, it would be found that it would be well for masters or men as the case might be to accept the decision and abandon the idea of proceeding to the extreme measures of a strike. An stnplocer of labour had told him that his objection to compulsory arbitration was that he had no intention of producing his accounts in open coutt or of showing to the public the methods by which he carried on business. As a matter of fact, at the present 1 time, all who had an income above a certain amuunt had to oro iuce their books of accounts before a recoznised Government official. In the case of a dispute howeeer,the books wou'd not be looked at by the people in court but by a representative of the judge or the judge himself. On the other hand it was said that the men would never accept an arthzation decision given ageingt them, but he believed from experience, that when the men had confidence in an arbitrator they would accept his decision even if it were against them. He hoped some scheme would be formed and he saw signs that schemes were in the air, for lessening the number of strikes in the country and so leaving us free to compete with something like fairness with the nations of the world, Ho wished every success to the choir and also to the Warrington Trades' Council who had so heartily co-operated with them in making the concert such a distinct success. (ADpI4USB), Toe programme was then proceeded with. The choruses by the choir were most effective, their best efforts b-ing " Comrades' song of hope" and " S:ildiers' Chorus." These popular compositions were sung with an expression and verse which delighted the audience, and the cordial applause which succeeded each, wee expressive of appreciation of the choir's effici- ency. " Climbing Snowden" was another ponular chorus. It wee sung in the Welsh language, and though perhaps the audience did not comprehend the meaning of ths words, they thorougnly apprf - ciated the musical merits ef this item. All the selections by the choir were sung from memory. Each member knew his part thoroughly, and the evemble was perfect, The duet " Excelsior " was spiritedly rendered by Messrs H L Williams (tenor) and R Tuomas (bass). The accompaniments were played by Mr R 0 Elwerds who also gave a pianoforte solo " Welsh airs " with taste and akin. In addit,on to the choir,two local artistes Messrs Tom boothresd M.I.U.M. (violin), and Tom Gunson, Manchester Co'lsge of music (alt) gave their services. Mr Boothroyd executed two violin solos in masterly style, and earned the hearty plaudits of the audience. Mr Guns= possesses a remark- ably sweet voice which was heard to advantage in " A dream of paradise." 'Later in the evening he sang Lidding " Abide with met" with good effect. A highly successful concert was brought to a close with the sing. leg of" Land of my fathers "by the choir. During the evening Mr E W LLOYD briefly stated the grievances of the Penrbyn quarrymen, and said they had asked for the matter to he settled by arbitration, but Lord Peurhyn had not seen his way to agree to it. It was to be hoped that the Government would move in the matter. (Hear, bear.) In proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman, Mr T BIRCHALL, J.P., said he sympathized with the quarry- men who had dared to call th -jr bodies and their souls ! their own, and who had come to tho conclusion that the earth was the Lord's and not the landlords, and had thought they should,have some say in the conditions of ! their daily lives. (Applause.) ! The Rev Jona YONG&amp; seconded.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with acclamation and the Rev E R Tanzucx having responded the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
