      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Batley.</p>
        <p>The Cup-Final Reversed.</p>
        <p>The most interesting match of sll those on Saturday was certainly the one down for decision at Warrington, where the cup-finalists of last season met to once more 820 which was the better team. At Leeds, Batley were certainly the best, but on e.aturday they found War- rington a different lot altogether. Both sides had absentees, but on the whole the sides were pretty much the alma as taced one another in tile cup-final. Batley came without Fitzgerald and Oakland, while Warring- ton were short of Fell, Edmondson, and Egen. The day was an excellent one for the opening ot the season and a crowd of some 6,000 assembled to seethe tables turned cu the cup-holders. Mr Robinson of Mactingham was the referee and the teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>Warringcou : Hallam, full back ; Fish, laterwood, Dckenson and Harris,threEqqarters; Burgess and Allen, hale backs ; Boardman, Morrison, W swift, F Swift, Scboltz?, Panberton, Cunningham, and Jolley, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Girner, full back • Davies, Goodall, Auty, and Fozzad, dare. quirters ; I.;lidgley and Lloyd, balf- b icks; Spurr, Scubley, Carrpil,Judge,lioninson, P Good- all, Snaw,aud ft)gere, forward.</p>
        <p>, Isher wood won the toss and elected to play with the wind behind him, Spurr starting the game five minutes late. Burgess returned to midfield where Batley secured from the pack, but duty missed his pass. Again the bill CarS3 out on the Batley side,Nlidgley sending on to Davies who was safely held by Harris. Burgess was .:03 eager and got penalised, the punt falling into the safe hands of Dickenson who returned. followed up and grassed duty in flue style. Warrington now made bead avay,encouraged by the cheers of their excited followers. Smart following up saw Davies again upset, this time well within the Batley quarter. Garner WilS held near toe line, and Dickenson made a fine attempt to get through. Batley forwards cleared with a rush but Isherwood pluckily dropped on the ball, Warrington maintaining a hot attack. At last a penalty against Boardman for offside reliEved the visitors, and fine play by D tales and Hallam for their respective sides was hearily caeered. Davies had a shot at goal from a punalty, ad nearly succeeded in scoring. Auty failed to stop a rush by the home front rank, anl Warriugtoa got the upper hand again. Good play on bo:h sides was witnessed, but toe home backs did not appear to have found their feet properly. Burgess broke away Once and nearly scored, Igierwood also trying his luck with a drop at goal, which nearly came off. Boardman was off hereabouts with a damaged leg, but returned to find Warringt3a still at it. Dickenson got hold near the line and running strongly he broke through at the corner, scoring Warrington'a first try in clever fashion. 'rae cheering was loud and long. Fish failed at goal. Batley worked hard but found Warrington too quick tor them and they had to defend. Burgess upset Davies and the latter retaliated with his fiat, the sequel being that both were ordered off the field by the referee. Davies lost his head and jumped the rails, and before anyone could iaterfere he strurk one of the specta-ors in the face. A serious melee look !d preb- abls, but the Warrington officials and police promptly interfered and the game proceeded. Fish went half-back for Warrington, Pemberton taking his place, while Etagere took Davies' place on the Batley side. Fith was soon active, aud throwing to Isher- wood, the latter punted cleverly to Harris, who fielded well and dashed over at the carver. No goal resulted. Warrington quite overplayed their opponents and Fish crossed the line, only to be called back. Rogers got away with a clear field, but he lacked speed and Isherwood brought him down. Hallam was the Cause of another attack by Warrington, Swift marking in nice position, but Fish missed the goal. Half-tinae, Warrington two tries. Batley nil. Swift restarted arid play settled at half-way. Fish got hold arid passed to Isherwood, who smartly bluffed Auty and returned, Fish sprinting in beautifully from the centre, the Batley custodian having no chance with him whatever. Isherwood took the goal-kick and hit one of the uprights. Batley rallied and got close to the home line, Allen relieving with a powerful run to the 25 line. Warriogton dotendei well for a time, exchange punting slowing the pace of the game considerably. Operations were again in midfield when Fish set the three-quarter line going, Dickenson and Harris crossing the Batley line in company, the former grounding the ball after a grand run. Hallam then had a shot bat missed toe goal. Batley seemed hopelessly beaten and very soon after Fish, Isherwood, and Harris exchanged with the result that Harris dashed over at the corner with the fifth try, Fish as usual miss- ing the goal. Warrington now relaxel their efforts and Batley came again, Fozzard getting through almost un- opposed at the corner. No goal. Warrington attacked once more. Harris raced down the field with the ball at his toes, Garner just kicking dead in the nick of time. Final, Warrington 5 tries (15 points); Batley 1 try (3 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Runcorn v. Hull.</head>
        <p>Runcorn v. Hull.</p>
        <p>113 e aeason opened very auspiciously at Hull in the first Northern League match, and Runcorn provided the spectators with a rare run for their money. The attendance was, as is now usual at the seaport, very fine and large, and the atmospherical conditions of the brightest and best from a spectator's point of view. The players may have found it rather perspiring, but there were a few who seemed to need 3 running down process. With the exception of the Cheshire county I man, Faulkner, who was displaced by H Jones in the IRuncorn team, the sldes were as originally selected. Hull had the advantage of the wind at the opening, but Runcorn were the first to annex territory, and Jolley was being fed so repeatedly by his forwards that there were visions of an early store on the Runcorn side. Butterworth got nicely away on one occasion, but he was brought t 3 grief by Taylor, and then Houghton, when he had a wide stretch of ground to himself, knocked the ball on so badly that all advantage was lost. Prater and Kitson made their toes rattle, and Houghton WAS again called upon. As he was tackled a scrummage followed, and the forwards kept up a close contest near the Runcorn lice. Frank tried to dodge Richardaon, but faded, and there was a suspicion of selfishness about this move on the part of the Welsh- man, though he soon atoned for it, and transferred the ball to Thompson. The latter made a good opening for Hanabrecht, and the old Bromley threequarter sprinted over the lino with the opening try of the season. Parkin- son lauded a good goal. Runcorn narrowly missed scoring when the Rides set-off again, but the interval found Hull five points to the good. The resumption suggested a score for the visitors, for Frank and Taylor juggled badly with the ball, and Inglesfield nearly beat the pair. The Yorkshire county full back recovered his equanimity in time to avert dlsastereann then,following a fruitless penalty to Hull, Richardson dropped a clever goal for the visitor?. Runcorn were now making mat- ters warm for their opponents, and Hull's defence was sorely tried. The tide was turned by Frank again, and the diminutive one nearly scored,falling a few feet from : the lice. Sharp, however, did the trick a few minutes later, but Parkinson failed at goal. Runcorn were now six points in arrears, but they were by no means dis- couraged, and good work by Farmer, Messenger, and Butterworth kept Hull eosely penned. couple. of transgressions by Hull in a few minutes enabled Jolley to land a couple of coals, and this broueht the visitors within two points ef tho home score. Tne battle was exciting, herealioute, but Hull kept their line intact,and a close game ended as follows :—Hull, 1 goal, 2 tries (8 Ipoints) ; Runcorn, 3 goals (6 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Leigh V. Oldham.</head>
        <p>Leigh V. Oldham.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before 6,000 spectators. Leigh, with the aid of the wind, attacked, and after some play by the forwards, Molyneux dropped a goal. Shortly before the intnval Eluzz.v scored a try, after good passing, no goal resulting. After changing ends Huzzny placed a goal from a penalty kick.. Leigh afterwards had hard luck in not scoring. frlnal:-o:dham 1 goal 1 try (5 points) to Leigh 1 try (2 pins).</p>
        <p>Halifax fall before the Broughton Rangers. penalty shot, when the brothers before 5,000 Halifax played their first game at home, spectators. Halifax had but a brief look in, during Halifax were fas:o sl c on roe d and which Rigg misled a James got the ball out to their backs, and Wilson scored a fine try in five minutes.W James making the score as many pointe.The Etanson-laners effected a brilliant burst after this, but the effort was spasmodic, and the Broughton halves were soon at the other end, where Harris dropped a prett i, goal. Joe Riley., Rigg, and I Arnold tried hard to get away, and Jones failed with a penalty. Fine passing fellowed from the Broughton backs, W James, Hogg. and Jackson playing splendidly, Played much better and Wilson completed a fine r ff?rt in meritorious fashion, match for the cianVi g iJtthaoemr sse.elsevok jni cdskrnibneaggi bait, hut dghovipustl Wilson swo the score at the finish VMS—Broughton Rangers five goals four tries (22 points), to Halifax nil. Brighouse Beaten by the Royal Borough Though favoured by fine weather, the appearance of the Brighouse Rangers at Salford did not prove an at- traction of exceptional merit, a cr?wd of only some 1 seven or eight thousand people turning out. Brown,</p>
        <p>Price 2 and T Williams floored for the home team. The combination of the Salford ream was occasionally at fault, but they were nevertheless faster and smarter all</p>
        <p>round, and unquestionably deserved their victory, the closing score being—Salford two goals, four tries (16 points), to Brighouse Rangers nothing. Defeat of the Yorkshire Champions.</p>
        <p>The opening match of the season, at Hunslet with Bradford, was not remarkable for fine play, but the genet&amp; performance proved quite as good as there was reason to expect. Bradford had all the best of the first half, but when the change of ends tock place Bunslet vastly improved, and though deprived of the services of Smith, one of their three-quarters, whoie shoulder was put out, they played up with great vigour, and ap- peared to last better than the Bradford men. Hunslet finally won by eight points to six.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Widnes v. Rochdale Hornets.</p>
        <p>„. At Widnes, in fine weather, before 1,500 spectators. be opening play was desultory, b.it towards the inter- val Widnes attacked strfflogly, flown, Hampson, and Rispon Lein prominent, and Curtis scoring an unimproved try. In the second half Aspey landed a penalty goal for Widncs,aud Riley scored an unimproved try. Result—Widnes one goal and two tries (8 points) • to Rochdale Hornets nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>St. Helens v. Wigan.</head>
        <p>St. Helens v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>At Wigan. In the first half Wigan had the best of play, Webster scoring a good try after five minutes play. Then St. Helens were penalised, and Brown kicked a goal, and just before the interval, Mu'linens made his mark, and Foulkes kicked • goal for St. Helens. In the second half pay was even. Rouse scored again for Wigaa. Final—Wigan one g4al cad two tries (8 points). to St. Helens one goal (2 points). Birkenhead v. Radcliffe.</p>
        <p>At Birkenhead, the weather was bright and the attendance good. Resul—Rirkenhead two goals and four tries (16 points), to Radcliffe nothing.</p>
        <p>Morecambe v Barrow.</p>
        <p>On tho ground of the former, in fine weather. More- cambe had the bast of the game in the first half, lead- log by five points to three. Barrow then quickly reversed:matters, Williams scoring twice and Walton kicking a goal from a mark. Result :—Barrow two goals and three trieg (13 points) to Morecambe, one goal and a try (5 points.) Millcirn v. Lancaster.</p>
        <p>At Millona. Hall kicked a penalty goal, and Lancas- ter led by Wig at the interval. Turning round. Young scortd at once, followed by Austin tvrice,Eagers goaling one frnill touch. R,Wlt—Millom one eoal and three tries (11 points), to Lancaster one goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Stockport v. Altrincham.</head>
        <p>Stockport v. Altrincham.</p>
        <p>At Stockport. before three thousand spectators. Trotter kicked off, and •fter ten minute ‘.• play Oram kicked a goal.which was followed by tries by Foster and Itlbinson. Half-time—Stockport sight point s, to Altrin- charn nil. On resuming, Stocicport maintained the upper hand. B,oh kicked a vat Trevaski• scored a try. Orarn failing to convert. Final—Stockvrt two goals sad three tries (13 points), to Altrinchans nothing.</p>
