      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>LANCASHIRE RUGBY COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE RUGBY COM- BINATION.</p>
        <p>Runcorn "A" v. Fletcher Russells.</p>
        <p>The football season at Runcorn was opened with the above match. Tee weather was fine and there was a fair attendance. The home team was slightly altered from that advertizad, Weston taking the place of Her- bert Jones in the three quarter line. Tomlinson also filled the vacancy caused by the absence of Brown. tietley started for Runcorn and the visitors were immediately compelled to touch down. Ashurst for the visitors, afterwards had a shot at goal from a mark but failed, the angle being a difficult one. The visitors showed plenty of dashi but their efforts were nullified by the smart tsc!ies of Wilkinson. W Houghton narrowly escaped landing a goal for Runcorn but eventually Weston received and handing to White at the right moment the latter player scored in a nice position. Houghton, however, failed to convert. A short time aft,rwards tha Runcorn forwards got the ball at their toes and dribbled almost the length of the field, C Houghton scoring. Eiketh added the necessary points and half time arrived with Runcorn leading by eight Pointe to nil. Runcorn almost scored again in the initial stages of the second half the visitors being penned continually in their own 25. Clare, however, brought relief with a fine run and dashed over the line, the goal kick failing. A short time before the close Williams un- fortunately collided with BAley and was somewhat severely injured. Final: Runcorn A. 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points), Fletcher Russells, 1. try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Rec. v. St. Helens Shamrocks.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn. The Recreation tried eight fresh men. Shamrocks played down the slope. The Recreation defence was tested by good kicking and passing. The Recreation assumed the aggressive, Hall, of the Sham- rocks, being sent off for striking an opponent. Final : Rune= Recreation, 0 ; Sr. Helens Shamrocks, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Lancashire League.</p>
        <p>Warrington Lose Their First Match.</p>
        <p>The Warringtonians journeyed to Barrow-in-Furness on Saturday, where they met the new acquisitions to the Lancashire League in Barrow who during the course of the week had fulfilled a successful engagement with Blackpool. Pies ing with the wind and slope in their favour the visitors at once became aggressive and were only pulled up within sicooting range by Eccleston. Barrow retaliated and Saunders bad a shot but Aspinall cleared effeztively. Combined play by Sharpies and Murray was responsible for Warrington again invading, but the finishing touches were weak and Docberty had little trouYe• in forcing a hasty retreat. Sharpies eluded the Barrow rear line, but was pulled up from offside when he looked dangerous. Tricky play by Hammond and Murray almost yielded something sub- stantial, and it req „deed all the attentions of the Berrow backs, Findlay and Docherty, to keep the visitors at bay. Hammond missed by inches whilst a brilliant shot by Crafg from a long range almost took effect. Barrow next had a turn, and fine defensive work was exhibited by Cook and Haworth. Sharpies raced away in fine style. but the defence always proved superior to the attack and at the interval there was no score. Restarting Wood made tracks for the Warrington goal and Asninall was early tested, a clever clearance being duly acknowledged. Combined play by the Barrow front rank resulted in Hell scoring a clever goal. Aspin all Saved several shots in fine style, and eventually War- rington had a long spell of attacking, but all efforts to draw level were futile, and at the close the score read: Barrow one goal, Warrington nil. Haydock V. Chorley.</p>
        <p>• This was the opening match at Haydock. Both sides were greatly changed in composision to that of last season. HaT dock had the benefit of the breeze, but the sun in their foe's, in the first half. For the first few minutes play Was in the 113v:tack half. The home lot then found their feet and had hard lines ; they ought to have scored twice. The game was fought with great spirit by both ideg. Half-time—No score. Final— Chortey 3, Haydock nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR ClalP.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR ClalP.</p>
        <p>(Preliminary Round). St Helens Town v Earlestown•</p>
        <p>Played at the primrose ground, S: Helens. The home team bad the wind and sun in their favour, and alter opening exchanges they vigorously attacked and were repelled, .nd the. vltiting right became dangerous. Wainwright clearing just in the nick of time. This he was called upon t..) do again after Stirling. who was playing instead of 04krs, had been beaten. Earestown came again, and Barkley got well round Wainwright, whose partner had to concede a coiner. The pressure was ultimately relieved by Sidebotbam,who transferred to Lee and E7,clee. These two raced away splendidly, sending the ball to Lancaster on the left, and Lancaster passed to Jackson, who sctred from close quarters. This succe iss came n ten minutes from the start. Final —St Helens Town. 2 goals ; Earlestown, nil.</p>
