        <p>ART EXHIBITION AT WARRINGTON --- Warrington has been specially favoured by the Board of Education, South Kensington. in being selected as a centre for the exhibition of works selected from the national competition of schools of art. Three other towns only in England are to be similarly honoured, namely Leeds, Norwich, and Leicester. It will therefore be seen that a special privilege is being conferred upon the public of Warrington and district, in being brought within easy access of an exhibition of works which have gained gold and silver medals in the National Competition. Three years ago a similar exhibition was held in Warrington, and such was the appreciation manifested by students and others interested in art, that the Board have again selected Warrington as a centre. Some idea of the excellence of tee works may be formed from the fact that, they are the best that have been produced in the Schools of Art throughout Great Britain and Ireland. Tue premiated works in the National Competition were sent up from 149 towns and 31. London districts, and the s-lection exhibited at the Warrington School of Art represents about one-sixth of the total nu.mb7. The number of works sent up for examination was , as follows i-44,474 from 303 Schools of Art and Branch schools : 28,592 from 266 Science schools; 11,408 from 429 Art classes. Or these 8,902 works were entered for national competition, The works exhibited at Warrington are those which were awarded gold and silver medals,and number about 200. Admirable ar-angements have been made for the displaying of the works, under the supervision of Mr C 13 Aylward, headmaster at the School of Art. The school has recently been fitted with thf- electric light and has been renovated throughout, and at the present time presents a bright and neat appearance. On entering the building the visitor should first proceed to the modelling room which is devoted almost exclusively to designs, with their practical applications. For instance, alongside some of the drawings is co be seen a piece of tapestry or embroi- dery on which the design is carried our. Many of the designs can be applied to industries, and serve to show the value of art as adapted for manufacs taring purpoies. The elementary room ig utilized for the, exhibition of fancy design and drawings of ; plant life, together with works selected from the various examinations held in May. Proceeding to the antique room, upstairs, the visitor may see a number of architectural drawings, which cannot fail to be of interest. In the painting room on the opposite side of the corridor there are a number of black and white illustrations suitable for books, and also a_collection of coloured drawings to be utilized in the production of children's story bolks. For instance the nursery rhyme "Sing a song o' sixpence "is fully illustrated by about a dozen pictures. In addition some sculptury by local students is exhibited, the promising work of J Millard being prominent. In this connection it is interesting to note that Mr Millard won his scholarship at the Warrington School of Art and has recently been successful in securing the appointment of modelling master at Dover. He willleave Warrington shortly to take up the position. The exhibition was opened in the School of Art on Saturday and continued until Thursday the 12th inst. The works will be on view from 10 a.m. to 9 p.m. On Friday evening,at the invitation of the Chairman (Alderman Monks) and members of the Technical Instruction Sub-committee, the members of the Council and their friends had a private view of the exhibition.</p>
