      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. C. Parr Asked to Accept the</p>
        <p>Position.</p>
        <p>Cross-voting at_a Caucus Meeting.</p>
        <p>The absence of the Mayor (Alderman Roberts J.P.), on his holidays seems to have encouraged the members of the Town Council to consider the question of the Mayoralty for the ensuing year. Additional interest is lent to the appointment in view of the Coronation next year which is supposed to add extra inducements for•the office. In War- rington, the meeting of members of the Council at which the question of the Mayor- alty is considered, is usually held at the end of September, but for some reason or other, the meeting was held on Thursday last week. The proceedings were field in camera,but we learn that the meeting was presided over by the Deputy- Mayor (Alderman Fairclough). Twenty-nine mem- bers of the Council were present, and ttie business of the meeting was the selection of a gentleman to be Mayor for the en-uing year, and the election of five aldermen. Toe aldermen who retire on the 9:h of November next are Aldermen Roberts, Bolton, Hutchinson, Heaton and Francomb. It was resolved that the retiring aldermen be re-elected. This business dis- posed of, the selcctionl'of a Mayor was considered. The discussion which ensued, was at times unusu- ally warm, but as no :reporters were present the comments of the different speakers are unrecorded. It may however be stated that a resolution was submitted by Alderman Sutton that Mr J Charlton ! Parr, JP, should be asked to accept the office of Mayor for the ensuing yea.. Alderman Bolton seconded the proposition, and Councillor William Pierpoint supported t. After some discussion, Councillor Elli- son moved a proposition that Alderman Henry Roberts, J.P., be asked to continue in office for another year. This was seconded by Councillor A J Jolley. On a vote being taken 12 voted for Mr Parr, nine for Alderman Roberts and eight members did tiot vote at all. There was some cross-rioting. A sub-committee consisting of Aldermen Sutton and Bolton and Councill,,r William Pierpoint was appointed to wait upon Mr parr with a request that he should occupy the position of Mayor. On Friday afternoon the deputation relerred to had an interview with Mr J C Parr, who gave no definite decision, but promised to give careful con- sideration to the matter.</p>
        <p>GARDEN FETE AT PADGATE. ---- Opening by Captain Appleton.</p>
        <p>Success attended the annual garden fke in aid of various parish organizations which was held in the grounds attached to Christ Church Vicarage, Padgate, on Ttoursday last • week. The climatic conditions were all that could be desired, and consequently many visitors were attracted to the. grounds. The stalls had been tastefully arranged in tents, and flags and bunting had been utilized for decorative purposes. A new departure this year was a shooting gallery in the erection of which much time and labour had been expended. The stalls were heavily laden with a variety of u'seful and fancy artic'es which commanded a ready gale. Captain Appleton, who has been closely identified with the work of the parish, performed the opening ceremony. He also provided a highly interest ing feature of the ri:e in the shape of a collection c- photographs and curios from South Africa, which were exhibited in a separate tent. The collection is a most valuable one, the photographs including those of the scenes of many hard fought engagements, such as Spion Kop, Pieter's Hill, and-the Tugela. There were also portraits of Volunteers who had died in the service of their country, and one of the late Colone O'Leary, who commanded the "Fighting Fortietti" and was killed at Pieter's Hill. The curios inolude a num- ber of Boer shells, bullets, &amp;c. The Penksth Tannery Band was in attendance and gave a capital selection of music.</p>
        <p>1 At the opening ceremony the Vicar (Rev W Pollock Hill) presided and the attendance included the Revs i Canon Willis, F E Powell, H V Pigot, E H Tartmck, G I Vale Owen, B Percival and E C E Carleton, Mrs Bea. moat and Mrs Sharpe. The Vicar said he was sure that if nowhere else, at least at Padgate Captain Appleton needed no ictroduc- tion whatever. When he left to serve his country in I leouth Africa he was a churchwarden at Carlin Church, and after his departure he (the speaker) eould assure him that they had all watched the course of events at the front and read the Captains letters with the great est interest. They had been delighted with the honours showered upon him by his fellow townsmen but he did not want Captain Appleton to think for a moment that they were adding another honour by ask- ing him to open that flee. On the contrary they felt . the honour was the other way. With regard to the objects of the 16te, the rev gentleman said that in a small parish like that—tut nevertheless a parish in which there was a great deal of work going on—it was absolutely necessary in order to keep above water, to have come special effort each year. Thie wag the seventh annual fite and he hoped it would prove exceedingly successful. The funds depend ent on the proceeds of the fi e were the mothers' meeting, the church house (a most admirable institution in which they held a great many meetings), the churchwarden's fund which this year he was afraid had been left with a balance on the wrong side, and above all there was the school enlarge- ment fund. They hoped in a short time to be able to commence building. They had obtained a site,and hal a fair amount of money in the bank,but unfortunately it was not suffloiont, and they did not wish to com- mence to build knowing that they should remain in debt. After supporting the other funds mentioned the surplus would be devoted to the enlargement fund. Captain Appleron,who bad a cordial reception ascured the gathering that he considered it a very great honour to be asked to open the sale. To come th Padgate was like a second home coming, and only those who had been away from home for some time could realize what home coming meant. He should like to say a few words about the wk he had seen the Church doinc whilst he was in Soeth Africa, not only the Church of England but the Roman Catholic Church and the Nonconformists. Re and his men experienced a great deal of kindness from all of them. When they were in Dundee the Vicar of Dundee not only came to call on them, but in the midst of his Sun lay work he would cscle over to Talana HPI, where they were encamped, to conduct a service. On the first anniversary of the battle of Talana Hill, many of the clerg) and people of Dundee came to decorate the graves of soldiers who fell at Talana Hill, and also sent all kinds of luxuries for his men. What was particu- larly Sad about the Vicar of Dundee, was that after sticking to Ilia church all through the Boer occupation of Dundee, and saving it aid his home froth the general wreckage in the town, he was taken ill with enteric, and when the volunteers were encamped at Gregory's Nek they heard of his death. The Captain went on to speak of the hardships endured by Archdeacon Barker and his family during the seige of Ladysmith. His church was struck with a shell and another shell burst within ten yards of where Mrs Barker was sitting in the garden. He was sure the work of the clergy in Ladysmith would never die. , Captain Appleton also spoke of the work done by the Roman Catholic Priest, Father Collin,who was rewarded with the distinguished service order. He was one of the chaplains of the 11th brigade, lane when there was any fighting going on he was always to be found with a large water bottle, ready to do all he could for the wounded and never sparing himself. The Volunteers also received great kindness at the hands of a Wesleyan minister in Ladysmith: who buried the last Volunteer who died in South Africa. The Captain then declared the fete open. On the motion of the Rector seconded by Mr Dennett a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Captain Apple- ton and business commenced for the day. The following is a list of the stall holders :—Fancy stall, Mrs Pcllock Hill, assisted by Mrs Royston and Mrs Cowin ; Flower stall. the Misses Dirties; Market stall, Mrs James Houghton assisted by Mesdames Houle,</p>
