      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 13, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON UNION ASSESSMENT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNION ASSESSMENT</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Trade of a Bewsey Beer-house A meeting of this committee was be d on Friday in the Board Room at the Workhouse. Mr G Pilling pre- sided and there were also present:—Messra n Davies, .1 Billinge, T Halton, A Jolley, R Forshaw, with J C Sut- ton (clerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk). WAIE ASSESSMENT OF THE "GROVE.' Mr A BRowNE, on behalf of Mr George Beard, ob- jected to the increased assessment of the "Grove" beer- nouse, stables and yard in Florsey-road, the assessment being gross £7O, rateable £53 103. He said the ground of his objection was that the assessment had been in- creased tor three successive years. The CLERK said the explat ation was that two lorry loads of beer went to the house every week. Mr BROWNE said the applicant was not doing ong third of the businers he did four years ago. It was well known that the business at Dallam Forgo was in wretched condition. Toe CLERK asserted that business was all right there now. They could not get the men to work. Only that morning he had been assured that they had to go to the men and beg and pray them to go to their work. Mr BROWNE said it was a well known fact that for eighteen months business had been slack at the works Tne people over Bewsey Bridge had been starving. Mr Beard was of opinion that if he did not appeal, the committee woirld go on increasing the assessment every year- Although there Was not a quirquennial valua- tion in Warrington ho thought there was an under- standing that occe an assessment was fixed it should not be increased for five years. He did not object to an assessment of something like £5O. In reply to Mr BILLINGE, Mc BROwNE said that in four years only one new house had been put up over Bowsey Bridge. The Cr.tax said it would not he long before Greening's new works were finished, and it was probable that they would lead to an increase or custom for Mr Beard. The trade would increase as the population irereased. Mr LAWRENCE (who represented Mr Woodceck (the assistant overseer) said the original assessment stood a £4O-104 for a considerable number of years* The rent was £52. Mr DAvIES said that he had made it his business to watch the house on two nights for a period of about half an hour on each occasion, and for one man who went in there, 20 or 30 went to the house on the other side of the bridge. I: did not seem therefore that the house was doing a good trade. Mr LAWRENCE said the hou3e was a tied one, and Mr Beard sold about 20 barrels of beer per week. It was supposed to be a very good house. Mr Beard paid the rates and taxes. The CLERK said the injustice to the tenant was that I the brewer made such a tlistinction betreen the free and the tied house. The brewer ought not to compel the tenant of a tied house to pay a fee per b.rrel, or, if he did, he, and not the tenant, out to pay the rates and taxes. The committee decided to make no alteration .in the assessment.</p>
        <p>THE VALUE OF AN OFF LICENSED HOUSE. Mr WHITFIELD ot 58 L-gu-street, appealed against the assessmenr. of an off licensed house which was assessei at £BO gr3ss, and £5l rateable. He pointed out that the rent was £4O. Mr LAWRENCE informed the committee that the rent piid by the brewery company to the owner ;was £6O and the place :was recently bought by Messrs Peter Walker and Sons for £2,500. It was decided to make no alteration. SUGGESTIONS TO THE SOMAIITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr E L 1;1;1-1-N-G, who appealed on hehatt of Messrs Bishop and Company Limited against the full assess- ment of their works, asked the committees whether they would consider the advisability of making batter accommodation tor those who had to appeal against avieements. At present they were obliged to wait out- side. .</p>
        <p>-- The CHAIRMAN said an .alteration was being made in the Board room and that had caused the inconvenience that morning. Mr FLEXING said he would like also to suggest that a list should he affixed outside the Board room so that parties waiting might know when to expect their turn of being called into the room. The CLERK : That would bind the committee to follow the list. We do not do so at present. For instance you are at the bottom of the list this morning and you have been called in before others who are higher up on the list. VT.W Mr FLEMING then preceeded with his appeal and stated that some time ago he came before the committee and explained that the works had been stopped for want of raw material. Tne committee thereupon re- duced the asse+siment. Since then the assessment had been raised. Toe works were being partially worked and the company asked for the committee's clemency until they had succeeded in establishing a constant supply of raw material. It was decided to leave the matter in the hands of , Mr Owen.</p>
      </div>
