      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>-- Solid Food taken with Relish.</head>
        <p>-- Solid Food taken with Relish.</p>
        <p>An Exchange Company's Buffalo telegram states : —The bulletin issued at 9 20 yesterday reports that the President spent a quiet and restful night. He has taken much nourishment, and feels better this morning than at any time since he was shot. He has taken a little solid food, which he relished, His pulse is 120, and his temperature 100'2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>Commandant Vandermerwe</p>
        <p>Killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Son of Mr. Kruger Surrenders.</head>
        <p>A Son of Mr. Kruger Surrenders.</p>
        <p>The War Office yesterday issued the following despatch frem Lord Kitchener : Presidency. Pretoria, 3 p.m. 11th September.</p>
        <p>French reports that Crabbe's column engaged Vaudermerwe commando to the east of Laingsburg at dawn yesterday. _</p>
        <p>, Killed Vandermerwe and one other, and captured 37 prisoners. lhe remai,ider fled east. C Kruger, son of the ex-President, and Captain Ferreira surrendered to-day.</p>
        <p>Meritorious Services. Lord Hobert&amp;S List. The Local Names. Tuesday night's 1851.13 of the " London Gaznte " contained a lengthy dermatch from Lord Roberts, in which his lordship brings to the notice of the Secretary for war the names of regimental officers. sioned officers, and men of the regulars, mililia,l yeomanry, and volunteers, together with a few irregulars and civilians " who with their various units have rendered special and meritorious service." The despatch is in continuation of one dated April 2nd, in which Lord Roberts brought to notice the names of certain staff and other ofli :erg. The list of names now published by the Commanderin.Claief, occupies over 40 pages of the " Gazette." Amongst the best-known names now brought to 'notice are those of Colonel J H Yule, Colonel Sir CS B Parsons, Colonel Lord W C C Gerard, Lancashire BUSSaTS Imperial Yeomanry ; Major Lord Rosmearl, Lancashire Fusiliers ; Captain the Marquis of Tollibardine,Royal Horse Guards ; Honorary Colonel Viscount Valentia, Oxfordshire Imperial Yeom- anry ; Captain the Earl of Cardi,ran, Wilts Imperial Yeomanry; Lieutenant-Colonel the Earl of Scarborough, Yorkshire Dragoons Imperial Yeomanry ;and Lieutenant- Colonel the Earl of Albemarle, 12:h Middlesex Volusteer I Rifle Corps. The civil list included the names of Major Sir John , Willoughby, Major Burnham (1130 American scout), and a number of civil chaplains, amorg them being the Rev Canon Knox Little. A ffunabr of StITZPOGIS and manes are also mentioned, this portion of the list including the name of Lady Sarah Wilson, who acted as matron of the Convalescent Hospital at Msfeking. It is interesting to note also that five native drivers— Belmont, Hewitt, Jim. Minto, and Willie—find a place , in the list for their services with the Royal Berks trans- port. In conclusion the Commander-in•Claief commends the work of the civil hospitals in South Africa, including the Irish, the Yeomanry, the Langraan, the Welsh, the Princess Chri.tian. the Edinburgh, the Scottish. the National, the Van Alen, and the Portland, and brings to notice the names of persons who !plated to Equip them and maintain them in a Ftat9 of efficiency. LOCAL List Staff Offieers—Colonal Lord W C Gerrard (Lancashire Huesara), Imperial Yeomanry. The Prince of Wales (South Lancashire) Regiment.— Lieutenant-Colonel R W Hall, MD ior T Lamb,Major F I Adam, Captain 9 Upperton. Cantain E T James,Captain B R Govens, Captain E F Oakelev, Lieutenant A 11 Railer, Lieutenant a K Woods, Quartermaster and Honorary •Lieutenant J Huxford, Sergeant-Major G Devlin, Colour-Sergeant H Cleaver, Colour-Sergeant T Foster, Colour-Sergeant J Nolan. Colour-Sergeant O'Brien, Colour-Sergeant Simon, Corporal 13 Mellor. Lance-Corporal J Cunningham, Lance-Cornora: J Ludlow, Private P ("matter, Private T Frost, Private G Johneon, Private J;Lawson,Private S OrGaw,Druserner G Handley. The Prince of Walea's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment) 3rd Battalion.—Lieutenant-Colonel R I Black- bursae, Majors M H A F Tarbst. C MG, Captain R Greg, Lieutenant H G Clarkson, Quartermaster and Tfon-LieutecantJ Morrell, Sergeant-Major J A Altmann, 1939 Quartermaerer-Sergeant S T Boast. 2133 Serpeant Instructor J H Kirk. 2335 Sergeants H Lynes, 6739 J Matthews, and 63705 J Richardson. WARRINGTON TRADES' COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>There was large attendance at the minthly meet- ing of the Warrington Trades' Council which was held in the Druids' Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. Mr J Edwardson occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>THE PENRYHN CHOIR CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY said they would all agree that the concert given by the Penchyn Cho:r was a sp:endid suclesi. He cordially thanked all who had rendered assistance. The members of the choir were highly de- lighted with the sUCCPSS of the concert, as a reau!t of which they would be able to hand over to the relief fund st least £4o,pribatly more. The choir had taken £3l. with them tor which be (Mr Stott) had a receipt. He hoped at the next meeting to present a detailed balance sheet.</p>
        <p>DI2PCITE AT LoNCIFORD DEPYT. A letter was read tr:m the secretary of the local branch of the National Union of Gas Workers and General Labourers, stating that they were about to hold a public meeting, to protest against the conduct of the Sanitary Committee cf the Warrington Corporation in rrfusing to negotiate with them on the question of in- creased wages of the men eruplovrd at Longford depot. If the Council was prepared ta give the meeting its support, they would esteem it a favour. The SECRETARY stated that Mr Clynes, the district secretary of the Union, had written to the Chairman and the Committee and to Mr Demi and had received no reply. The men were receiving wages below the rate paid in moat towns for the Farce class of work. Mr Clynes bad amked for the matter to be considered by the Committee, but they would not recognise Mr Clynes nr the Society. The men had consequently decided to hold a pubic meeting on the 20th inst. Mr Joe, the secretary of the local branch of the Union, said the men wanted the publlc to know the conditions under which they ' were working. About 19 men were effected. Am regarded the rate ot wages, farnacemen received about 27e. a week, hopper feederr, ; and the driers-up, lar. They wanted to receive the standard rate paid in other districts. Another delegate stated that men similarly employed at St. Helens worked only eight hours a day whilst those at Warrington worked 62 or 68 hours a week. After some diacuision the Council decided to give the meeting its support.</p>
        <p>AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Me99TB FA RGFUR and Wiccocics were elected auditors for the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>As the next meeting of the Council is the annual meeting, nominations for the various office were now taken. The following delegates were nominated for Varintl9 positior;s and the election will take place at the neat meeting :—President, Messrs J Edwardson, E Plinstom and Greiler; Vicc-President, Mcssrs Battersby. Plumb. Plinston. Podmore, Edwardson. Wilcocks and liamh!et ; Secretary, Me3srs F Smtt, Rillock, Piton, Grealey, Plumb, Farghar ; Treasurer, Mr Malone. This was all the tusiness. A Local Volunteers Success. -</p>
        <p>The annual rifle co-nte,t between teams of 20 men a side, selected from the members of the Ist V.B. (King's Liverpool Regiment) and the 2ad V.B. South Lancashire Regiment, was held at Altcar ranges on Saturday. The weather was fine, and notwithstanding the prevalence of a strong vari- able wind some excellent shouting was accom- pliebed. The Liverpool men beat their opponents by one point at 200 yards, whilst at the longer ranges they improved considerably, gaining 54 at 500 and 40 at 600 yards,winning easily by 95 points The highest scorer on the winning side was Private G Watmough, who is a Warringtonian,and was for many years a member of the local corps. He was the recipient of a handsome gold medal, pre- sented by Lieutenant Wilcocke, of the 2nd V.B. south Lancashire Regiment. The GLEN-SPET DISTILLERY. Situate in the finest Whisky-producing District of Scotland, is the property of W. &amp;A. Gilhey. This Whisky Is made entLrely from home-grown Barley and Is kept absolutely unblended In his Msjenty's Bonded Warehonses to mature, and sold by W.&amp; A. 011 bey's agents in every town e $3. 6d per bottle;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON•I</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON•I</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD RESIDENT. At the age of 69 years, Mr James Lowe, ef 298, Earle-street, Earlestown,tdied very suddenly as the result of an apoplectic stroke. The deceased left home last Saturday week on a visit to his son at Crewe and was at the time in his usual state of health. He was in excellent spirits until about 5 o'clock on the Thursday evening following, when be was seized with a fit and fell from his chair. He was rendered unconscious and remained in this condition until he died early on Saturday morning. The deceased for very many years was employed as a wheelwright at the Viaduct works, and was held in high esteem by his fellow workmen. About 3 years ago, owing to failing health, and having reached the age limit he retired on a pension, but he has not enjoyed the best of health, being a martyr to rheumatism. Many years ago, when the penny readings were an institution at Earlestown. Mr Lowe was a prominent figure and several old residents can well remember the songs and recita- tions deceased gave to entertain his audiences. Expressions of sympathy were numerous on the news of his daath becoming known. The funeral took place on Wednesday at the Cemetery and was attended by a large number of relatives and friends who sent many beautiful final tributes. The officiating clergyman was the Rev J Carson, rector of Newton. PRIrE DISTRIBUTION AT THE NEWTON REFORMATORY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Bishop of Liverpool paid a visit to the Farm School, Newton-le-Willows and distributed prizes to the boys for meritorious work and condue-. during the year. On an averagp 150 boys are in training at Newton, and reports of the past year's work show that much progress has been made in industrial and technical pursuits, while as regards education—which includes every subject in the curriculum of the bast Board schools —discipline, religious, moral,and physical develop- ment, there is every reason for congratulation. As an evidence of the excellent results achieved it may be mentioned that no fewer than 94 prizes have been won by the boys during the twelve months for proficiency in music.athletics and religious and industrial subjects, the latter including farm work gardening, joinery, tailoring, shoemaking, laundry work, baking, printing, and even darning and hair- cutting. Ten of the awards were granted for good conduct, two for tidiness, and one, a silver watch and chain, presented by Lord Newton for trust- worthiness. This was won by W Bannister. Seven prizes were won by the hand,the members of which discoursed selections in the school yard prior to the prize distribution. The boys also gave an impres- sive rendering of the "Psalm of Life" Among those present were Canon Penrhyn, Rev J.Ryder, Rev E Probyn, Bishop's Chaplain, Dr Watkins, Messrs R Stone, C B F Borron, W Collingwood, and J Digweed (superintendent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ST PETER'S CHURCH NEWTON. -- Dedication of Pulpit and Bells.</head>
        <p>ST PETER'S CHURCH NEWTON. -- Dedication of Pulpit and Bells.</p>
        <p>Since the erection of the fine edifice of St. Peter's a great stimulus hie been given te the work of the Established Church in Newton, and the Vicar (Rev J Ryder) not only finds himself sarrounded by a devoted energetic congregation, but many generous friends. Proof of this assertion was afforded on Saturday night, when the Lord Bishop of the Diocese (Dr Chavasse) de- dicated a richly embellished pulpit and a full peal of bells which had been presented to the cnurch. At the same time a valuable clock, which had been purchased and platted in the steeple at ths expense of the Newton Urban District Council, was set in motion. The provision of this magnificent chrono- meter will meet a long-felt public want, and certainly a more convenient site could not have been procured thaa the lofty tower of Sr. Peter's, easily discernible as it is throughout the surrounding districts. Mrs Airey, of Sandfiell Hal:, Newton-le-Willows, is the generous donor of the costly pulpit, her doeire being to pernetulte the memery of her eeteerned father, the late Mr Hop- wood, of Wigan, whilst adding a lasting and appropri- ate adornment to an otherwise beautiful place of worship. The pulpit is wrought of the finest alabaster and marble, ornstele designed, and sten ,s as a superb specimen of the most delicate workmanship. Each of the eight bells represents the goodwill ef various perscnages. No. 1 bell was subscribed for by the congregation ; the second has bean given by the Masonic brethren of the province ; the third by Mr G Aire)? ; fourth by the choir, day and Sunday school teachers and the men's bible class: fifth by Lieut.-Colonel Pilkington. M.P. ; sixth by Mr F J WOOO : seventh by Mr T J Gillespie ; and the eighth by the Sunday school children. To many ears, therefore, the chimes will sound with a partlcular sweetness. Prior to the formal dedication ceremony a well- attended Service was held at the church, the vicar (Rev J Rysler) and the Rev Canon Pearhyn.rector of Wiovekk, officiating. The choir rendered the anthem, " Lift up your heads," and Dr E Hutton Middleton, F.R.C.O , pre- sided at the organ. In the course of an eloquent ser- mon the Bishop referred to the use and abuse of the pulpit. It rnugt not, he observed, be a political plat- form, A clergyman had a perfect right to hold his own political views and to exercise his vote as a Briti3h citizen, but he had no right to proclaim his politic's' views from any pulpit of the Courch of England. Instancing the duty of the laity, his Lordship declared that there must be receptivity, moral earnestness, and wholeheartedness on the part of those who attended church, otherwise what was preachod from the pulpit would be of no avail. In his deity . avecations the</p>
        <p>• typical Lancashire man despised half-heartednePs, and it was to be hoped he would infuse his characteristic spirit of whole-heartedinsi more and more into church life. After the dedication the ringers from St .L.mes', Pootstock, Wigan, gave a peal on the bells.</p>
        <p>Remarkable Accident to Mr Baxter,Dutton Hall: Last Friday morning an accident befel Mr Thomas Baxter, Dutton Hall, which happily has not proved of a very serious character. He was going about the farm at 6.30. Seeing two men wiih a large load of straw ready for binding, and the other workers being busy he got on the top to give them a pull, whilst the waggoner manipulated the ropes at the bottom, he being the best man at tying the slip boose. The rope broke and Mr Baxter fell a dis- tance of about 15 feet. He was conveyed home and Dr Smith,Weaverharn,was speedily summoned. That gentleman on examination found his patient suffering from several bruises on the face, &amp;c„ and a severe shaking, but no lire's were broken. A remarkable incident happened in connection with the accident. Mr Baxter's valuable gold watch fell from his pocket, and was not missed until three o'clock in the afternoon, when be asked for it. His wife went to the spot, and discovered it lying face downwards, in perfect going order, and without a single scratch upon it,notwithstanding that 15 cart loads of clover had been drawn over the roadway in which it was found. Mr Baxter is a member of the Cheshire County Council and the Runcorn Rural Council: be is also One of the most prominent agri- culturists in the county. Under these circum- stances he ie very extensively known, and his host of friends will be delighted to know that he now feels very little the worse for the accident.</p>
        <p>70,PISE4SIB OF THU &amp;YRS cm be cured even when other means have failed: MELLOWS TREATMENT! Pamphlet free.—MELLon, 233, Oxford. street, Man- ehester.—Advt (3). Military Funeral at Warrington. The remains of the late Lance-Corporal Hodges, of the Warrington Volunteer battalion. whose sad death was reported in our last issue, were interred in the cemetery with full military honours on Sunday afternonn. The deceased went to South Africa with the first contingent of Volunteers, under Captain Appleton. and unfortunately contracted dysentery. He was invalided home,rind despite care- ful medical attention succumbed to the disease on Tuesday in last week. The deceased was employed at Messrs Peter Walker and Sons, Dallam•lane, and every possible effort was put forth with a view to his recovery, both by the employee of the firm, and also by the officers of the Volunteer battalion and the Transvaal R-lief Committee.—At half-past twelve on Sunday a detachment of Volunteers, under the ummand of Captain Appleton. assembled at the Drill Hall, and headed by the Regimental Band, proceeded to the deceased's late residence in Sharp-street. The band played the Dead March in • "Saul " at intervals. until the Cemetery gate was reached, The funeral party were met at the Chapel by the Rector (the Rev Canon Willis) and-the Rev W Bracecamp (St. Peter'sl. The fir:tt portion of the I service Noss read by the Rev W Bracenemp, whilst the concluding half was rend by the Rector. The service in the church being finiAled, the sad pro- cession wended its way to the graveside, where the detachment of Volunteers formed a cordon, outside which the general public stood. The con- cluding portion of the Burial Service having been read, the firing Party fired a volley. The " Last Post " was sounded by the bugle section, and the 'Volunteers proceeded to the gates, and were there formed up. after which they proceeded to the Drill Hall, where they were dismissed. or,ri PAU'S TEETH BOUGHT. -Fall value in cash or offer per return of post. —E. D. &amp; B. iriti.intß, LTD.. 1 Princes dtreet, Ipswich, the largest buyers its the world. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 13, 1901 PATRIOTIC DEMONSTRATION AT NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. [WEDDING OF MISS SHAWGREEN, WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 13, 1901 PATRIOTIC DEMONSTRATION AT NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. [WEDDING OF MISS SHAW- GREEN, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton and Colonel Pilkington on Compulsory Military Service.</p>
        <p>In common with every loyal city, town, and hamlet in the kingdom the district of Newton-le-Willows can recall with feelings of pride the names of gallant sons who responded to their country's call in the dark days when disasler overwhelmed the British troops in South Africa, and last Saturday the residents assembled in goodly Rumbers at the Town Hall to do honour to and place on record their practical appreciation of the returned warriors. Sonia of the men tok part in the campaign as members of C Troop Lancashire Hussars and others with the 32nd Company Imperial Yeomanry, G Company lit Vol. Batt. South Lancashire Regiment, the Cambridge University Rifle Volunteers, and the let Sulk Regiment. On their return from the front it was felt in the locality that, independent of the service medals, they should be the recipients of some tangible testimonial of public esteem, and as the outcome of a fund of which the Rev J Ryder was chairman, Mr W Hindley hon treasurer, and Messrs W A Mawdsley and T Mayor bon secretaries, inscribed watches were available for presentation on Saturday night. The ceremony was presided over by the Rev J Ryder. who was supported by the Bishop of Liverpool, Lord Newton, Lieutenant. Colonel Pilkington, M P, Cap- tainiWithisgton, Captain Appleton, Lieutenant E 8 P.lkington, Lieutenant Dr Strut, the Rev E Probyn,and Messrs C B F Borron, B btone, and R Barton.</p>
        <p>After presenting the awards to the Yeomanry Lord Newton alluded to the efficiency of the Yeomen. de said the ordinary yeoman or volunteer was in many , cases very far from being as well acquainted with his profession as he cught to be. In his (Lord Newtot.'s) own case, for instance he believed he was classed as lefficient by the War-office, but he was very far from being efficient. Be had had no real training as a :soldier, and he was afraid he was now too old to learn the duties satisfactorily. (Laughter.) Candidly he would have to admit that in a military sense he was a complete fraud—(laughter)—and yet in the military organisation he figured as a yeomanry officer of high rank, who was highly efficient and in time of emer- gency would command a body of men. Well, it a large , body of men were despatched to South Africa under his control it would be hardly worieo while for the men to take return tickets. (Renewed laughter.) Still he did not suppose he was very much worse than anyone else, and it was a great fallacy to imagine that because a man called himself a volunteer et a yeoman he was thereby a thorough efficient and the equivalent of such men as they were honeuring that evening. There had been a good many unfavourable comments upon the corn- positien of what was known as the new yeomanry, and he:had seen it stated that many of them were unequal to the tasks they were called upon to perform. Be would have been extremely surprised if they had been really efficient. It wee all very well to talk about mak- ing them more efficient, but if they tried to improve them too raucti they would improve tnern cut at exist- ence altogether. A volunteer and a yeoman was a citizsn, who, from feelings of pure patriotism,gave such leisure time as was at his disposal for the benefit of the country, and he ought not to be expected to do more. Why should a man who already did far more than anybody else be celled upon to do a great deal more in future, while others simply did nothing ? Tne vast majority of the people in this country rendered no military service at all. Their patriotism consisted of bawling songs about absent minded beggars and soldiers at the Queen,while the burden of military defence fell upon an infinitesimal proportion of the population. It was an absolute absurdity that this should be allowed to go on. (Applause.) In his opinion volunteering in England had reached its limits, and if things were to be put upon a satisfactory footing they must adopt a new principle altogether. An army was not created in the time of war, but in time of peace. (Hear, hear.) They were drawing totally wrong lessons from the war. The war in South Africa was distinct from the wars they might have to face in the future. The Boers had only been able to attack us in one portion of the Empire, and had not been able to endanger the safety of the Empire ; consequently England had had time to train her men and turn untrained men into soldiers, but did anyone in his senses think that if we, were involved in a war with a foreign country more our own strength we should be given time for carrying out such a process ? fhe idea was absurd, and although the solution awes so simple, be was looked upon by some people as a fool and by others as somewhat of a lunatic when he propounded it. (Laughter.) The only possible solution for our difficulties was to have a regular army, as at present, recruited voluntarily, but the burden which now fell upon an infinitesimal section of the population in the shape of the volunteers and the yeo.nanry must be borne by the whole cammunite , and behind the regular army there must be a universal militia service in which every able bodied young man should be obliged to serve a certain period, thereby giving to the country a force which might be relied upon in time of crisis. (Applause.) If that principle were eatabliehed they would not only render themselves safe as a nation, but confer an incalculable;moral and physical benefit upon the nation itself, (Applause). Colonel Pilkington, M.P., in handing the trophies to the volunteers, paid a.high tribute to the self-sacrifice of the men, and endoreed the underlying principle of Lord Newton's sentiments with regard to the military sysrem. In his opinion every man between the ages of 20 and 26 ought to go through the military mill. If be did not pass through the militia or the line, be ought to go through the volunteers, so that evezy man in the country, when he attained the age of 21, would know something of the use of arms and military training, and at least know as much as the efficient volunteer of the present day. (Applause). In time of war the, Bing as head of the State, or the Government ought to be able to call open the services of evatry able-bodied man in a certain order. That this was a nenessity was clearly demonstrated by what had happened in Sauth Africa. Of the 250,000 troops engaged, nearly one-half I were volunteers. - - - - -</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool maintained that as a naticn Eng'and had great and terrible sins to deplore, but he was convinced that whf u her history Came to he written the fair historian wad be bound to confess that she had done her very best to teach the nations coin- mitted to her care the nobility of justice, the sweetness of liberty, and the beauty of truthfulnes. If it had 1111:- p.ned that Eogland's sun had set in Ssoth Atrice, and if the nation had bled to death as some of her foes de- ' sired she should, one of the most beneficent and blessed a-,ents for good in the civilised world would have been swept out of esistence,anel the cause of civilisation and Christianity would have been seriously retarded. (Ap plause.) The yeomat.ry and volunteers had gallantly helped to avoid such a distster, and also to abolish slavery in South Africa. Englishmen might have com- mitted acts of barbarism on natives, and En,gl;sh societies and communities might have done savage and oppressive acts of which they ought to he ashamed, but wherever England ruled slavery was illegal, and henceforth the native races of South Africa would receive the right cf British citizenship, and be as free as their white deliverers. (Applause.) The recipients of thd trophies were :—Sergeants R Ball:and W Warren, Troopers R Boardman, R Collinge, A E Jones S Swann, and A Oubridge, yeomanry. Oubridge were killed, and the watches were handed to their representatives ; Cor- porals A Dean and SSmith, Privates 8 Smith, A Hough- ton, T Shalicross, G Singleton, T Moore, C Booth, and E Bevan, volunteers. Other local man who are still at the. front will also receive 9imitar tokens of esteem.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks to the subscribers and the speakers, and the singing of the National Anthem terminated the proceedings.</p>
        <p>Bawsey-road Wesleyan Church was the scene; of a pretty wedding on Wednesday afternoon, when Miss Gertrude Shaw-Green. (second daughter of Alderman b'haw-Green, J P. of Warrington) and Mr J Wilfrid Dupre, (second son of Mr James J Dupris, of Cape House, Samares, Jersey), were united in the holy bonds of matrimony. The bride has bleed closely associated with the work of Bewsey-road church and school, and her father a regular worshipper since its erec- tion and a zealous worker in its behalf. It was therefore not surprising to find at noon on Wednesday the church filled with a congregation of friends and well-wishers anxious to witness the wedding ceremony. During the assembling of the guests, Mr W H Payton, Mus. Bac„ who presided at the organ, played several appropriate voluntaries, includ- ing " Canzona " (Bohm), " .s;alut d'Amour (Elgar), and "My heart ever faithful " (Bach). Promptly at one o'clock the bride entered the church and looked charming in a dress of white silk, delicately trimmed, and wearing a veil and wreath of orange blossoms. She carried a beautiful bouquet of white exotics. Her father (Alderman Shaw Green) gave her away. She was accompanied by three prettily-attired bridesmaids, namely, Miss Shaw Green (sister of the bride). Miss Muriel Brooke., of Bath,and Miss Ida M Silcock,of Warrington. The groomsmen were Mr Leonard , Bennett, of Westle,nds,Padgate,Mr Beverley White, ' of Southampton and Mr Darold Dupre of London (brother of te bridegroom). The service commenced with the singing of the hymn," The voice that breathed o'er Eden." The solemnization of marriage followed, the officiat- ing ministers being the RiV John Grange Bennett (brother-in-law of the bridegroom), Rev John Gunnell (Penketh), and Rev U 11 Hocken (Be wsey-road Wesleyan Church.) At the conclusion of the ceremony, the anthem "God be merciful unto us and bless us "was sung and the Lord's prayer was afterwards joined in by the 'congregation. An interestiw, and impressive ceremony concluded with the singing of" Captain of Israel's host " and the pronouncing of the bene- diction. The wedding party then adjourned to the vestry where the marriage certificate was signed Meanwhile, the organist played the Bridal March from " Lohengrin " (Wagner) and as the party left, the church the strains of Mendelssohn's " Wedding March" resounded through the sacred nuilding. A reception was afterwards held in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, but before proceeding there, the wedding party, by the kind permission of the Member for the Borough (Mr R Pierpoint M.P.,) was photographed upon the lawn within the grounds of St. Austin's, by Mr Thomas Birtles. A large number of invited guests attended the recep- tion, the catering being in the hands of Mrs Hay- ward, of the Winmarleigh Ca!e. An elaborately decorated bride's cake of three tiers had been pro- vided by Mrs Mottram, The wedding presents were displayed upon side tables and the numerous beautiful and choice gifts were greatly admired. At five o'clock, the newly-married couple left for Bank Quay Station and entrained for Cornwall, wl.ere the honeymoon is being spent. Their de- parture was witnessed by numerous friend who gave expression of their hearty good wishes. The bride and bridegroom intend to take up their abode in Southampton et which place the bride- groom will take charge of a branch of his father's business in the perfumery trade.</p>
        <p>It may be added that on Monday last, the bride, who has been in Charge of the youths' Class Silver-street S.'unday School. was presented with a handsome silver salver by the teachers and officers. The presentaticn was made by Mr E Mitchell, the senior teacher, who handed the gift to the bride and conveyed the best wishes of the donors which were admirably expressed in writing, Miss Shaw , Green suitably acknowledged the gilt. Appended will be fcund a list of the wedding preSenti Bridegroom to Bride, sapphire and diamond ring Bride to Bridegroom, sapphire and diamond breastpin Father of Bridegroom, cheque and silver tea and cans service. Father of Bridegroom to Bride, cheque Father of Bride. cheque and picture Mother of Bride, house linen &amp;o. Mother of Bridegroom, silver tea spoons and sliver carvers Allen Mrs. Latchford. silver sugar basket and sifter Amy Mr John, Jersey, 4 silver salts Bennett Mr and Mrs John and Misses, Padgate, silver bread tray and anife Bennett Rev J G and Mrs. Boltoo, case dessert knives Bennott Mr and Mrs Joseph, Padgate pair silver salts Bennett Doctor J and Mrs, Padgate, fruit epergne Bennett Mr Leonard, silver muffineers and mustard pot Bennett Mr F,London, pair poteries artistiques ' Beamont Mrs L. Orford Hall, silver and cut glass jam dish Bayley Mr and Mrs W. Warrington, case of scissors Browne Mrs A. Warrington. silver pin try Brookes Miss M. Bath, Worcester not pound vase Birtles Misses, Warrington, •• Sheffield " picture Burden Mr and Mrs, Hendon, silver cake basket Bayley Mr and Mrs SC Warrington. silver cake knife Corning Miss, Barnsley. silver mounted Austrian vase Cook Mr and Mrs. Warrington, silver dessert spoons Cartwright Mr and Mrs. Southampton, ph:dare Cooling Miss, Manchester, nightdress case. Comb and brush bag Cooke Mr and Mrs W. Jersey. silver flower stand Cooke Mr and Mrs Percy V. Jersey, cheque Cooper Mr T, Warrington. Minton placque Chappell Mrs W. Warrington, coal box Davies Mr Robert. Warrington, silv