      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH FOX, RUN OORN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. JOSEPH FOX, RUN OORN.</p>
        <p>Memoir and Funeral.</p>
        <p>In the early house. of Saturday morning there passaa into the sbadowland of death a gentleman, in the person of Mr. Joseph Fox, who might truly be described as one of Runcorn's noblest patriarchs. For many years the personality of Mr Fox—though he was not in the strictest sense a public man—has been so interwoven with the warp and woof of the life of the town that it will be hard •to recognise ' that his genial, sturdy face will no longer be seen. Born nearly eighty years ago in Hulme,Manchester 1 he became associated with the town of his adop- tion some ten years later and at the proper time became apprenticed to the Anderton Company las a • shipwright. His parents were strong ' supporters of the Established Church but being brought at an early age, by some means, under the influence of Nonconformity, he joined the Wesleyan Methodist body and soon afterwards became converted. It is interesting to know that Mr Fox sprang from a very high patrician stock, and had almost blue blood running in his veins. An old family relic, in the shape of an old Bible—itself three hundred years old—traces his ancestors back for hundreds of years. He was a lineal descendant of Sir Charles Fox, who was Secretary of State to George 111., and the " family circle " includes several baronets. As the late respected John and Thomas Johnson, who established what is popularly known as "Johnson's Soapery,'' were also own cousins to the deceased it will be seen that the late Mr Fox belonged to a circle of which Runcornians may well be proud—a circle whose names,it is stated on the best authority, are written more than once on the pages of British history. As soon as ever Mr Fox could teach he was placed in charge of the bott sm class in the Methodist Sunday school, and from that time for- ward his life and work have been inseparably bound up in the history of the town. By slow stages ha gradually worked his way to the first class and up to two years ago had the honour of conducting the first young men's class:in Brunswick Sunday school. During that time he materially assisted—financially and otherwise—in the de- velopment of Nonconformity in the town, but more particularly his beloved Wesleyan Methodism. He was one of she few teachers who stood firm by "Old Brunswick "when in 1850 the great secession took place over church polity which split up -Runcorn Wesleyati Methodism and made the " Free Church "-in Ellesmere- street. He has also the unique distinction of having been a teacher in connection with the Wesleyan body over 51 years. During his teaure of Office hundreds of young men have passed through his hands who have since made their marks in the different worlds of thought and who have good reason to bless the name of Joseph fr,x. Some short time previous to his official resignation which occurred about two years ago, some little hitch took place in the school which had the effect of showing how deeply loyal were the young men of Methodism to the aged Christian veteran. Idis Sunday School lessons were marked with a deep sense of ' religious conviction and were set in an experience of human nature and knowledge that made them as instructive as they were entertaining. His value ass recognised by the chiefs ofiMethodism when, upon his resignation on account of old age, he was presented with an illuminated address and a purse of gold. His fame as a Sunday school wor- ker was also noised abroad to such an extent that a short time ago a leading religious paper presented him with a silver medal as a memento of his long and use- ful work. With the exception of Mr Thomas Bowyer he was the oldest Wesleyan in the town and has tug been revered as one of the fathers of local Methodism by the rising generations. A peculiar thing about the ancestors of Mr Fox is the fact that many of them have reached over one hundred years of age and an Nosily interesting fact not generall3 known is that Mr Fox's father was the first captain of the Runcorn Volunteers— a branch of our army service in which the deceased always took the deepest interest. Slowly but surety Time remorselessly stamped the wrinkles deep in the brow of "Old Joseph " as he was affectionately called and, though having made :a competency and living I practically independently, :failing health necessitated ' his severance from the school he loved al dearly. Since then his decline has been a rapid one and the end came with painful suddenness. Be leaves behind him, I a widow, four sons and three daughters. The funeral of the late Mr Joseph Marshall Fox took place on Tuesday afternoon. Previous to c raying the body to its last resting place a funeral service was held in St Pam's Church conducted by the Rev Alfred Wells, superintendent minister. Mn R Morris conducted the singing, the first hymn b&amp;ng " Oh for the wings of faith to rise." In a very eloquent address the minister referred to the deceased gentleman's connec- tion with Runcorn Methodism which extended, he said, over 66 years, If he were asked to write an mitaph on Mr Fox's tile it would he in the words of Holy Writ, "Be west all faith and full of the Holy Ghost." As the coffin left the church Mr 'V Pritchard played the "Dead March " Welkinr in front of the hearse were Captain Millinger, Messrs .1 Unsworth, W Uosworth, D 13 Jones, W Jones, J Percival, D Sisbrown, T Boots, W Kirkham, junior, 8 Coventry, T Kilgrass, R Moores, A R Norman, J P, C'C. The chief mourners were Messrs J Fox, John Fox, and T Fox, sons, J'lloyle and P Royle. sons-in-law, C Fox, W Royle. and J Royle, grandsons. The bearers were Messrs s Hopkins, P Faulkner, El Pilgrim, and G Hill. The concluding portion of the burial service was read at the grave side by the Rev A Wells in an impres- sive manner after which the mourners Saw?. ens of the deceased's favourite hymns entitled "Jesu, Lover of my Soul."</p>
        <p>Sad Death of a Thelwall Farmer. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr Robert Dobson, coroner, held an inquest at the Oakland lye Farm, Thelwall, the other day on the body of James Brereton, fortner of Oakland Eve Farm. Thelwall•—Annie Brereton, widow of the de- ceased. said that on Monday, the 2nd inst, he left with a horse and cart to take milk to Warrington. Witness met him in the lane about a quarter of a mile from home on his return. Deceased went in a field with the horse and cart to avoid a thrashing machine engine which was going down the lane. He turned into the lane again when an Irishman stopped him and asked him about some work. Her husband had his hand on the back of the cart, and the horse, which was a young one, started away. Deceased ran alongside the cart and held the reins. The lane was very narrow, and when the cart came up to a tree deceased came into contact with the trunk and was crushed' He was rendered uncon- scious, but having recovered,walked home with the assit-tance of two men. Death occurred at six o'clock on Wednesday morning. Dr Leadbetter attended the deceased, whose ribs were broken and his lungs injured.—The jury returned a verdict that deceased died from "pulmonary hiemorrhage, due to lacera- tion of the lungs and fractured ribs."</p>
        <p>IL M ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC I 8 rnii KING OF TONICS.-63, SINSET-STEEET Warrington.—(Advt). LADIES REST MACH ROM'S AND SHO HI UNDER TEE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STRBIIT.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS. Trinity Chapel, Frodsham.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the harvest festival services were canduc- ted by the Rev T H Bailey, who preachod morning and evening. In the afternoon the service of song on the life of toe Rev Charles Garrett was given. The in- terior of the chapel was beautifully decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Mission Church, Frodsham Bridge.</head>
        <p>Mission Church, Frodsham Bridge.</p>
        <p>The interior of this church, by willing hands, bad been made to assume a charming appearance by flowers, fruits and cereals. The Rev A P Reynolds, vicar ot Kingsley, preached at the evening service to a thronged congregation. The singing was very attractive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>St. Paul's Church Runcorn.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with the abovle church will be held on Sunday and Monday. Sonia anthems will be rendered by the choir. The mornin service will be cor:ducted by the Rev J B Fester, an the evening service by the Rev Alfred Wells, superin- tendent minister. A nright service will be held on the Monday over which the Rev G B Saul will preside. For further pirticulars see advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Salvation Army, Runcorn,</head>
        <p>Salvation Army, Runcorn,</p>
        <p>The harvest festival was observed on Sunday. Special services were held at 7 am. II am, 3 p in, and 645 p. to. The barracks were decorated with harvest pro- ducts, while on the gallery was the model of a farm house, surrounded by shrubs, flowers Sin. Costume marches were held during the day, the officers and men being attired in red blouses and harvesting hats, under the direction of Captain Young. The latter was the preacher for the day. The collections realised nearly:£s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church, Runcorn.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with Sr. John's Presbyterian Church, was held on Sunday, when the special preacher was the R v P.ichard Whilams, of Rhos The church was neatly decorated w:th the fruits of harvest,the work ot the ladif s connected with the place. Special hymns and anthems were sung. At the afternooc service, the chair was taken by Mr James Pritchard. An interesting address was given he tha kt,v Richard IVi.liams, and solos were sung by Miss Mabel Morris and Miss Louie Bootia,while alrecitation was given by Ger 'if) Thomas The same minister preached at the evering service when there was a good congregation, Mr .1 Richardson presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Halton Wesleyan Church</head>
        <p>Halton Wesleyan Church</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with the Halton Wesleyan Church was celebrated on Sunday and Mon- day. The churcla had been beautifully decorated, gifts of fruit, !lowers, and vegetables from members of the congregation having been utilized with much effect. The preacher in the morning was the Rev H Morrison, and at the evening service the pulpit wis occupied by the Rev A vVells,superiatendent minister. In the after- noon a service of bong, entitled 4. Charles Garratt, preacher, philanthropist, at d evangelist," was rendered by the choir, under tbe conductorship of Mr Albert Rowe, the connective readings being given by Mr Rosenberg, sen., Weston Point: The staging was quite in keeping with the reputation of the choir. Oa Mon- day evening the closinr, service was held, ween the circuit steward, Mr R H Posnett, of Runcorn, preached an appropriate sermon. The collections realised a very satisfactory sum.</p>
        <p>Camden Wesleyan Chapel, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving servics in connection with the above place of worship were held on Sunday. A large quantity of fruit, fliwers,and vegetables had been presented by the congregation, and these had been used in the decoration of the chapel. This work had been performed with much skill and ' taste by toe ladies of the congregation. By the pulpit and the choir stalls were grouped a profusion of corn, flowers, and fruit, and the walls were also beautified with the same products. The preacher in the morning, the Rev A- Wells, delivered a practical sermon from Matt. xiii. 39. In the morning the anthetni "Praise, 0 praise our God and King (by the Rev E V Hall), and "Ye shall dwell in the land" (Stainer), were rendered with marked precision. The soluists were Miss Gleave (soprano), Mr Parry (tenor), and Mr W Worrall (bass). The solos were a distinct feature of the music, being rendered with splendid effect. In the evening the church was crowded to its utincst capacity. The preacher was the Rev J B Foster. The anthems, " Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem " (Rev E V Hall), " Incline thine ear." boa solo and quartet (Himmel!), " Honour the Lord (Stainer), and " Praise the Lord (Matuader),were treated in a manner that spoke volumes for the efficient training the choir had received from the conductor, Mr J W Tetnperley, who also presided at the organ. The bass solo and quartette " Incline thine ear" were rendered by Mr W Hopwood, Miss Katie fbomam, A.V.C.M. (soprano), and Mr Whiteside (tenor), of Liverpool. 'flan contralto portion was admirably sustained by Miss Nellie Tern- parley, and the combination was very rffective. Special harwst hymns were also rendered. The collections were well in advance of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>DEAIH OF INSPECTOR NORMAN.</head>
        <p>DEAIH OF INSPECTOR NORMAN.</p>
        <p>Another zealous end conscientious servant of the Warrington Corporation has this week passed away in the person of Chief Inspector Ralph Norman.who died at his residence, 46. Beweey-street, at 11 30 on Sunday night. The late Inspector pos.ees;ed 3 re- markably strong constitution, being prowl of the fact that he never had a serious illness in his life, and it was with surprise that the public heard six weeks ago toot he was confined to his bed with a severe illness which ultimately proved fatal. He first complained of being ill on the day that he attended the funeral of the late Medical Officer of Health, and:his condition became so serious that it was deemed advisable to call in -medical assistance. Dr Joseph and Dr Guest Gornall attended him.and later on a specialist in the person of Dr. Barr, of Liverpool, was called in. Notwith- standing all that 'medical kill could do he became weaker almost daily, and died at the time stared, the cause of death being cancer in the liver. Mr Norman was a very familiar figure in Warring- ton, baying for a cortiderable number of years been associated with the official life of the bor- ough. He joined the Borough Police Force on November 29h. 1863,was made.firet-class constable on June 18%, 186.5, merit class klay 24th, 1867, and sergeant March 26th, 1868. He left the force on the 29th July, 1873. He was appointed an inspec- tor in the service of the Corporation on May 31st, 1875, succeeding the late Mr William Heaton,and at the time of his death he was chief sanitary inspec- tor of the borough. He was held in high esteem by sanitary inspectors generally and was elected chairman of the sanitary Inspectors' Assoc'a- Mon. All who knew him will agree that Mr Nor- man was a most conscientious and painstaking official and discharged his duties with a commend- able impartially which gained the confidence of his committee as well as of the members Of the Coun- cil. His last annual report was presented two or three months ago,and it shows that the department for which he was responsible is fast becoming in- creasingly important. A great deal of work has been done,and there is no doubt that the improved ' sanitary condition of the borough is in no small measure due to his efforts. The late Mr Norman was a member of the Church of England, and for many years was prominently identifie 1 with St. Peter's Church. He was a member of the Admiral Nelson Lodge of the In 'ependent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), being a Paq Provincial Grand Master and also holding the office of treasurer. He was also a member of the Ma.onic liodge of Equity (Widnes). On all .hands;his death will be deeply regretted, and much sympethy is ex- pressed with his relatives in their bereavement. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemeter y on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE.—A meeting of this committee was held on Thursday, Mr C E Liaker in the chair. Tne accounts produced were signed on brhalf of the committee. The fcllswing classes were then arranged for the coming session:—Drawing. shorthand, arittunetic, mathematics or book-keeping, carpentry, ambulance (males acd femiles), or possibly woodcarv- ing. It was reported that 39 pupils had made a full att-ridance at the classes. The neeretary was authorized tr, apply to the Cheshire County Council for a grant of £7O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-09-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>THE'SALE O WORK AND GARDEN Frrg.--Thursclay last proved a great smicess financially, as the gross receip's were slightly over £l2l, of which it is hoped that a claar £lOO will he available for parochial nurpreles. The previous he was last year's sale, when £BO 10; was taken, with £B7 10i net proceeds. The winners of the various competitions were as follows :—Blindfold draw- ing of a OE : 1, Miss May Warburton ; 2, Miss Collier. Gentlemeo's hat-trimming : 1, Rev W Pollock-Hill ; 2, Mr F Blackwell. Sunlight waahing comnerition (fr,r ladies) : 1, lis3 Edith Granger ; 2, Mrs Whitlow ; 3, Mrs Hopkinson ; 4, Mrs R Denham ; for gentlemen : 1, Mr T Smith ; 2, Mr Gorton. Air-gun shooting (ladies) : 1, Mrs R Dennarn : 2, Mrs Allen ; gentlemen : 1, Mr T J Weir ; 2, Mr G Roughley.</p>
      </div>
